insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-hello$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Your first Python program","kind":"code","task":"Print the three lines described and add a comment.","hint":"Three print lines: print(\"Hello, world!\"), print(\"My name is Ada\"), print(365 * 24). A comment starts with #.","body":"## What is Python?\n\nA computer is a very fast, very obedient worker who understands nothing unless you spell it out. A **programming language** is how you write those instructions down. Python is the language most people learn first because it reads almost like English, and it is the same language used by Instagram, Netflix, NASA, banks and nearly every AI lab.\n\nThink of a Python program as a **recipe**. The cook (the computer) starts at the top and follows one line at a time, in order. If a step is written wrongly, the cook stops and tells you which line confused it.\n\n## Your first instruction: print()\n\n`print()` tells Python to show something on the screen:\n\n```python\nprint(\"Hello, world!\")\nprint(\"I am learning Python\")\n```\n\n- The words inside quotes are **text** (programmers call it a *string*: a string of letters).\n- The brackets `( )` hold what you give to print.\n- Every `print` starts a new line.\n\nYou can print numbers and sums too, without quotes:\n\n```python\nprint(5 + 3)      # shows 8\nprint(\"5 + 3\")    # shows 5 + 3 (it is text, so Python does not calculate it)\n```\n\n## Comments: notes for humans\n\nAnything after `#` is a **comment**. Python skips it. Comments are sticky notes you leave for yourself and your team: *why* you did something, not what every line does.\n\n## When it goes wrong\n\nMistakes are normal: every programmer sees errors every day. If you forget a quote, Python says something like `SyntaxError: unterminated string literal` and points to the line. Read the **last line** of the error first: it says what went wrong. Then look at the line number.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe editor on the right already runs Python in your browser. Press **Run** to see the output panel.\n\n1. Print exactly `Hello, world!`\n2. On the next line, print the sentence `My name is` followed by your name, for example `My name is Ada`.\n3. Print the answer to `365 * 24` (the hours in a year), as a number, not as text.\n4. Add at least one comment.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":false,"order":0}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-values$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Variables, numbers and text","kind":"code","task":"Work out subtotal, vat and total, then build the receipt with an f-string.","hint":"vat = round(subtotal * 0.075, 2) and receipt = f\"{quantity} items, total {total}\"","body":"## Variables are labelled boxes\n\nImagine a market stall with labelled boxes: one says *price*, one says *quantity*. You can look inside a box, or empty it and put something new in. A **variable** is exactly that: a name that holds a value.\n\n```python\nprice = 1500\nquantity = 3\ntotal = price * quantity\nprint(total)   # 4500\n```\n\nThe `=` sign means **\"put the value on the right into the box on the left\"**. It is not \"equals\" like in maths. `count = count + 1` is perfectly sensible: take what is in the box, add one, put it back.\n\nGood names describe the content: `total_price`, not `tp` or `x`. Python names use lowercase words joined by underscores.\n\n## Kinds of values (types)\n\n| Type | Example | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `int` | `42`, `-7` | whole numbers: counts, quantities |\n| `float` | `3.5`, `0.075` | numbers with decimals: prices, rates |\n| `str` | `\"Abuja\"` | text |\n| `bool` | `True`, `False` | yes / no answers |\n\n`type(value)` tells you the type. Mixing types carelessly causes errors: `\"5\" + 3` fails because Python will not guess whether you meant text or maths. Convert on purpose: `int(\"5\") + 3` gives `8`, `str(8)` gives `\"8\"`.\n\n## Arithmetic\n\n`+  -  *  /` work as you expect. Also:\n\n- `//` whole-number division: `17 // 5` is `3` (3 full boxes of 5)\n- `%` remainder: `17 % 5` is `2` (2 left over)\n- `**` power: `2 ** 10` is `1024`\n- `round(x, 2)` rounds to 2 decimal places, which is what you want for money.\n\n## f-strings: filling in a form\n\nAn f-string is like a printed form with blanks. Put `f` before the quotes and write variable names inside `{ }`:\n\n```python\nname = \"Chika\"\nitems = 3\nprint(f\"{name} bought {items} items\")\nprint(f\"Total: {4500:,}\")       # Total: 4,500   (the comma adds thousands separators)\n```\n\n## Your task\n\nA shop sells a phone case for 2,500. A customer buys 4. VAT is 7.5%.\n\n1. Set `price = 2500` and `quantity = 4`.\n2. `subtotal`: price times quantity.\n3. `vat`: 7.5% of the subtotal, rounded to 2 decimals.\n4. `total`: subtotal plus vat.\n5. `receipt`: an f-string that reads exactly `4 items, total 10750.0` (use your variables, not typed numbers).\n6. Print the receipt.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":false,"order":1}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-decisions$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Decisions and your first functions","kind":"code","task":"Write grade, delivery_fee and can_vote.","hint":"Check the highest grade first. For delivery_fee, check the free case first, then the city.","body":"## Programs that choose\n\nSo far the recipe has one path. Real programs choose: *if the customer spent over 50,000, give a discount; otherwise, don't*. Think of a **road junction** with signs: the car (your program) checks the sign and takes one road.\n\n```python\namount = 62000\nif amount >= 50000:\n    print(\"You get 10% off\")\nelse:\n    print(\"No discount today\")\n```\n\nTwo rules matter:\n\n1. The line ends with a colon `:`.\n2. The lines that belong to the branch are **indented** (4 spaces). Indentation is not decoration in Python: it is how Python knows which lines are inside the `if`.\n\n## Comparisons give True or False\n\n`==` equal (two signs! one `=` puts a value in a box), `!=` not equal, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`.\n\nCombine them with `and`, `or`, `not`:\n\n```python\nif age >= 18 and has_id:\n    print(\"You may open an account\")\n```\n\n## More than two roads: elif\n\n```python\nif score >= 70:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif score >= 60:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelif score >= 50:\n    grade = \"C\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"F\"\n```\n\nPython checks from the top and takes the **first** road whose sign is true. Order matters: if you checked `score >= 50` first, a 90 would get a C.\n\n## Functions: a machine you build once\n\nA **function** is like a blender: you put things in (the *inputs*, or parameters), it does its job, and something comes out (the *return* value). You build it once and use it as often as you like.\n\n```python\ndef grade(score):\n    if score >= 70:\n        return \"A\"\n    return \"F\"\n\nprint(grade(85))   # A\nprint(grade(40))   # F\n```\n\n- `def` starts a function, then its name and inputs in brackets, then a colon.\n- `return` sends the answer back and **stops** the function immediately.\n- Functions make your code testable: the checks for this lesson call your function with different scores.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `grade(score)`: return `\"A\"` for 70 and above, `\"B\"` for 60 to 69, `\"C\"` for 50 to 59, `\"D\"` for 45 to 49 and `\"F\"` below 45.\n2. `delivery_fee(amount, city)`: free (`0`) when the amount is 50,000 or more; otherwise `1500` if the city is `\"Abuja\"` and `3000` anywhere else.\n3. `can_vote(age, registered)`: `True` only when age is 18 or more **and** registered is True.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":2}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-decisions$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-decisions","title":"Decisions and your first functions","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"Comparisons, if / elif / else, and and / or, and wrapping logic in a function you can reuse.","body":"## Programs that choose\n\nSo far the recipe has one path. Real programs choose: *if the customer spent over 50,000, give a discount; otherwise, don't*. Think of a **road junction** with signs: the car (your program) checks the sign and takes one road.\n\n```python\namount = 62000\nif amount >= 50000:\n    print(\"You get 10% off\")\nelse:\n    print(\"No discount today\")\n```\n\nTwo rules matter:\n\n1. The line ends with a colon `:`.\n2. The lines that belong to the branch are **indented** (4 spaces). Indentation is not decoration in Python: it is how Python knows which lines are inside the `if`.\n\n## Comparisons give True or False\n\n`==` equal (two signs! one `=` puts a value in a box), `!=` not equal, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`.\n\nCombine them with `and`, `or`, `not`:\n\n```python\nif age >= 18 and has_id:\n    print(\"You may open an account\")\n```\n\n## More than two roads: elif\n\n```python\nif score >= 70:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif score >= 60:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelif score >= 50:\n    grade = \"C\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"F\"\n```\n\nPython checks from the top and takes the **first** road whose sign is true. Order matters: if you checked `score >= 50` first, a 90 would get a C.\n\n## Functions: a machine you build once\n\nA **function** is like a blender: you put things in (the *inputs*, or parameters), it does its job, and something comes out (the *return* value). You build it once and use it as often as you like.\n\n```python\ndef grade(score):\n    if score >= 70:\n        return \"A\"\n    return \"F\"\n\nprint(grade(85))   # A\nprint(grade(40))   # F\n```\n\n- `def` starts a function, then its name and inputs in brackets, then a colon.\n- `return` sends the answer back and **stops** the function immediately.\n- Functions make your code testable: the checks for this lesson call your function with different scores.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `grade(score)`: return `\"A\"` for 70 and above, `\"B\"` for 60 to 69, `\"C\"` for 50 to 59, `\"D\"` for 45 to 49 and `\"F\"` below 45.\n2. `delivery_fee(amount, city)`: free (`0`) when the amount is 50,000 or more; otherwise `1500` if the city is `\"Abuja\"` and `3000` anywhere else.\n3. `can_vote(age, registered)`: `True` only when age is 18 or more **and** registered is True.","task":"Write grade, delivery_fee and can_vote.","starter":"def grade(score):\n    return \"F\"\n\ndef delivery_fee(amount, city):\n    return 0\n\ndef can_vote(age, registered):\n    return False\n","checks":[{"label":"grade boundaries are right","test":"return await py(\"[grade(s) for s in (100, 70, 69, 60, 59, 50, 49, 45, 44, 0)] == ['A','A','B','B','C','C','D','D','F','F']\")"},{"label":"free delivery from 50,000","test":"return await py(\"delivery_fee(50000, 'Lagos') == 0 and delivery_fee(90000, 'Abuja') == 0\")"},{"label":"city fees below 50,000","test":"return await py(\"delivery_fee(49999, 'Abuja') == 1500 and delivery_fee(1000, 'Kano') == 3000\")"},{"label":"can_vote needs both","test":"return await py(\"can_vote(18, True) and not can_vote(17, True) and not can_vote(30, False)\")"}],"hint":"Check the highest grade first. For delivery_fee, check the free case first, then the city."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-loops$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Lists and loops","kind":"code","task":"Write average, count_above, running_totals and biggest.","hint":"biggest: start with best = nums[0] (after checking the list is not empty), then loop and replace best when you find a bigger number.","body":"## A list is a row of lockers\n\nOne variable holds one value. A **list** holds many, in order, like a row of numbered lockers:\n\n```python\nsales = [12000, 8500, 15000, 9200]\nprint(sales[0])     # 12000   the first locker is number 0\nprint(sales[-1])    # 9200    negative numbers count from the end\nprint(len(sales))   # 4\nsales.append(11000) # add a locker at the end\n```\n\nCounting from 0 feels odd at first. Think of it as \"how many steps from the start\": the first item is 0 steps away.\n\nPython has helpers that work on whole lists: `sum(sales)`, `min(sales)`, `max(sales)`, `sorted(sales)`.\n\n## Loops: do this for each one\n\nA **for loop** is a teacher walking down a row of desks, doing the same thing at each desk:\n\n```python\nfor amount in sales:\n    print(amount)\n```\n\nThe indented lines run once for every item, and `amount` holds the current item each time round.\n\n## The accumulator pattern\n\nThe most useful loop pattern in all of programming: start with an empty \"running total\", and add to it as you go. It is exactly how a cashier counts a pile of notes.\n\n```python\ntotal = 0\nfor amount in sales:\n    total = total + amount     # or: total += amount\nprint(total)\n```\n\nThe same pattern counts things (`count += 1` when a condition is true) or builds a new list (`big.append(x)`).\n\n## range(): counting loops\n\n`range(5)` gives 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. `range(1, 6)` gives 1 to 5. Use it when you need to repeat something a number of times:\n\n```python\nfor week in range(1, 5):\n    print(f\"Week {week}\")\n```\n\n## while loops (briefly)\n\n`while` repeats *as long as* something is true, like \"keep pouring until the cup is full\". Be careful: if the condition never becomes false, the loop never stops.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite these **using loops** (you may also use `len`):\n\n1. `average(nums)`: the mean of a list. Return `0` for an empty list (never divide by zero).\n2. `count_above(nums, limit)`: how many numbers are strictly greater than `limit`.\n3. `running_totals(nums)`: a new list of running totals: `[5, 3, 2]` becomes `[5, 8, 10]`.\n4. `biggest(nums)`: the largest number, **without** using `max`. Return `None` for an empty list.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":3}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-loops$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-loops","title":"Lists and loops","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"Store many values in a list, loop over them with for, use range, and build totals step by step.","body":"## A list is a row of lockers\n\nOne variable holds one value. A **list** holds many, in order, like a row of numbered lockers:\n\n```python\nsales = [12000, 8500, 15000, 9200]\nprint(sales[0])     # 12000   the first locker is number 0\nprint(sales[-1])    # 9200    negative numbers count from the end\nprint(len(sales))   # 4\nsales.append(11000) # add a locker at the end\n```\n\nCounting from 0 feels odd at first. Think of it as \"how many steps from the start\": the first item is 0 steps away.\n\nPython has helpers that work on whole lists: `sum(sales)`, `min(sales)`, `max(sales)`, `sorted(sales)`.\n\n## Loops: do this for each one\n\nA **for loop** is a teacher walking down a row of desks, doing the same thing at each desk:\n\n```python\nfor amount in sales:\n    print(amount)\n```\n\nThe indented lines run once for every item, and `amount` holds the current item each time round.\n\n## The accumulator pattern\n\nThe most useful loop pattern in all of programming: start with an empty \"running total\", and add to it as you go. It is exactly how a cashier counts a pile of notes.\n\n```python\ntotal = 0\nfor amount in sales:\n    total = total + amount     # or: total += amount\nprint(total)\n```\n\nThe same pattern counts things (`count += 1` when a condition is true) or builds a new list (`big.append(x)`).\n\n## range(): counting loops\n\n`range(5)` gives 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. `range(1, 6)` gives 1 to 5. Use it when you need to repeat something a number of times:\n\n```python\nfor week in range(1, 5):\n    print(f\"Week {week}\")\n```\n\n## while loops (briefly)\n\n`while` repeats *as long as* something is true, like \"keep pouring until the cup is full\". Be careful: if the condition never becomes false, the loop never stops.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite these **using loops** (you may also use `len`):\n\n1. `average(nums)`: the mean of a list. Return `0` for an empty list (never divide by zero).\n2. `count_above(nums, limit)`: how many numbers are strictly greater than `limit`.\n3. `running_totals(nums)`: a new list of running totals: `[5, 3, 2]` becomes `[5, 8, 10]`.\n4. `biggest(nums)`: the largest number, **without** using `max`. Return `None` for an empty list.","task":"Write average, count_above, running_totals and biggest.","starter":"def average(nums):\n    pass\n\ndef count_above(nums, limit):\n    pass\n\ndef running_totals(nums):\n    pass\n\ndef biggest(nums):\n    pass\n","checks":[{"label":"average works (and 0 for an empty list)","test":"return await py(\"average([2, 4, 9]) == 5 and average([]) == 0\")"},{"label":"count_above counts strictly greater","test":"return await py(\"count_above([10, 50, 51, 90], 50) == 2 and count_above([], 1) == 0\")"},{"label":"running_totals builds a new list","test":"return await py(\"running_totals([5, 3, 2]) == [5, 8, 10] and running_totals([]) == []\")"},{"label":"biggest without max","test":"return await py(\"biggest([3, 99, -2, 7]) == 99 and biggest([-5, -9]) == -5 and biggest([]) is None\")"}],"hint":"biggest: start with best = nums[0] (after checking the list is not empty), then loop and replace best when you find a bigger number."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-strings$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Working with text","kind":"code","task":"Write initials, clean_phone and mask_email.","hint":"initials: \"\".join(w[0].upper() + \".\" for w in full_name.split()). clean_phone: build a string of the characters where ch.isdigit().","body":"## Text is a list of characters\n\nA string behaves like a list of letters. You can count it, index it and slice it:\n\n```python\ncity = \"Port Harcourt\"\nlen(city)      # 13\ncity[0]        # 'P'\ncity[0:4]      # 'Port'   from position 0 up to (not including) 4\ncity[-8:]      # 'Harcourt'\n```\n\nSlicing is like cutting a loaf of bread: `[start:end]` keeps the slices from *start* up to, but not including, *end*.\n\n## String methods: tools on a belt\n\nEvery string carries a tool belt of **methods**. You call them with a dot:\n\n| Method | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.strip()` | removes spaces at both ends | `\"  hi \".strip()` → `\"hi\"` |\n| `.lower()` / `.upper()` | changes case | `\"Ada\".upper()` → `\"ADA\"` |\n| `.title()` | Capitalises Each Word | `\"ada obi\".title()` → `\"Ada Obi\"` |\n| `.replace(a, b)` | swaps text | `\"1,200\".replace(\",\", \"\")` → `\"1200\"` |\n| `.startswith(x)` | True / False | `\"+234...\".startswith(\"+\")` |\n| `.split(sep)` | cuts into a list | `\"a,b,c\".split(\",\")` → `['a','b','c']` |\n| `sep.join(list)` | glues a list | `\"-\".join(['a','b'])` → `\"a-b\"` |\n\nStrings are **immutable**: methods never change the original, they give you a new string. So you must keep the result: `name = name.strip()`.\n\n## Checking characters\n\n`.isdigit()` is True when every character is a digit. `\"7\" in \"2347\"` checks whether one string appears inside another. You can loop over a string: `for ch in text:` visits each character.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nMost real data is typed by people, and people are inconsistent. Customer names arrive as `\"  aDA  obi \"`, phone numbers as `\"0803 123 4567\"`, `\"+2348031234567\"` or `\"234-803-123-4567\"`. Before you can count customers or send messages, you must clean the text. That is everyday Python work in offices around the world.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `initials(full_name)`: `\"  ada  ngozi obi \"` → `\"A.N.O.\"` (first letter of each word, upper case, each followed by a dot).\n2. `clean_phone(text)`: keep only the digits; then if the result starts with `0` and has 11 digits, replace the leading 0 with `234`. `\"0803 123 4567\"` → `\"2348031234567\"`, `\"+234-803-123-4567\"` → `\"2348031234567\"`. Anything else stays as its digits.\n3. `mask_email(email)`: keep the first letter of the part before `@`, replace the rest of that part with `***`: `\"chika@mail.com\"` → `\"c***@mail.com\"`.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":4}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-strings$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-strings","title":"Working with text","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"String methods, slicing, split and join, and cleaning real-world text like names and phone numbers.","body":"## Text is a list of characters\n\nA string behaves like a list of letters. You can count it, index it and slice it:\n\n```python\ncity = \"Port Harcourt\"\nlen(city)      # 13\ncity[0]        # 'P'\ncity[0:4]      # 'Port'   from position 0 up to (not including) 4\ncity[-8:]      # 'Harcourt'\n```\n\nSlicing is like cutting a loaf of bread: `[start:end]` keeps the slices from *start* up to, but not including, *end*.\n\n## String methods: tools on a belt\n\nEvery string carries a tool belt of **methods**. You call them with a dot:\n\n| Method | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.strip()` | removes spaces at both ends | `\"  hi \".strip()` → `\"hi\"` |\n| `.lower()` / `.upper()` | changes case | `\"Ada\".upper()` → `\"ADA\"` |\n| `.title()` | Capitalises Each Word | `\"ada obi\".title()` → `\"Ada Obi\"` |\n| `.replace(a, b)` | swaps text | `\"1,200\".replace(\",\", \"\")` → `\"1200\"` |\n| `.startswith(x)` | True / False | `\"+234...\".startswith(\"+\")` |\n| `.split(sep)` | cuts into a list | `\"a,b,c\".split(\",\")` → `['a','b','c']` |\n| `sep.join(list)` | glues a list | `\"-\".join(['a','b'])` → `\"a-b\"` |\n\nStrings are **immutable**: methods never change the original, they give you a new string. So you must keep the result: `name = name.strip()`.\n\n## Checking characters\n\n`.isdigit()` is True when every character is a digit. `\"7\" in \"2347\"` checks whether one string appears inside another. You can loop over a string: `for ch in text:` visits each character.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nMost real data is typed by people, and people are inconsistent. Customer names arrive as `\"  aDA  obi \"`, phone numbers as `\"0803 123 4567\"`, `\"+2348031234567\"` or `\"234-803-123-4567\"`. Before you can count customers or send messages, you must clean the text. That is everyday Python work in offices around the world.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `initials(full_name)`: `\"  ada  ngozi obi \"` → `\"A.N.O.\"` (first letter of each word, upper case, each followed by a dot).\n2. `clean_phone(text)`: keep only the digits; then if the result starts with `0` and has 11 digits, replace the leading 0 with `234`. `\"0803 123 4567\"` → `\"2348031234567\"`, `\"+234-803-123-4567\"` → `\"2348031234567\"`. Anything else stays as its digits.\n3. `mask_email(email)`: keep the first letter of the part before `@`, replace the rest of that part with `***`: `\"chika@mail.com\"` → `\"c***@mail.com\"`.","task":"Write initials, clean_phone and mask_email.","starter":"def initials(full_name):\n    pass\n\ndef clean_phone(text):\n    pass\n\ndef mask_email(email):\n    pass\n","checks":[{"label":"initials from messy names","test":"return await py(\"initials('  ada  ngozi obi ') == 'A.N.O.' and initials('Musa') == 'M.'\")"},{"label":"local numbers get 234","test":"return await py(\"clean_phone('0803 123 4567') == '2348031234567'\")"},{"label":"international numbers are cleaned","test":"return await py(\"clean_phone('+234-803-123-4567') == '2348031234567' and clean_phone('(44) 20 7946') == '44207946'\")"},{"label":"emails are masked","test":"return await py(\"mask_email('chika@mail.com') == 'c***@mail.com' and mask_email('bo@x.org') == 'b***@x.org'\")"}],"hint":"initials: \"\".join(w[0].upper() + \".\" for w in full_name.split()). clean_phone: build a string of the characters where ch.isdigit()."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-dicts$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Dictionaries: data with labels","kind":"code","task":"Write word_counts, sales_by_branch and top_branch.","hint":"Use totals[o[\"branch\"]] = totals.get(o[\"branch\"], 0) + o[\"amount\"]. For the top: if not totals: return None, then max(totals, key=totals.get).","body":"## A dictionary is a phone book\n\nA list finds things by position (locker 0, locker 1). A **dictionary** finds things by **name**, like a phone book: you look up *\"Bola\"* and get her number.\n\n```python\nprices = {\"bread\": 1200, \"milk\": 900, \"eggs\": 3000}\nprices[\"milk\"]          # 900\nprices[\"rice\"] = 7500   # add or change an entry\n\"eggs\" in prices        # True\nprices.get(\"sugar\", 0)  # 0: a safe lookup with a default\n```\n\nEach entry is a **key** (the name you look up) and a **value** (what you get). Keys must be unique: a phone book cannot have two entries for exactly the same name.\n\n## Looping over a dictionary\n\n```python\nfor item, price in prices.items():\n    print(f\"{item}: {price}\")\n```\n\n`.keys()` gives the keys, `.values()` the values, `.items()` both as pairs.\n\n## The counting pattern\n\nThe second most useful pattern in programming (after the running total): **count how often each thing appears**. It is a tally sheet: for each new name, start a tally at 0; each time you see it, add one stroke.\n\n```python\ncounts = {}\nfor fruit in [\"mango\", \"orange\", \"mango\"]:\n    counts[fruit] = counts.get(fruit, 0) + 1\n# {'mango': 2, 'orange': 1}\n```\n\nThe same pattern **sums** per group: `totals[branch] = totals.get(branch, 0) + amount`. This one line is the heart of every \"sales by branch\" report.\n\n## Records: a list of dictionaries\n\nReal data usually looks like a table, and in Python each row is a dictionary:\n\n```python\norders = [\n    {\"customer\": \"Ada\", \"amount\": 5000},\n    {\"customer\": \"Musa\", \"amount\": 12000},\n]\nfor o in orders:\n    print(o[\"customer\"], o[\"amount\"])\n```\n\nThis is the same shape as JSON, the format almost every website and API uses to send data. Learn it well.\n\n## Finding the best\n\nTo find the key with the biggest value, loop and remember the best so far, or use `max(d, key=d.get)`.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `word_counts(text)`: count each word, ignoring case: `\"Buy rice buy beans\"` → `{\"buy\": 2, \"rice\": 1, \"beans\": 1}`.\n2. `sales_by_branch(orders)`: orders is a list of dictionaries with `\"branch\"` and `\"amount\"`; return a dictionary of total amount per branch.\n3. `top_branch(orders)`: the name of the branch with the highest total (use your function above). Return `None` when there are no orders.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":5}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-dicts$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-dicts","title":"Dictionaries: data with labels","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"Key-value data, looking things up, counting with dictionaries and lists of records.","body":"## A dictionary is a phone book\n\nA list finds things by position (locker 0, locker 1). A **dictionary** finds things by **name**, like a phone book: you look up *\"Bola\"* and get her number.\n\n```python\nprices = {\"bread\": 1200, \"milk\": 900, \"eggs\": 3000}\nprices[\"milk\"]          # 900\nprices[\"rice\"] = 7500   # add or change an entry\n\"eggs\" in prices        # True\nprices.get(\"sugar\", 0)  # 0: a safe lookup with a default\n```\n\nEach entry is a **key** (the name you look up) and a **value** (what you get). Keys must be unique: a phone book cannot have two entries for exactly the same name.\n\n## Looping over a dictionary\n\n```python\nfor item, price in prices.items():\n    print(f\"{item}: {price}\")\n```\n\n`.keys()` gives the keys, `.values()` the values, `.items()` both as pairs.\n\n## The counting pattern\n\nThe second most useful pattern in programming (after the running total): **count how often each thing appears**. It is a tally sheet: for each new name, start a tally at 0; each time you see it, add one stroke.\n\n```python\ncounts = {}\nfor fruit in [\"mango\", \"orange\", \"mango\"]:\n    counts[fruit] = counts.get(fruit, 0) + 1\n# {'mango': 2, 'orange': 1}\n```\n\nThe same pattern **sums** per group: `totals[branch] = totals.get(branch, 0) + amount`. This one line is the heart of every \"sales by branch\" report.\n\n## Records: a list of dictionaries\n\nReal data usually looks like a table, and in Python each row is a dictionary:\n\n```python\norders = [\n    {\"customer\": \"Ada\", \"amount\": 5000},\n    {\"customer\": \"Musa\", \"amount\": 12000},\n]\nfor o in orders:\n    print(o[\"customer\"], o[\"amount\"])\n```\n\nThis is the same shape as JSON, the format almost every website and API uses to send data. Learn it well.\n\n## Finding the best\n\nTo find the key with the biggest value, loop and remember the best so far, or use `max(d, key=d.get)`.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `word_counts(text)`: count each word, ignoring case: `\"Buy rice buy beans\"` → `{\"buy\": 2, \"rice\": 1, \"beans\": 1}`.\n2. `sales_by_branch(orders)`: orders is a list of dictionaries with `\"branch\"` and `\"amount\"`; return a dictionary of total amount per branch.\n3. `top_branch(orders)`: the name of the branch with the highest total (use your function above). Return `None` when there are no orders.","task":"Write word_counts, sales_by_branch and top_branch.","starter":"def word_counts(text):\n    return {}\n\ndef sales_by_branch(orders):\n    return {}\n\ndef top_branch(orders):\n    return None\n","checks":[{"label":"counts words, ignoring case","test":"return await py(\"word_counts('Buy rice buy beans BUY') == {'buy': 3, 'rice': 1, 'beans': 1}\")"},{"label":"totals per branch","test":"return await py(\"sales_by_branch([{'branch': 'Wuse', 'amount': 5}, {'branch': 'Garki', 'amount': 7}, {'branch': 'Wuse', 'amount': 4}]) == {'Wuse': 9, 'Garki': 7}\")"},{"label":"finds the top branch","test":"return await py(\"top_branch([{'branch': 'Wuse', 'amount': 5}, {'branch': 'Garki', 'amount': 7}, {'branch': 'Wuse', 'amount': 4}]) == 'Wuse'\")"},{"label":"no orders: None","test":"return await py(\"top_branch([]) is None and sales_by_branch([]) == {}\")"}],"hint":"Use totals[o[\"branch\"]] = totals.get(o[\"branch\"], 0) + o[\"amount\"]. For the top: if not totals: return None, then max(totals, key=totals.get)."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-functions$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Functions in depth","kind":"code","task":"Write vat, invoice_total and split_bill.","hint":"In invoice_total: subtotal = sum(price * qty for price, qty in items), then v = vat(subtotal, rate) and return subtotal, v, round(subtotal + v, 2).","body":"## Why functions are the most important idea\n\nGood programs are built like a car from parts: engine, wheels, brakes, each made and tested on its own. Functions are those parts. Each one should do **one job**, have a clear name, and be small enough to understand in one glance.\n\n## Default values\n\nA coffee machine has a default size: press the button and you get a medium, unless you choose otherwise. Parameters can have defaults too:\n\n```python\ndef price_after_discount(price, percent=0):\n    return price - price * percent / 100\n\nprice_after_discount(1000)          # 1000.0 (no discount)\nprice_after_discount(1000, 10)      # 900.0\nprice_after_discount(1000, percent=25)  # keyword argument: clearer to read\n```\n\nParameters with defaults go **after** the ones without.\n\n## Returning several values\n\nReturn a **tuple** (values separated by commas) and unpack it on the other side:\n\n```python\ndef min_max(nums):\n    return min(nums), max(nums)\n\nlow, high = min_max([4, 9, 1])\n```\n\n## Scope: what happens in the function stays in the function\n\nVariables created inside a function are **local**: they exist only while it runs, like scribbles on a whiteboard wiped clean after each meeting. This is a feature: functions cannot accidentally break each other's variables. Give functions what they need through parameters and get results back with `return`, rather than reaching for outside variables.\n\n## print versus return\n\nBeginners often `print` the answer inside a function. That shows it on screen but gives nothing back to the code that called it, like a calculator that flashes the answer and then forgets it. **Return** values; print only at the edges of your program.\n\n## Docstrings\n\nThe first line inside a function can be a string that explains it. Editors and AI assistants read it, and so do your teammates:\n\n```python\ndef vat(amount, rate=7.5):\n    \"\"\"VAT on an amount, rounded to 2 decimals.\"\"\"\n    return round(amount * rate / 100, 2)\n```\n\n## Functions can use functions\n\nBuild big things from small, tested pieces: `invoice_total` can call `vat`, which you already trust.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `vat(amount, rate=7.5)`: the VAT on an amount, rounded to 2 decimals.\n2. `invoice_total(items, rate=7.5)`: items is a list of `(price, quantity)` pairs. Return a tuple `(subtotal, vat, total)`, using your `vat` function. Round the total to 2 decimals.\n3. `split_bill(total, people, tip_percent=0)`: each person's share including the tip, rounded to 2 decimals. If `people` is less than 1, return `None`.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":6}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-functions$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-functions","title":"Functions in depth","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"Default values, keyword arguments, returning several values, scope, docstrings and small reusable tools.","body":"## Why functions are the most important idea\n\nGood programs are built like a car from parts: engine, wheels, brakes, each made and tested on its own. Functions are those parts. Each one should do **one job**, have a clear name, and be small enough to understand in one glance.\n\n## Default values\n\nA coffee machine has a default size: press the button and you get a medium, unless you choose otherwise. Parameters can have defaults too:\n\n```python\ndef price_after_discount(price, percent=0):\n    return price - price * percent / 100\n\nprice_after_discount(1000)          # 1000.0 (no discount)\nprice_after_discount(1000, 10)      # 900.0\nprice_after_discount(1000, percent=25)  # keyword argument: clearer to read\n```\n\nParameters with defaults go **after** the ones without.\n\n## Returning several values\n\nReturn a **tuple** (values separated by commas) and unpack it on the other side:\n\n```python\ndef min_max(nums):\n    return min(nums), max(nums)\n\nlow, high = min_max([4, 9, 1])\n```\n\n## Scope: what happens in the function stays in the function\n\nVariables created inside a function are **local**: they exist only while it runs, like scribbles on a whiteboard wiped clean after each meeting. This is a feature: functions cannot accidentally break each other's variables. Give functions what they need through parameters and get results back with `return`, rather than reaching for outside variables.\n\n## print versus return\n\nBeginners often `print` the answer inside a function. That shows it on screen but gives nothing back to the code that called it, like a calculator that flashes the answer and then forgets it. **Return** values; print only at the edges of your program.\n\n## Docstrings\n\nThe first line inside a function can be a string that explains it. Editors and AI assistants read it, and so do your teammates:\n\n```python\ndef vat(amount, rate=7.5):\n    \"\"\"VAT on an amount, rounded to 2 decimals.\"\"\"\n    return round(amount * rate / 100, 2)\n```\n\n## Functions can use functions\n\nBuild big things from small, tested pieces: `invoice_total` can call `vat`, which you already trust.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `vat(amount, rate=7.5)`: the VAT on an amount, rounded to 2 decimals.\n2. `invoice_total(items, rate=7.5)`: items is a list of `(price, quantity)` pairs. Return a tuple `(subtotal, vat, total)`, using your `vat` function. Round the total to 2 decimals.\n3. `split_bill(total, people, tip_percent=0)`: each person's share including the tip, rounded to 2 decimals. If `people` is less than 1, return `None`.","task":"Write vat, invoice_total and split_bill.","starter":"def vat(amount, rate=7.5):\n    pass\n\ndef invoice_total(items, rate=7.5):\n    pass\n\ndef split_bill(total, people, tip_percent=0):\n    pass\n","checks":[{"label":"vat with a default rate","test":"return await py(\"vat(1000) == 75.0 and vat(1000, 20) == 200.0 and vat(333) == 24.98\")"},{"label":"invoice_total returns three values","test":"return await py(\"invoice_total([(1000, 2), (500, 1)]) == (2500, 187.5, 2687.5)\")"},{"label":"invoice_total uses the rate","test":"return await py(\"invoice_total([(100, 1)], rate=0) == (100, 0.0, 100.0)\")"},{"label":"split_bill with tip and bad input","test":"return await py(\"split_bill(9000, 3) == 3000.0 and split_bill(10000, 4, tip_percent=10) == 2750.0 and split_bill(100, 0) is None\")"}],"hint":"In invoice_total: subtotal = sum(price * qty for price, qty in items), then v = vat(subtotal, rate) and return subtotal, v, round(subtotal + v, 2)."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-errors$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Errors and defensive code","kind":"code","task":"Write safe_divide, parse_quantity and total_quantities.","hint":"parse_quantity: n = int(text.strip()) raises ValueError by itself for \"abc\" and \"2.5\"; then if n < 1: raise ValueError(\"Quantity must be at least 1\").","body":"## Errors are messages, not failures\n\nWhen Python cannot do something, it raises an **exception** and prints a **traceback**. Read it from the **bottom**:\n\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"main.py\", line 3, in <module>\n    total = int(text)\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'twelve'\n```\n\nThe last line names the kind of error (`ValueError`) and what went wrong. The line above shows where. Common ones:\n\n| Error | Usually means |\n|---|---|\n| `SyntaxError` | a typo: missing colon, bracket or quote |\n| `NameError` | a misspelled variable or one used before it was created |\n| `TypeError` | mixing types, like `\"5\" + 3` |\n| `ValueError` | right type, bad value, like `int(\"twelve\")` |\n| `KeyError` | a dictionary key that does not exist |\n| `IndexError` | a list position past the end |\n| `ZeroDivisionError` | dividing by zero |\n\n## try / except: the safety net\n\nA trapeze artist works above a net. Most of the time it is not needed, but when something slips, the show goes on. `try` / `except` is that net:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    qty = int(user_text)\nexcept ValueError:\n    qty = 0\n    print(\"Please type a number\")\n```\n\nAlways catch a **specific** error. A bare `except:` catches everything, including your own typos, and hides bugs.\n\n## raise: refusing bad input\n\nSometimes your function should refuse to continue, like a bank teller refusing a cheque with no signature. Raise an exception with a helpful message:\n\n```python\ndef withdraw(balance, amount):\n    if amount <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Amount must be positive\")\n    if amount > balance:\n        raise ValueError(\"Not enough money\")\n    return balance - amount\n```\n\nThe code that calls `withdraw` decides what to do: show the message, log it, or try again.\n\n## Defensive programming\n\nData from people, files and the internet will be wrong sometimes. Check it at the door: is it the right type, in range, not empty? Fail early with a clear message rather than producing a wrong answer later. A wrong total in a report is far worse than an error that says \"row 14 has no amount\".\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `safe_divide(a, b)`: return `a / b`, or `None` if b is zero (catch `ZeroDivisionError`).\n2. `parse_quantity(text)`: turn text like `\" 12 \"` into the int `12`. Raise `ValueError` if it is not a whole number **or** if it is less than 1.\n3. `total_quantities(texts)`: add up the valid quantities in a list of texts, skipping bad ones, and return a tuple `(total, bad_count)`. Use your `parse_quantity` with try / except.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":7}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-errors$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-errors","title":"Errors and defensive code","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"Read tracebacks, catch exceptions with try / except, raise your own errors and validate input.","body":"## Errors are messages, not failures\n\nWhen Python cannot do something, it raises an **exception** and prints a **traceback**. Read it from the **bottom**:\n\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"main.py\", line 3, in <module>\n    total = int(text)\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'twelve'\n```\n\nThe last line names the kind of error (`ValueError`) and what went wrong. The line above shows where. Common ones:\n\n| Error | Usually means |\n|---|---|\n| `SyntaxError` | a typo: missing colon, bracket or quote |\n| `NameError` | a misspelled variable or one used before it was created |\n| `TypeError` | mixing types, like `\"5\" + 3` |\n| `ValueError` | right type, bad value, like `int(\"twelve\")` |\n| `KeyError` | a dictionary key that does not exist |\n| `IndexError` | a list position past the end |\n| `ZeroDivisionError` | dividing by zero |\n\n## try / except: the safety net\n\nA trapeze artist works above a net. Most of the time it is not needed, but when something slips, the show goes on. `try` / `except` is that net:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    qty = int(user_text)\nexcept ValueError:\n    qty = 0\n    print(\"Please type a number\")\n```\n\nAlways catch a **specific** error. A bare `except:` catches everything, including your own typos, and hides bugs.\n\n## raise: refusing bad input\n\nSometimes your function should refuse to continue, like a bank teller refusing a cheque with no signature. Raise an exception with a helpful message:\n\n```python\ndef withdraw(balance, amount):\n    if amount <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Amount must be positive\")\n    if amount > balance:\n        raise ValueError(\"Not enough money\")\n    return balance - amount\n```\n\nThe code that calls `withdraw` decides what to do: show the message, log it, or try again.\n\n## Defensive programming\n\nData from people, files and the internet will be wrong sometimes. Check it at the door: is it the right type, in range, not empty? Fail early with a clear message rather than producing a wrong answer later. A wrong total in a report is far worse than an error that says \"row 14 has no amount\".\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `safe_divide(a, b)`: return `a / b`, or `None` if b is zero (catch `ZeroDivisionError`).\n2. `parse_quantity(text)`: turn text like `\" 12 \"` into the int `12`. Raise `ValueError` if it is not a whole number **or** if it is less than 1.\n3. `total_quantities(texts)`: add up the valid quantities in a list of texts, skipping bad ones, and return a tuple `(total, bad_count)`. Use your `parse_quantity` with try / except.","task":"Write safe_divide, parse_quantity and total_quantities.","starter":"def safe_divide(a, b):\n    return a / b\n\ndef parse_quantity(text):\n    return int(text)\n\ndef total_quantities(texts):\n    pass\n","checks":[{"label":"safe_divide handles zero","test":"return await py(\"safe_divide(10, 4) == 2.5 and safe_divide(1, 0) is None\")"},{"label":"parse_quantity reads clean numbers","test":"return await py(\"parse_quantity(' 12 ') == 12\")"},{"label":"parse_quantity refuses bad input","test":"return await py(\"def _raises(t):\\n    try:\\n        parse_quantity(t)\\n    except ValueError:\\n        return True\\n    return False\\nall(_raises(t) for t in ['abc', '0', '-3', '2.5'])\")"},{"label":"total_quantities skips and counts bad rows","test":"return await py(\"total_quantities(['3', 'x', ' 4', '0', '10']) == (17, 2)\")"}],"hint":"parse_quantity: n = int(text.strip()) raises ValueError by itself for \"abc\" and \"2.5\"; then if n < 1: raise ValueError(\"Quantity must be at least 1\")."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-csv$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Reading data files (CSV)","kind":"code","task":"Write load and summarise for the sales CSV.","hint":"In summarise, loop over load(text); amount = float(row[\"amount\"].replace(\",\", \"\")) inside try, and except ValueError: bad += 1; continue.","body":"## CSV: the language spreadsheets speak\n\nAlmost every system can export a **CSV** file (comma-separated values): bank statements, POS sales, school results, Google Sheets. It is just text, one row per line, commas between columns:\n\n```\ndate,branch,product,amount\n2026-11-01,Wuse,Bread,1200\n2026-11-01,Garki,Milk,900\n```\n\nIf a spreadsheet is a printed table, a CSV is that same table read out loud over the phone: plain, simple, and understood everywhere.\n\n## The csv module\n\nPython comes with batteries included: a **module** is a toolbox you `import`. The `csv` module reads CSV text correctly, even tricky cases like a comma inside quotes (`\"Abuja, FCT\"`), which a simple `split(\",\")` would get wrong.\n\n```python\nimport csv\nimport io\n\ntext = open(\"sales.csv\").read()          # on your own computer\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(text)))\nrows[0]   # {'date': '2026-11-01', 'branch': 'Wuse', 'product': 'Bread', 'amount': '1200'}\n```\n\n`DictReader` uses the first line as the column names and gives each row as a dictionary. Notice that **every value is text**, even the amount: `'1200'`. You must convert numbers yourself.\n\nIn this lesson the data comes as a string in the code, through `io.StringIO`, which makes a string behave like a file. On your own computer you would use `open(\"file.csv\")` with exactly the same code after that.\n\n## Clean as you read\n\nReal exports contain blank lines, missing amounts and typos. The professional habit: **never crash on one bad row, and never silently include it**. Skip it, count it, and report it.\n\n```python\nfor row in rows:\n    try:\n        amount = float(row[\"amount\"].replace(\",\", \"\"))\n    except (ValueError, AttributeError):\n        bad += 1\n        continue        # skip to the next row\n```\n\n`continue` jumps straight to the next round of the loop.\n\n## Writing files\n\n`with open(\"report.txt\", \"w\") as f: f.write(text)` writes a file. The `with` block closes the file for you, even if something goes wrong.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe starter contains `DATA`, a small sales export with some bad rows.\n\n1. `load(text)`: return the rows as a list of dictionaries (use `csv.DictReader` and `io.StringIO`).\n2. `summarise(text)`: return a dictionary with:\n   - `\"total\"`: the sum of all valid amounts (amounts may contain commas, like `\"1,500\"`),\n   - `\"by_branch\"`: a dictionary of total per branch (branch names trimmed),\n   - `\"bad_rows\"`: how many rows had an amount that is missing or not a number.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":8}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-csv$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-csv","title":"Reading data files (CSV)","minutes":35,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"Read CSV data with the csv module, turn rows into records, clean values and build a summary report.","body":"## CSV: the language spreadsheets speak\n\nAlmost every system can export a **CSV** file (comma-separated values): bank statements, POS sales, school results, Google Sheets. It is just text, one row per line, commas between columns:\n\n```\ndate,branch,product,amount\n2026-11-01,Wuse,Bread,1200\n2026-11-01,Garki,Milk,900\n```\n\nIf a spreadsheet is a printed table, a CSV is that same table read out loud over the phone: plain, simple, and understood everywhere.\n\n## The csv module\n\nPython comes with batteries included: a **module** is a toolbox you `import`. The `csv` module reads CSV text correctly, even tricky cases like a comma inside quotes (`\"Abuja, FCT\"`), which a simple `split(\",\")` would get wrong.\n\n```python\nimport csv\nimport io\n\ntext = open(\"sales.csv\").read()          # on your own computer\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(text)))\nrows[0]   # {'date': '2026-11-01', 'branch': 'Wuse', 'product': 'Bread', 'amount': '1200'}\n```\n\n`DictReader` uses the first line as the column names and gives each row as a dictionary. Notice that **every value is text**, even the amount: `'1200'`. You must convert numbers yourself.\n\nIn this lesson the data comes as a string in the code, through `io.StringIO`, which makes a string behave like a file. On your own computer you would use `open(\"file.csv\")` with exactly the same code after that.\n\n## Clean as you read\n\nReal exports contain blank lines, missing amounts and typos. The professional habit: **never crash on one bad row, and never silently include it**. Skip it, count it, and report it.\n\n```python\nfor row in rows:\n    try:\n        amount = float(row[\"amount\"].replace(\",\", \"\"))\n    except (ValueError, AttributeError):\n        bad += 1\n        continue        # skip to the next row\n```\n\n`continue` jumps straight to the next round of the loop.\n\n## Writing files\n\n`with open(\"report.txt\", \"w\") as f: f.write(text)` writes a file. The `with` block closes the file for you, even if something goes wrong.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe starter contains `DATA`, a small sales export with some bad rows.\n\n1. `load(text)`: return the rows as a list of dictionaries (use `csv.DictReader` and `io.StringIO`).\n2. `summarise(text)`: return a dictionary with:\n   - `\"total\"`: the sum of all valid amounts (amounts may contain commas, like `\"1,500\"`),\n   - `\"by_branch\"`: a dictionary of total per branch (branch names trimmed),\n   - `\"bad_rows\"`: how many rows had an amount that is missing or not a number.","task":"Write load and summarise for the sales CSV.","starter":"import csv\nimport io\n\nDATA = \"\"\"date,branch,product,amount\n2026-11-01,Wuse,Bread,1200\n2026-11-01,Garki,Milk,900\n2026-11-02,Wuse ,Eggs,\"1,500\"\n2026-11-02,Garki,Rice,\n2026-11-03,Maitama,Oil,abc\n2026-11-03,Garki,Bread,1200\n\"\"\"\n\ndef load(text):\n    return []\n\ndef summarise(text):\n    return {}\n","checks":[{"label":"load gives 6 records with column names","test":"return await py(\"len(load(DATA)) == 6 and load(DATA)[0]['product'] == 'Bread'\")"},{"label":"total of valid amounts","test":"return await py(\"summarise(DATA)['total'] == 4800\")"},{"label":"totals per branch (names trimmed)","test":"return await py(\"summarise(DATA)['by_branch'] == {'Wuse': 2700, 'Garki': 2100}\")"},{"label":"bad rows counted","test":"return await py(\"summarise(DATA)['bad_rows'] == 2\")"}],"hint":"In summarise, loop over load(text); amount = float(row[\"amount\"].replace(\",\", \"\")) inside try, and except ValueError: bad += 1; continue."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-classes$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Classes and objects","kind":"code","task":"Write the Account class with deposit, withdraw, history and __str__.","hint":"Check the bad cases first and raise ValueError(\"...\"); only then change self.balance and append to self.history.","body":"## A class is a blueprint\n\nAn architect draws one blueprint for a house, and builders use it to put up many houses. Each house is separate: paint one blue and the others stay white. In Python, the blueprint is a **class** and each house built from it is an **object** (or *instance*).\n\n```python\nclass Account:\n    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):\n        self.owner = owner\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        self.balance += amount\n        return self.balance\n\nada = Account(\"Ada\")\nmusa = Account(\"Musa\", 5000)\nada.deposit(2000)\nprint(ada.balance, musa.balance)   # 2000 5000\n```\n\n## The parts\n\n- `class Account:` names the blueprint (class names use CapitalWords).\n- `__init__` runs automatically when an object is built. It sets up the object's starting data.\n- `self` means \"this particular object\". `self.balance` is *this* account's balance, not every account's.\n- **Attributes** are the object's data (`owner`, `balance`). **Methods** are functions that belong to it (`deposit`).\n\n## Why bother?\n\nFunctions and dictionaries can do a lot. Classes help when data and the rules about that data belong together. An account's balance must never be changed carelessly: the only way in is through `deposit` and `withdraw`, which check the rules. That idea, keeping the rules next to the data, is called **encapsulation**. It is how banking, inventory and booking systems stay correct.\n\n## Keeping objects valid\n\n```python\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Amount must be positive\")\n        if amount > self.balance:\n            raise ValueError(\"Insufficient funds\")\n        self.balance -= amount\n```\n\n## A history list\n\nObjects can hold lists. Recording every transaction in `self.history` gives you a statement for free, the way a bank keeps a ledger rather than just a final number.\n\n## __str__: a friendly description\n\nDefine `__str__(self)` to control what `print(ada)` shows, for example `Ada: 2000`.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a class `Account`:\n\n1. `Account(owner, balance=0)` stores both, and starts `self.history` as an empty list.\n2. `deposit(amount)`: raise `ValueError` if amount is not positive; otherwise add it, append `(\"deposit\", amount)` to history and return the new balance.\n3. `withdraw(amount)`: raise `ValueError` if amount is not positive or more than the balance; otherwise subtract, append `(\"withdraw\", amount)` and return the new balance.\n4. `__str__` returns `\"Ada: 2000\"` (owner, colon, space, balance).","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":9}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-classes$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-classes","title":"Classes and objects","minutes":35,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"Model real things with classes: attributes, methods, __init__, and keeping data valid inside an object.","body":"## A class is a blueprint\n\nAn architect draws one blueprint for a house, and builders use it to put up many houses. Each house is separate: paint one blue and the others stay white. In Python, the blueprint is a **class** and each house built from it is an **object** (or *instance*).\n\n```python\nclass Account:\n    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):\n        self.owner = owner\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        self.balance += amount\n        return self.balance\n\nada = Account(\"Ada\")\nmusa = Account(\"Musa\", 5000)\nada.deposit(2000)\nprint(ada.balance, musa.balance)   # 2000 5000\n```\n\n## The parts\n\n- `class Account:` names the blueprint (class names use CapitalWords).\n- `__init__` runs automatically when an object is built. It sets up the object's starting data.\n- `self` means \"this particular object\". `self.balance` is *this* account's balance, not every account's.\n- **Attributes** are the object's data (`owner`, `balance`). **Methods** are functions that belong to it (`deposit`).\n\n## Why bother?\n\nFunctions and dictionaries can do a lot. Classes help when data and the rules about that data belong together. An account's balance must never be changed carelessly: the only way in is through `deposit` and `withdraw`, which check the rules. That idea, keeping the rules next to the data, is called **encapsulation**. It is how banking, inventory and booking systems stay correct.\n\n## Keeping objects valid\n\n```python\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Amount must be positive\")\n        if amount > self.balance:\n            raise ValueError(\"Insufficient funds\")\n        self.balance -= amount\n```\n\n## A history list\n\nObjects can hold lists. Recording every transaction in `self.history` gives you a statement for free, the way a bank keeps a ledger rather than just a final number.\n\n## __str__: a friendly description\n\nDefine `__str__(self)` to control what `print(ada)` shows, for example `Ada: 2000`.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a class `Account`:\n\n1. `Account(owner, balance=0)` stores both, and starts `self.history` as an empty list.\n2. `deposit(amount)`: raise `ValueError` if amount is not positive; otherwise add it, append `(\"deposit\", amount)` to history and return the new balance.\n3. `withdraw(amount)`: raise `ValueError` if amount is not positive or more than the balance; otherwise subtract, append `(\"withdraw\", amount)` and return the new balance.\n4. `__str__` returns `\"Ada: 2000\"` (owner, colon, space, balance).","task":"Write the Account class with deposit, withdraw, history and __str__.","starter":"class Account:\n    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):\n        self.owner = owner\n","checks":[{"label":"stores owner and balance","test":"return await py(\"a = Account('Ada', 500)\\na.owner == 'Ada' and a.balance == 500 and Account('Bo').balance == 0 and a.history == []\")"},{"label":"deposit adds and records","test":"return await py(\"a = Account('Ada')\\na.deposit(2000) == 2000 and a.history == [('deposit', 2000)]\")"},{"label":"withdraw subtracts and records","test":"return await py(\"a = Account('Ada', 1000)\\na.withdraw(300) == 700 and a.history == [('withdraw', 300)]\")"},{"label":"bad amounts are refused","test":"return await py(\"def _r(f):\\n    try:\\n        f()\\n    except ValueError:\\n        return True\\n    return False\\na = Account('Ada', 100)\\n_r(lambda: a.withdraw(500)) and _r(lambda: a.deposit(0)) and _r(lambda: a.withdraw(-1)) and a.balance == 100\")"},{"label":"prints nicely","test":"return await py(\"str(Account('Ada', 2000)) == 'Ada: 2000'\")"}],"hint":"Check the bad cases first and raise ValueError(\"...\"); only then change self.balance and append to self.history."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-pro$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Writing Python like a professional","kind":"code","task":"Write with_vat, top_products, buses_needed and median_sale.","hint":"top_products: [p[\"name\"] for p in sorted(products, key=lambda p: p[\"sold\"], reverse=True)[:n]]","body":"## Comprehensions: a loop in one line\n\nMany loops build a new list from an old one. Python has a short form for that, called a **comprehension**. It reads like a sentence: *\"give me the price times 1.075, for each price in prices, if the price is over 1000\"*.\n\n```python\nprices = [500, 1200, 3000]\nwith_vat = [p * 1.075 for p in prices]\nbig = [p for p in prices if p > 1000]\nby_name = {name: len(name) for name in [\"Ada\", \"Musa\"]}   # dictionary comprehension\n```\n\nUse them for simple transformations. If you need several steps or an `if` / `else` chain, a normal loop is clearer.\n\n## Sorting with a key\n\n`sorted` sorts numbers and text. To sort records, tell it **what to compare** with `key`. A `lambda` is a tiny function without a name, perfect for this:\n\n```python\nproducts = [{\"name\": \"Rice\", \"sold\": 40}, {\"name\": \"Oil\", \"sold\": 95}]\nsorted(products, key=lambda p: p[\"sold\"], reverse=True)   # best sellers first\n```\n\nThink of `key` as telling a librarian whether to arrange the books by title, author or year.\n\n## The standard library\n\nPython ships with hundreds of modules. A few you will use constantly:\n\n- `math`: `math.sqrt`, `math.ceil` (round up: how many buses for 130 pupils if each takes 50?)\n- `statistics`: `mean`, `median`, `stdev`\n- `datetime`: dates and times, `date(2026, 12, 25) - date.today()`\n- `random`, `json`, `csv`, `re` (patterns in text), `pathlib` (files and folders)\n\nBeyond it, the Python Package Index has more than half a million free packages: `pandas` for data, `requests` for websites and APIs, `Django` and `FastAPI` for web back ends, `scikit-learn` and `PyTorch` for machine learning.\n\n## Clean code habits\n\n- Names that explain: `unpaid_invoices`, not `lst2`.\n- Small functions that do one thing.\n- No magic numbers: `VAT_RATE = 7.5` at the top, not `7.5` scattered everywhere.\n- Follow **PEP 8**, the Python style guide: 4-space indents, lowercase_with_underscores names.\n- Test your functions with a few known answers, the way the checks in this course do. `assert average([2, 4]) == 3` stops the program loudly if it is ever wrong.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `with_vat(prices, rate=7.5)`: a list comprehension returning each price with VAT added, rounded to 2 decimals.\n2. `top_products(products, n)`: products is a list of dictionaries with `\"name\"` and `\"sold\"`. Return the **names** of the `n` best sellers, best first.\n3. `buses_needed(pupils, seats=50)`: how many buses (round up, use `math.ceil`).\n4. `median_sale(sales)`: the median, using the `statistics` module.","practice":null,"track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":10}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-pro$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-pro","title":"Writing Python like a professional","minutes":35,"kind":"code","lang":"python","outline":"List and dictionary comprehensions, sorting with key and lambda, the standard library and clean code habits.","body":"## Comprehensions: a loop in one line\n\nMany loops build a new list from an old one. Python has a short form for that, called a **comprehension**. It reads like a sentence: *\"give me the price times 1.075, for each price in prices, if the price is over 1000\"*.\n\n```python\nprices = [500, 1200, 3000]\nwith_vat = [p * 1.075 for p in prices]\nbig = [p for p in prices if p > 1000]\nby_name = {name: len(name) for name in [\"Ada\", \"Musa\"]}   # dictionary comprehension\n```\n\nUse them for simple transformations. If you need several steps or an `if` / `else` chain, a normal loop is clearer.\n\n## Sorting with a key\n\n`sorted` sorts numbers and text. To sort records, tell it **what to compare** with `key`. A `lambda` is a tiny function without a name, perfect for this:\n\n```python\nproducts = [{\"name\": \"Rice\", \"sold\": 40}, {\"name\": \"Oil\", \"sold\": 95}]\nsorted(products, key=lambda p: p[\"sold\"], reverse=True)   # best sellers first\n```\n\nThink of `key` as telling a librarian whether to arrange the books by title, author or year.\n\n## The standard library\n\nPython ships with hundreds of modules. A few you will use constantly:\n\n- `math`: `math.sqrt`, `math.ceil` (round up: how many buses for 130 pupils if each takes 50?)\n- `statistics`: `mean`, `median`, `stdev`\n- `datetime`: dates and times, `date(2026, 12, 25) - date.today()`\n- `random`, `json`, `csv`, `re` (patterns in text), `pathlib` (files and folders)\n\nBeyond it, the Python Package Index has more than half a million free packages: `pandas` for data, `requests` for websites and APIs, `Django` and `FastAPI` for web back ends, `scikit-learn` and `PyTorch` for machine learning.\n\n## Clean code habits\n\n- Names that explain: `unpaid_invoices`, not `lst2`.\n- Small functions that do one thing.\n- No magic numbers: `VAT_RATE = 7.5` at the top, not `7.5` scattered everywhere.\n- Follow **PEP 8**, the Python style guide: 4-space indents, lowercase_with_underscores names.\n- Test your functions with a few known answers, the way the checks in this course do. `assert average([2, 4]) == 3` stops the program loudly if it is ever wrong.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `with_vat(prices, rate=7.5)`: a list comprehension returning each price with VAT added, rounded to 2 decimals.\n2. `top_products(products, n)`: products is a list of dictionaries with `\"name\"` and `\"sold\"`. Return the **names** of the `n` best sellers, best first.\n3. `buses_needed(pupils, seats=50)`: how many buses (round up, use `math.ceil`).\n4. `median_sale(sales)`: the median, using the `statistics` module.","task":"Write with_vat, top_products, buses_needed and median_sale.","starter":"import math\nimport statistics\n\ndef with_vat(prices, rate=7.5):\n    pass\n\ndef top_products(products, n):\n    pass\n\ndef buses_needed(pupils, seats=50):\n    pass\n\ndef median_sale(sales):\n    pass\n","checks":[{"label":"with_vat adds VAT to every price","test":"return await py(\"with_vat([1000, 200]) == [1075.0, 215.0] and with_vat([100], rate=10) == [110.0]\")"},{"label":"top_products returns the best names in order","test":"return await py(\"top_products([{'name': 'Rice', 'sold': 40}, {'name': 'Oil', 'sold': 95}, {'name': 'Salt', 'sold': 60}], 2) == ['Oil', 'Salt']\")"},{"label":"buses_needed rounds up","test":"return await py(\"buses_needed(130) == 3 and buses_needed(100) == 2 and buses_needed(10, seats=4) == 3\")"},{"label":"median_sale","test":"return await py(\"median_sale([5, 1, 9, 3]) == 4 and median_sale([7]) == 7\")"}],"hint":"top_products: [p[\"name\"] for p in sorted(products, key=lambda p: p[\"sold\"], reverse=True)[:n]]"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$python-ai-career$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Python with AI, and your next steps","kind":"prompt","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## AI is a pair programmer, not a replacement for understanding\n\nAI assistants (like the tutor in CDM Studio) can write a working Python function in seconds. That is powerful, and it changes how programmers work. But an AI is like a very fast junior colleague who is confident even when wrong. **You** are the senior: you must read, test and understand every line before you trust it.\n\n## Good ways to use AI with Python\n\n1. **Explain**: paste code and ask \"explain this line by line for a beginner\".\n2. **Debug**: paste the full traceback *and* the code. Ask for the cause first, then the fix. Last line of the traceback first, remember?\n3. **Generate tests**: \"write 5 test cases, including edge cases, for this function\".\n4. **Review**: \"how could this be clearer or safer?\"\n5. **Translate**: \"how would I do this with pandas?\"\n\n## A strong prompt has context\n\nWeak: *\"fix my code\"*.\nStrong: *\"This Python function should return the total per branch from a list of dictionaries. For the input below it returns an empty dictionary. Here is the code and the input. Explain the bug, then show the corrected function.\"*\n\n## Rules for safety\n\n- Never paste passwords, API keys or customers' personal data into any AI tool.\n- Run generated code on test data first.\n- If you cannot explain a line, do not ship it.\n\n## What to learn next\n\n| If you want to… | Learn |\n|---|---|\n| Analyse data and make charts | pandas, matplotlib, Jupyter notebooks (and the **Data analytics** course) |\n| Build websites and APIs | FastAPI or Django, databases (the **SQL** course) |\n| Automate office work | openpyxl (Excel), python-docx, schedules, emails |\n| Build AI apps | APIs of AI models, the **AI engineering** and **Machine learning** courses |\n\n## Earning with Python\n\n- **Portfolio**: put 3 to 5 small, real projects on GitHub with a clear README: a sales report generator, a price tracker, a quiz bot. Your final project in this course is a good first one.\n- **Freelancing**: automation scripts, data cleaning and report generation are common small jobs on Upwork and Fiverr.\n- **Jobs**: junior developer, data analyst, QA automation, back-end developer. Practise on sites like Exercism or LeetCode (easy problems) before interviews.\n\n## Practise\n\nUse the tutor to explain, debug and test code.","practice":"Paste one of your solutions from this course (for example summarise from the CSV lesson) and ask the tutor to: 1) explain it line by line, 2) suggest 3 edge cases that could break it, 3) suggest one improvement. Then deliberately break your code (remove a colon or misspell a variable), paste the error and ask the tutor to explain the traceback before giving the fix.","track":"python","track_title":"Python programming","paid":true,"order":11}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$python-ai-career$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"python-ai-career","title":"Python with AI, and your next steps","minutes":25,"kind":"prompt","outline":"Use AI to write, explain and debug code safely; what to learn next; building a portfolio and earning with Python.","body":"## AI is a pair programmer, not a replacement for understanding\n\nAI assistants (like the tutor in CDM Studio) can write a working Python function in seconds. That is powerful, and it changes how programmers work. But an AI is like a very fast junior colleague who is confident even when wrong. **You** are the senior: you must read, test and understand every line before you trust it.\n\n## Good ways to use AI with Python\n\n1. **Explain**: paste code and ask \"explain this line by line for a beginner\".\n2. **Debug**: paste the full traceback *and* the code. Ask for the cause first, then the fix. Last line of the traceback first, remember?\n3. **Generate tests**: \"write 5 test cases, including edge cases, for this function\".\n4. **Review**: \"how could this be clearer or safer?\"\n5. **Translate**: \"how would I do this with pandas?\"\n\n## A strong prompt has context\n\nWeak: *\"fix my code\"*.\nStrong: *\"This Python function should return the total per branch from a list of dictionaries. For the input below it returns an empty dictionary. Here is the code and the input. Explain the bug, then show the corrected function.\"*\n\n## Rules for safety\n\n- Never paste passwords, API keys or customers' personal data into any AI tool.\n- Run generated code on test data first.\n- If you cannot explain a line, do not ship it.\n\n## What to learn next\n\n| If you want to… | Learn |\n|---|---|\n| Analyse data and make charts | pandas, matplotlib, Jupyter notebooks (and the **Data analytics** course) |\n| Build websites and APIs | FastAPI or Django, databases (the **SQL** course) |\n| Automate office work | openpyxl (Excel), python-docx, schedules, emails |\n| Build AI apps | APIs of AI models, the **AI engineering** and **Machine learning** courses |\n\n## Earning with Python\n\n- **Portfolio**: put 3 to 5 small, real projects on GitHub with a clear README: a sales report generator, a price tracker, a quiz bot. Your final project in this course is a good first one.\n- **Freelancing**: automation scripts, data cleaning and report generation are common small jobs on Upwork and Fiverr.\n- **Jobs**: junior developer, data analyst, QA automation, back-end developer. Practise on sites like Exercism or LeetCode (easy problems) before interviews.\n\n## Practise\n\nUse the tutor to explain, debug and test code.","practice":"Paste one of your solutions from this course (for example summarise from the CSV lesson) and ask the tutor to: 1) explain it line by line, 2) suggest 3 edge cases that could break it, 3) suggest one improvement. Then deliberately break your code (remove a colon or misspell a variable), paste the error and ask the tutor to explain the traceback before giving the fix."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
