insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-select$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Databases and your first query","kind":"code","task":"Select name, category, price and price_in_usd from products.","hint":"select name, category, price, price / 1600.0 as price_in_usd from products;","body":"## What is a database?\n\nPicture a school office with a big filing cabinet. One drawer holds student records, another holds fees paid, another holds teachers. Every folder in a drawer has the same layout. A **database** is that cabinet, kept by a computer: it stores information in **tables**, and it can answer questions about millions of records in a fraction of a second.\n\n- A **table** is a drawer: customers, products, orders.\n- A **row** is one folder: one customer.\n- A **column** is one field on the form: name, city, date joined.\n\nBanks, hospitals, schools, shops and apps like WhatsApp and Uber all keep their data in databases. **SQL** (say *\"S-Q-L\"* or *\"sequel\"*) is the language you use to ask them questions. It has been around since the 1970s, and it is still one of the most requested skills in job adverts, because nearly every company has a database.\n\n## Your first query\n\n```sql\nselect name, city from customers;\n```\n\nRead it as a sentence: *\"select the name and city from the customers table\"*.\n\n- `select` lists the columns you want, separated by commas.\n- `*` means every column: `select * from customers;`\n- `from` names the table.\n- The semicolon `;` ends the statement.\n\nSQL does not care about capital letters in keywords: `SELECT` and `select` are the same. Many people write keywords in capitals to make queries easier to scan. Text values, though, must match exactly and go in **single quotes**: `'Abuja'`.\n\n## Calculations in a query\n\nYou can calculate new columns and give them a name with `as`:\n\n```sql\nselect name, price, price * 1.075 as price_with_vat from products;\n```\n\nThe database does not change: `select` only **reads**. You cannot break anything with it, so explore freely.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a query that shows each product's `name`, `category` and `price`, in that order, plus a fourth column called `price_in_usd` that is the price divided by 1600 (use `price / 1600.0` so you get decimals).","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":false,"order":0}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-where$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Filtering rows with WHERE","kind":"code","task":"Filter the paid orders from October 2026 onwards with a total of at least 12,000.","hint":"Three conditions joined with and: status = 'paid', order_date >= '2026-10-01', total >= 12000.","body":"## WHERE is a sieve\n\nWhen you pour rice through a sieve, only what fits passes. `where` is a sieve for rows: only rows that make the condition **true** come through.\n\n```sql\nselect name, price from products where price > 10000;\nselect * from orders where status = 'paid';\n```\n\n## Comparisons\n\n`=` equal (just one sign in SQL), `<>` or `!=` not equal, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`.\n\n## Combining conditions\n\n```sql\nselect * from products where category = 'Food' and price < 20000;\nselect * from customers where city = 'Lagos' or city = 'Abuja';\n```\n\nWhen you mix `and` with `or`, use brackets so the meaning is clear: `where status = 'paid' and (city = 'Lagos' or city = 'Abuja')`.\n\n## Handy shortcuts\n\n- `in`: matches any value in a list: `where city in ('Lagos', 'Abuja')`\n- `between`: a range, including both ends: `where price between 1000 and 5000`\n- `like`: a text pattern. `%` means \"anything\", `_` means \"exactly one character\": `where name like 'P%'` finds names that start with P; `like '%bank%'` finds names containing *bank*.\n- `not`: flips a condition: `where status not in ('cancelled')`\n\n## NULL: the empty box\n\n`NULL` means **unknown or missing**, not zero and not empty text. Emeka's city is NULL: we simply do not know it. Because NULL is unknown, `city = NULL` is never true (is an unknown thing equal to another unknown thing? SQL cannot say). Always write:\n\n```sql\nselect * from customers where city is null;\nselect * from customers where city is not null;\n```\n\nThis trips up experienced people too, so remember it.\n\n## Dates as text\n\nIn this database dates are text in the form `YYYY-MM-DD`, which sorts and compares correctly: `where order_date >= '2026-10-01'`.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nShow the `id`, `order_date` and `total` (in that order) of orders that are **paid**, placed **on or after 1 October 2026**, with a total of **at least 12,000**.","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":false,"order":1}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-sort$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Sorting, limiting and unique values","kind":"code","task":"The 3 cheapest products outside Stationery, cheapest first.","hint":"where category <> 'Stationery' order by price limit 3","body":"## Rows have no order until you ask\n\nA database is like a pile of exam scripts on a desk: there is no promised order. If you want them by score, you must say so. Never rely on the order you happen to see.\n\n```sql\nselect name, price from products order by price;          -- cheapest first (asc is the default)\nselect name, price from products order by price desc;     -- dearest first\nselect name, city from customers order by city, name;     -- by city, then by name within each city\n```\n\n## LIMIT: just the top few\n\n```sql\nselect name, price from products order by price desc limit 3;   -- the 3 most expensive\n```\n\n`limit` without `order by` gives you *some* rows, not the top ones. Always sort first. `offset 3` skips the first 3, which is how apps show \"page 2\" of results.\n\n## DISTINCT: remove repeats\n\n```sql\nselect distinct city from customers;\n```\n\nThis gives each city once. It is like asking \"which towns do our customers come from?\" rather than reading every customer's address.\n\n## Aliases\n\n`as` renames a column in the result, which makes reports readable:\n\n```sql\nselect name as product, price as \"price (NGN)\" from products;\n```\n\n## Writing order versus running order\n\nYou write: `select … from … where … order by … limit …`. The database works roughly in the order **from → where → select → order by → limit**: it finds the table, filters the rows, picks the columns, sorts, then cuts. Knowing this explains many surprises later.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nShow the `name` and `price` of the **3 cheapest** products that are **not** in the Stationery category, cheapest first.","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":2}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-sort$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-sort","title":"Sorting, limiting and unique values","minutes":25,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"ORDER BY, LIMIT and OFFSET, DISTINCT, and readable aliases.","body":"## Rows have no order until you ask\n\nA database is like a pile of exam scripts on a desk: there is no promised order. If you want them by score, you must say so. Never rely on the order you happen to see.\n\n```sql\nselect name, price from products order by price;          -- cheapest first (asc is the default)\nselect name, price from products order by price desc;     -- dearest first\nselect name, city from customers order by city, name;     -- by city, then by name within each city\n```\n\n## LIMIT: just the top few\n\n```sql\nselect name, price from products order by price desc limit 3;   -- the 3 most expensive\n```\n\n`limit` without `order by` gives you *some* rows, not the top ones. Always sort first. `offset 3` skips the first 3, which is how apps show \"page 2\" of results.\n\n## DISTINCT: remove repeats\n\n```sql\nselect distinct city from customers;\n```\n\nThis gives each city once. It is like asking \"which towns do our customers come from?\" rather than reading every customer's address.\n\n## Aliases\n\n`as` renames a column in the result, which makes reports readable:\n\n```sql\nselect name as product, price as \"price (NGN)\" from products;\n```\n\n## Writing order versus running order\n\nYou write: `select … from … where … order by … limit …`. The database works roughly in the order **from → where → select → order by → limit**: it finds the table, filters the rows, picks the columns, sorts, then cuts. Knowing this explains many surprises later.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nShow the `name` and `price` of the **3 cheapest** products that are **not** in the Stationery category, cheapest first.","task":"The 3 cheapest products outside Stationery, cheapest first.","starter":"select name, price from products;\n","checks":[{"label":"name and price of the 3 cheapest non-Stationery products, in order","test":"return await same(\"select name, price from products where category <> 'Stationery' order by price limit 3\", true)"}],"hint":"where category <> 'Stationery' order by price limit 3"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-aggregates$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Counting and adding up","kind":"code","task":"One row: orders, revenue, average_order and biggest for paid orders.","hint":"select count(*) as orders, sum(total) as revenue, round(avg(total), 2) as average_order, max(total) as biggest from orders where status = 'paid';","body":"## From many rows to one number\n\nA manager rarely wants to read every receipt. She wants: *How many orders? How much money? What was the average sale?* **Aggregate functions** squeeze many rows into one answer, like a cashier counting the day's takings into a single figure.\n\n```sql\nselect count(*) from orders;                 -- how many rows\nselect sum(total) from orders;               -- add up a column\nselect avg(total) from orders;               -- the mean\nselect min(total), max(total) from orders;   -- smallest and largest\n```\n\nCombine them with `where` to count only some rows:\n\n```sql\nselect count(*) as paid_orders, sum(total) as revenue\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid';\n```\n\nThe `where` runs **first**, so the sums only see paid orders. This is the difference between \"all orders\" and \"revenue\": cancelled and pending orders are not money in the bank.\n\n## Counting carefully\n\n- `count(*)` counts rows.\n- `count(city)` counts rows where city is **not NULL**. With Emeka's missing city, `count(city)` from customers is 6, while `count(*)` is 7.\n- `count(distinct city)` counts different cities.\n\n## Rounding\n\n`round(avg(total), 2)` keeps two decimal places, which is what money needs. In SQLite, dividing two whole numbers gives a whole number (`7 / 2` is `3`); write `7 / 2.0` or multiply by `1.0` to keep decimals. PostgreSQL behaves the same way, so it is a good habit everywhere.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite **one** query, over **paid orders only**, that returns a single row with these columns in this order:\n\n1. `orders`: the number of paid orders\n2. `revenue`: their total\n3. `average_order`: the average total, rounded to 2 decimals\n4. `biggest`: the largest total","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":3}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-aggregates$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-aggregates","title":"Counting and adding up","minutes":25,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX and ROUND turn many rows into one answer.","body":"## From many rows to one number\n\nA manager rarely wants to read every receipt. She wants: *How many orders? How much money? What was the average sale?* **Aggregate functions** squeeze many rows into one answer, like a cashier counting the day's takings into a single figure.\n\n```sql\nselect count(*) from orders;                 -- how many rows\nselect sum(total) from orders;               -- add up a column\nselect avg(total) from orders;               -- the mean\nselect min(total), max(total) from orders;   -- smallest and largest\n```\n\nCombine them with `where` to count only some rows:\n\n```sql\nselect count(*) as paid_orders, sum(total) as revenue\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid';\n```\n\nThe `where` runs **first**, so the sums only see paid orders. This is the difference between \"all orders\" and \"revenue\": cancelled and pending orders are not money in the bank.\n\n## Counting carefully\n\n- `count(*)` counts rows.\n- `count(city)` counts rows where city is **not NULL**. With Emeka's missing city, `count(city)` from customers is 6, while `count(*)` is 7.\n- `count(distinct city)` counts different cities.\n\n## Rounding\n\n`round(avg(total), 2)` keeps two decimal places, which is what money needs. In SQLite, dividing two whole numbers gives a whole number (`7 / 2` is `3`); write `7 / 2.0` or multiply by `1.0` to keep decimals. PostgreSQL behaves the same way, so it is a good habit everywhere.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite **one** query, over **paid orders only**, that returns a single row with these columns in this order:\n\n1. `orders`: the number of paid orders\n2. `revenue`: their total\n3. `average_order`: the average total, rounded to 2 decimals\n4. `biggest`: the largest total","task":"One row: orders, revenue, average_order and biggest for paid orders.","starter":"select count(*) from orders;\n","checks":[{"label":"one row with the four paid-order figures","test":"return await same(\"select count(*), sum(total), round(avg(total), 2), max(total) from orders where status = 'paid'\")"}],"hint":"select count(*) as orders, sum(total) as revenue, round(avg(total), 2) as average_order, max(total) as biggest from orders where status = 'paid';"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-group$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"GROUP BY: totals per group","kind":"code","task":"Paid orders per customer, only those who spent over 20,000, biggest first.","hint":"where status = 'paid' group by customer_id having sum(total) > 20000 order by spent desc","body":"## Sorting coins into piles\n\nEmpty a jar of coins on the table. Before you count, you sort them into piles: all the ₦10 coins together, all the ₦20 coins together. Then you count each pile. That is exactly what `group by` does: it sorts rows into piles that share a value, then runs the aggregate on **each pile**.\n\n```sql\nselect category, count(*) as products, avg(price) as avg_price\nfrom products\ngroup by category;\n```\n\nOne row comes out **per group**: one for Food, one for Electronics, one for Stationery.\n\n## The golden rule\n\nEvery column in `select` must either be **in the group by** or **inside an aggregate**. `select category, name, count(*) … group by category` makes no sense: which of the many names in the Food pile should it show? (SQLite quietly picks one, which hides bugs; PostgreSQL refuses with an error. Follow the rule.)\n\n## Grouping by more than one column\n\n`group by customer_id, status` makes a pile for each combination: Ada's paid orders, Ada's pending orders, and so on.\n\n## HAVING: filter the piles\n\n`where` filters **rows before** grouping. `having` filters **groups after** grouping:\n\n```sql\nselect customer_id, sum(total) as spent\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid'          -- only paid rows go into the piles\ngroup by customer_id\nhaving sum(total) > 30000      -- only keep the big piles\norder by spent desc;\n```\n\nRemember the running order: **from → where → group by → having → select → order by → limit**.\n\n## Business questions this answers\n\nSales per branch, students per class, average fee per term, complaints per product, top customers. Most dashboards in the world are `group by` queries underneath.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nFor **paid** orders, show each `customer_id` with the number of orders as `orders` and the amount spent as `spent`, but only customers who spent **more than 20,000**. Sort by `spent`, highest first.","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":4}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-group$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-group","title":"GROUP BY: totals per group","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"Summaries per category, customer or month with GROUP BY, and filtering groups with HAVING.","body":"## Sorting coins into piles\n\nEmpty a jar of coins on the table. Before you count, you sort them into piles: all the ₦10 coins together, all the ₦20 coins together. Then you count each pile. That is exactly what `group by` does: it sorts rows into piles that share a value, then runs the aggregate on **each pile**.\n\n```sql\nselect category, count(*) as products, avg(price) as avg_price\nfrom products\ngroup by category;\n```\n\nOne row comes out **per group**: one for Food, one for Electronics, one for Stationery.\n\n## The golden rule\n\nEvery column in `select` must either be **in the group by** or **inside an aggregate**. `select category, name, count(*) … group by category` makes no sense: which of the many names in the Food pile should it show? (SQLite quietly picks one, which hides bugs; PostgreSQL refuses with an error. Follow the rule.)\n\n## Grouping by more than one column\n\n`group by customer_id, status` makes a pile for each combination: Ada's paid orders, Ada's pending orders, and so on.\n\n## HAVING: filter the piles\n\n`where` filters **rows before** grouping. `having` filters **groups after** grouping:\n\n```sql\nselect customer_id, sum(total) as spent\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid'          -- only paid rows go into the piles\ngroup by customer_id\nhaving sum(total) > 30000      -- only keep the big piles\norder by spent desc;\n```\n\nRemember the running order: **from → where → group by → having → select → order by → limit**.\n\n## Business questions this answers\n\nSales per branch, students per class, average fee per term, complaints per product, top customers. Most dashboards in the world are `group by` queries underneath.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nFor **paid** orders, show each `customer_id` with the number of orders as `orders` and the amount spent as `spent`, but only customers who spent **more than 20,000**. Sort by `spent`, highest first.","task":"Paid orders per customer, only those who spent over 20,000, biggest first.","starter":"select customer_id, total from orders;\n","checks":[{"label":"customer_id, orders and spent for big paid customers, biggest first","test":"return await same(\"select customer_id, count(*), sum(total) from orders where status = 'paid' group by customer_id having sum(total) > 20000 order by sum(total) desc\", true)"}],"hint":"where status = 'paid' group by customer_id having sum(total) > 20000 order by spent desc"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-joins$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Joining tables","kind":"code","task":"Revenue per customer city from paid orders, highest first.","hint":"select c.city, sum(o.total) as revenue from orders o join customers c on c.id = o.customer_id where o.status = 'paid' group by c.city order by revenue desc;","body":"## Why split data into tables?\n\nImagine writing Ada's full name and city on every one of her orders. If she moves to Lagos, you must fix every order, and you will miss one. Instead, each customer is stored **once**, and each order just says *which* customer by their `id`. That number is like a student's admission number: short, unique and never confused with someone else who has the same name.\n\n- A **primary key** is the unique id of each row: `customers.id`.\n- A **foreign key** is a column that points to another table's primary key: `orders.customer_id` points to `customers.id`.\n\n## JOIN: put the pieces back together\n\nTo show orders with the customer's name, we **join** the two tables where the keys match:\n\n```sql\nselect orders.id, customers.name, orders.total\nfrom orders\njoin customers on customers.id = orders.customer_id;\n```\n\nThink of matching two class lists by admission number: for every order, find the customer whose id equals the order's customer_id, and glue the two rows side by side.\n\n## Table aliases\n\nLong table names get tiring. Give each one a short alias:\n\n```sql\nselect o.id, c.name, o.total\nfrom orders o\njoin customers c on c.id = o.customer_id\nwhere o.status = 'paid';\n```\n\n## Many tables\n\nJoins chain. To see which products were in each order, go orders → order_items → products:\n\n```sql\nselect o.id, p.name, i.qty, p.price * i.qty as line_total\nfrom orders o\njoin order_items i on i.order_id = o.id\njoin products p on p.id = i.product_id;\n```\n\n## Inner join drops the unmatched\n\nA plain `join` (also written `inner join`) only keeps rows that have a match on **both** sides. Grace and Emeka have no orders, so they never appear in the query above. The next lesson shows how to keep them.\n\n## Joins with GROUP BY\n\nJoin first, then group, and you can answer questions like \"revenue per city\" or \"units sold per product\".\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nShow **revenue per city** from **paid** orders: columns `city` and `revenue`, highest revenue first. (Join orders to customers, filter, then group by city.)","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":5}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-joins$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-joins","title":"Joining tables","minutes":35,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"Why data is split into tables, keys, and INNER JOIN to combine them, including joins across three or four tables.","body":"## Why split data into tables?\n\nImagine writing Ada's full name and city on every one of her orders. If she moves to Lagos, you must fix every order, and you will miss one. Instead, each customer is stored **once**, and each order just says *which* customer by their `id`. That number is like a student's admission number: short, unique and never confused with someone else who has the same name.\n\n- A **primary key** is the unique id of each row: `customers.id`.\n- A **foreign key** is a column that points to another table's primary key: `orders.customer_id` points to `customers.id`.\n\n## JOIN: put the pieces back together\n\nTo show orders with the customer's name, we **join** the two tables where the keys match:\n\n```sql\nselect orders.id, customers.name, orders.total\nfrom orders\njoin customers on customers.id = orders.customer_id;\n```\n\nThink of matching two class lists by admission number: for every order, find the customer whose id equals the order's customer_id, and glue the two rows side by side.\n\n## Table aliases\n\nLong table names get tiring. Give each one a short alias:\n\n```sql\nselect o.id, c.name, o.total\nfrom orders o\njoin customers c on c.id = o.customer_id\nwhere o.status = 'paid';\n```\n\n## Many tables\n\nJoins chain. To see which products were in each order, go orders → order_items → products:\n\n```sql\nselect o.id, p.name, i.qty, p.price * i.qty as line_total\nfrom orders o\njoin order_items i on i.order_id = o.id\njoin products p on p.id = i.product_id;\n```\n\n## Inner join drops the unmatched\n\nA plain `join` (also written `inner join`) only keeps rows that have a match on **both** sides. Grace and Emeka have no orders, so they never appear in the query above. The next lesson shows how to keep them.\n\n## Joins with GROUP BY\n\nJoin first, then group, and you can answer questions like \"revenue per city\" or \"units sold per product\".\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nShow **revenue per city** from **paid** orders: columns `city` and `revenue`, highest revenue first. (Join orders to customers, filter, then group by city.)","task":"Revenue per customer city from paid orders, highest first.","starter":"select * from orders o\njoin customers c on c.id = o.customer_id;\n","checks":[{"label":"city and revenue from paid orders, highest first","test":"return await same(\"select c.city, sum(o.total) from orders o join customers c on c.id = o.customer_id where o.status = 'paid' group by c.city order by sum(o.total) desc\", true)"}],"hint":"select c.city, sum(o.total) as revenue from orders o join customers c on c.id = o.customer_id where o.status = 'paid' group by c.city order by revenue desc;"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-left-join$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"LEFT JOIN and finding what is missing","kind":"code","task":"Every customer with their paid spending (0 if none).","hint":"from customers c left join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id and o.status = 'paid' group by c.id, c.name, with coalesce(sum(o.total), 0) as spent","body":"## Keep everyone on the list\n\nA teacher takes the register by matching the class list against who is in the room. An inner join is like listing only the children who are present. A **left join** lists **every child on the register**, and leaves a blank next to those who are absent.\n\n```sql\nselect c.name, o.id as order_id\nfrom customers c\nleft join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id;\n```\n\nEvery customer appears. Where there is no matching order, the order columns are **NULL**. Grace and Emeka show up with an empty order_id.\n\nThe table written **first** (on the left) is the one whose rows are all kept.\n\n## Finding what is missing\n\nThe classic use: *which customers have never ordered?* Left join, then keep the rows where the right side is NULL:\n\n```sql\nselect c.name\nfrom customers c\nleft join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id\nwhere o.id is null;\n```\n\nBusinesses run this kind of query all the time: products that never sold, students who have not paid, staff with no timesheet this week.\n\n## A trap: filters on the right table\n\nIf you add `where o.status = 'paid'` to a left join, the customers with no orders disappear again, because their status is NULL, and NULL is not `'paid'`. Put that condition in the **on** instead, so it only decides which orders match:\n\n```sql\nleft join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id and o.status = 'paid'\n```\n\n## COALESCE: fill the blanks\n\n`coalesce(a, b)` gives the first value that is not NULL. `coalesce(sum(o.total), 0)` turns \"no orders\" into a clear 0, and `coalesce(city, 'Unknown')` labels missing cities in a report.\n\n## Counting with left joins\n\n`count(o.id)` counts only matched orders (NULLs are not counted), so customers with no orders get 0, which is right. `count(*)` would wrongly give them 1.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nList **every** customer with the money they have spent on **paid** orders: columns `name` and `spent`, where customers with no paid orders show `0`. Sort by `spent` highest first, then by `name`.","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":6}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-left-join$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-left-join","title":"LEFT JOIN and finding what is missing","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"Keep every row with LEFT JOIN, find customers with no orders, and replace NULLs with COALESCE.","body":"## Keep everyone on the list\n\nA teacher takes the register by matching the class list against who is in the room. An inner join is like listing only the children who are present. A **left join** lists **every child on the register**, and leaves a blank next to those who are absent.\n\n```sql\nselect c.name, o.id as order_id\nfrom customers c\nleft join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id;\n```\n\nEvery customer appears. Where there is no matching order, the order columns are **NULL**. Grace and Emeka show up with an empty order_id.\n\nThe table written **first** (on the left) is the one whose rows are all kept.\n\n## Finding what is missing\n\nThe classic use: *which customers have never ordered?* Left join, then keep the rows where the right side is NULL:\n\n```sql\nselect c.name\nfrom customers c\nleft join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id\nwhere o.id is null;\n```\n\nBusinesses run this kind of query all the time: products that never sold, students who have not paid, staff with no timesheet this week.\n\n## A trap: filters on the right table\n\nIf you add `where o.status = 'paid'` to a left join, the customers with no orders disappear again, because their status is NULL, and NULL is not `'paid'`. Put that condition in the **on** instead, so it only decides which orders match:\n\n```sql\nleft join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id and o.status = 'paid'\n```\n\n## COALESCE: fill the blanks\n\n`coalesce(a, b)` gives the first value that is not NULL. `coalesce(sum(o.total), 0)` turns \"no orders\" into a clear 0, and `coalesce(city, 'Unknown')` labels missing cities in a report.\n\n## Counting with left joins\n\n`count(o.id)` counts only matched orders (NULLs are not counted), so customers with no orders get 0, which is right. `count(*)` would wrongly give them 1.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nList **every** customer with the money they have spent on **paid** orders: columns `name` and `spent`, where customers with no paid orders show `0`. Sort by `spent` highest first, then by `name`.","task":"Every customer with their paid spending (0 if none).","starter":"select c.name, o.total\nfrom customers c\njoin orders o on o.customer_id = c.id;\n","checks":[{"label":"all 7 customers","test":"return (await result()).length === 7"},{"label":"name and paid spending, 0 for none, sorted","test":"return await same(\"select c.name, coalesce(sum(o.total), 0) from customers c left join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id and o.status = 'paid' group by c.id, c.name order by 2 desc, c.name\", true)"}],"hint":"from customers c left join orders o on o.customer_id = c.id and o.status = 'paid' group by c.id, c.name, with coalesce(sum(o.total), 0) as spent"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-case-dates$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"CASE, dates and monthly reports","kind":"code","task":"One row per month: month, paid, not_paid, orders.","hint":"select strftime('%Y-%m', order_date) as month, sum(case when status = 'paid' then total else 0 end) as paid, … group by month order by month","body":"## CASE: if / else inside a query\n\n`case` lets a query make decisions, like a teacher turning scores into grades:\n\n```sql\nselect name, price,\n  case\n    when price >= 15000 then 'premium'\n    when price >= 2000 then 'standard'\n    else 'budget'\n  end as band\nfrom products;\n```\n\nThe first true `when` wins, just like `if` / `elif` in Python. Without an `else`, unmatched rows get NULL.\n\n## Counting with CASE\n\nA powerful trick: put `case` inside `sum` to count or add only some rows, in the same query:\n\n```sql\nselect\n  sum(case when status = 'paid' then total else 0 end) as paid,\n  sum(case when status = 'pending' then total else 0 end) as pending\nfrom orders;\n```\n\nThis turns rows into columns, which is what a spreadsheet **pivot table** does.\n\n## Dates\n\nEvery database has date functions. Their names differ, but the ideas are the same:\n\n| Task | SQLite (this course) | PostgreSQL / Supabase |\n|---|---|---|\n| Year and month | `strftime('%Y-%m', order_date)` | `to_char(order_date, 'YYYY-MM')` |\n| Today | `date('now')` | `current_date` |\n| 30 days ago | `date('now', '-30 days')` | `current_date - 30` |\n| Days between | `julianday(b) - julianday(a)` | `b - a` |\n\nBecause our dates are stored as `YYYY-MM-DD` text, `substr(order_date, 1, 7)` also gives the month, like `2026-10`.\n\n## Monthly reports\n\nGroup by the month to see the trend:\n\n```sql\nselect strftime('%Y-%m', order_date) as month, sum(total) as revenue\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid'\ngroup by month\norder by month;\n```\n\nA month-by-month table like this is the first thing most business owners ask for, and you can now build it.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nMake a monthly report over **all** orders with these columns, one row per month, oldest month first:\n\n1. `month` (like `2026-09`)\n2. `paid`: the total of paid orders in that month\n3. `not_paid`: the total of orders that are pending or cancelled in that month\n4. `orders`: the number of orders in that month","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":7}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-case-dates$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-case-dates","title":"CASE, dates and monthly reports","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"Label rows with CASE WHEN, group by month with date functions, and build a pivot-style report.","body":"## CASE: if / else inside a query\n\n`case` lets a query make decisions, like a teacher turning scores into grades:\n\n```sql\nselect name, price,\n  case\n    when price >= 15000 then 'premium'\n    when price >= 2000 then 'standard'\n    else 'budget'\n  end as band\nfrom products;\n```\n\nThe first true `when` wins, just like `if` / `elif` in Python. Without an `else`, unmatched rows get NULL.\n\n## Counting with CASE\n\nA powerful trick: put `case` inside `sum` to count or add only some rows, in the same query:\n\n```sql\nselect\n  sum(case when status = 'paid' then total else 0 end) as paid,\n  sum(case when status = 'pending' then total else 0 end) as pending\nfrom orders;\n```\n\nThis turns rows into columns, which is what a spreadsheet **pivot table** does.\n\n## Dates\n\nEvery database has date functions. Their names differ, but the ideas are the same:\n\n| Task | SQLite (this course) | PostgreSQL / Supabase |\n|---|---|---|\n| Year and month | `strftime('%Y-%m', order_date)` | `to_char(order_date, 'YYYY-MM')` |\n| Today | `date('now')` | `current_date` |\n| 30 days ago | `date('now', '-30 days')` | `current_date - 30` |\n| Days between | `julianday(b) - julianday(a)` | `b - a` |\n\nBecause our dates are stored as `YYYY-MM-DD` text, `substr(order_date, 1, 7)` also gives the month, like `2026-10`.\n\n## Monthly reports\n\nGroup by the month to see the trend:\n\n```sql\nselect strftime('%Y-%m', order_date) as month, sum(total) as revenue\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid'\ngroup by month\norder by month;\n```\n\nA month-by-month table like this is the first thing most business owners ask for, and you can now build it.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nMake a monthly report over **all** orders with these columns, one row per month, oldest month first:\n\n1. `month` (like `2026-09`)\n2. `paid`: the total of paid orders in that month\n3. `not_paid`: the total of orders that are pending or cancelled in that month\n4. `orders`: the number of orders in that month","task":"One row per month: month, paid, not_paid, orders.","starter":"select order_date, status, total from orders;\n","checks":[{"label":"month, paid, not_paid and orders, oldest first","test":"return await same(\"select strftime('%Y-%m', order_date), sum(case when status = 'paid' then total else 0 end), sum(case when status <> 'paid' then total else 0 end), count(*) from orders group by 1 order by 1\", true)"}],"hint":"select strftime('%Y-%m', order_date) as month, sum(case when status = 'paid' then total else 0 end) as paid, … group by month order by month"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-subqueries$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Subqueries and CTEs","kind":"code","task":"Customers who spent more than the average paying customer.","hint":"Add where status = 'paid' inside the CTE, then join customers and keep where s.spent > (select avg(spent) from spending).","body":"## A question inside a question\n\n*\"Which products cost more than the average product?\"* To answer, you first need the average, then compare. In SQL, you can put one query **inside** another:\n\n```sql\nselect name, price\nfrom products\nwhere price > (select avg(price) from products);\n```\n\nThe inner query runs first and gives one number; the outer query uses it. It is like asking a friend for the class average before deciding who scored above it.\n\n## Subqueries that return a list\n\n```sql\nselect name from customers\nwhere id in (select customer_id from orders where status = 'pending');\n```\n\nThe inner query returns a list of ids; `in` checks each customer against it.\n\n## WITH: name your steps (CTEs)\n\nLong nested queries get hard to read, like a sentence with five brackets. A **common table expression** (CTE) lets you name each step, then use it like a table:\n\n```sql\nwith spending as (\n  select customer_id, sum(total) as spent\n  from orders\n  where status = 'paid'\n  group by customer_id\n)\nselect c.name, s.spent\nfrom spending s\njoin customers c on c.id = s.customer_id\nwhere s.spent > (select avg(spent) from spending);\n```\n\nRead it top to bottom: *first* work out each customer's spending, *then* keep those above the average spending. You can chain several steps: `with a as (…), b as (… from a …) select … from b`.\n\nCTEs are how analysts write long reports that colleagues can still check. Prefer them whenever a query does more than one thing.\n\n## EXISTS\n\n`where exists (select 1 from orders o where o.customer_id = c.id)` keeps customers who have at least one order. It stops looking as soon as it finds one, which is efficient on large tables.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nUsing a CTE, show the customers whose spending on **paid** orders is **above the average** of all paying customers' spending. Columns: `name` and `spent`, highest first.","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":8}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-subqueries$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-subqueries","title":"Subqueries and CTEs","minutes":30,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"Queries inside queries, IN with a subquery, and WITH (common table expressions) for readable step-by-step SQL.","body":"## A question inside a question\n\n*\"Which products cost more than the average product?\"* To answer, you first need the average, then compare. In SQL, you can put one query **inside** another:\n\n```sql\nselect name, price\nfrom products\nwhere price > (select avg(price) from products);\n```\n\nThe inner query runs first and gives one number; the outer query uses it. It is like asking a friend for the class average before deciding who scored above it.\n\n## Subqueries that return a list\n\n```sql\nselect name from customers\nwhere id in (select customer_id from orders where status = 'pending');\n```\n\nThe inner query returns a list of ids; `in` checks each customer against it.\n\n## WITH: name your steps (CTEs)\n\nLong nested queries get hard to read, like a sentence with five brackets. A **common table expression** (CTE) lets you name each step, then use it like a table:\n\n```sql\nwith spending as (\n  select customer_id, sum(total) as spent\n  from orders\n  where status = 'paid'\n  group by customer_id\n)\nselect c.name, s.spent\nfrom spending s\njoin customers c on c.id = s.customer_id\nwhere s.spent > (select avg(spent) from spending);\n```\n\nRead it top to bottom: *first* work out each customer's spending, *then* keep those above the average spending. You can chain several steps: `with a as (…), b as (… from a …) select … from b`.\n\nCTEs are how analysts write long reports that colleagues can still check. Prefer them whenever a query does more than one thing.\n\n## EXISTS\n\n`where exists (select 1 from orders o where o.customer_id = c.id)` keeps customers who have at least one order. It stops looking as soon as it finds one, which is efficient on large tables.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nUsing a CTE, show the customers whose spending on **paid** orders is **above the average** of all paying customers' spending. Columns: `name` and `spent`, highest first.","task":"Customers who spent more than the average paying customer.","starter":"with spending as (\n  select customer_id, sum(total) as spent\n  from orders\n  group by customer_id\n)\nselect * from spending;\n","checks":[{"label":"name and spent above the average, highest first","test":"return await same(\"with s as (select customer_id, sum(total) as spent from orders where status = 'paid' group by customer_id) select c.name, s.spent from s join customers c on c.id = s.customer_id where s.spent > (select avg(spent) from s) order by s.spent desc\", true)"}],"hint":"Add where status = 'paid' inside the CTE, then join customers and keep where s.spent > (select avg(spent) from spending)."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-change$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Creating and changing data","kind":"code","task":"Create suppliers, add 3, raise Electronics prices 10%, delete cancelled orders.","hint":"update products set price = round(price * 1.1) where category = 'Electronics'; delete from orders where status = 'cancelled';","body":"## From reading to writing\n\nSo far you have only read data. Now you will change it. These commands are powerful: an `update` without a `where` changes **every** row. Professionals treat them like a cheque book: check twice, sign once.\n\n## CREATE TABLE\n\n```sql\ncreate table suppliers (\n  id integer primary key,\n  name text not null unique,\n  phone text,\n  rating integer check (rating between 1 and 5)\n);\n```\n\nThe rules after each column are **constraints**: the database refuses data that breaks them.\n\n- `primary key`: unique id for each row\n- `not null`: a value is required\n- `unique`: no two rows may share it\n- `check (…)`: any rule you like\n- `references other(id)`: a foreign key: the value must exist in the other table\n\nConstraints are the database's security guard at the gate. Code in an app can have bugs; the guard still stops bad data.\n\n## INSERT\n\n```sql\ninsert into suppliers (id, name, phone, rating) values (1, 'Abuja Grains Ltd', '0803 000 0000', 5);\ninsert into suppliers (id, name, rating) values (2, 'Kano Oils', 4), (3, 'Lagos Tech', 3);\n```\n\nAlways list the columns: your insert keeps working if someone adds a column later.\n\n## UPDATE\n\n```sql\nupdate products set price = 1300 where id = 3;\nupdate products set price = round(price * 1.1) where category = 'Food';   -- a 10% rise for food\n```\n\n**Run the `where` as a `select` first** to see which rows will change. That one habit prevents most disasters.\n\n## DELETE\n\n```sql\ndelete from orders where status = 'cancelled';\n```\n\nMany real systems never delete business records. They mark them (`status = 'archived'`) so history and audits stay intact.\n\n## Transactions: all or nothing\n\nMoving money from one account to another is two updates. If the power goes off between them, money vanishes. A **transaction** groups statements so that either all happen or none do:\n\n```sql\nbegin;\nupdate accounts set balance = balance - 5000 where id = 1;\nupdate accounts set balance = balance + 5000 where id = 2;\ncommit;     -- or rollback; to undo everything since begin\n```\n\n## Your task\n\n1. Create a table `suppliers` with `id` (integer primary key), `name` (text, not null, unique) and `rating` (integer that must be between 1 and 5).\n2. Insert 3 suppliers.\n3. Raise the price of every **Electronics** product by **10%** (round to a whole number).\n4. Delete the **cancelled** orders.\n5. Finish with `select * from suppliers;`","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":9}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-change$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-change","title":"Creating and changing data","minutes":35,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"CREATE TABLE with constraints, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, and transactions to keep data safe.","body":"## From reading to writing\n\nSo far you have only read data. Now you will change it. These commands are powerful: an `update` without a `where` changes **every** row. Professionals treat them like a cheque book: check twice, sign once.\n\n## CREATE TABLE\n\n```sql\ncreate table suppliers (\n  id integer primary key,\n  name text not null unique,\n  phone text,\n  rating integer check (rating between 1 and 5)\n);\n```\n\nThe rules after each column are **constraints**: the database refuses data that breaks them.\n\n- `primary key`: unique id for each row\n- `not null`: a value is required\n- `unique`: no two rows may share it\n- `check (…)`: any rule you like\n- `references other(id)`: a foreign key: the value must exist in the other table\n\nConstraints are the database's security guard at the gate. Code in an app can have bugs; the guard still stops bad data.\n\n## INSERT\n\n```sql\ninsert into suppliers (id, name, phone, rating) values (1, 'Abuja Grains Ltd', '0803 000 0000', 5);\ninsert into suppliers (id, name, rating) values (2, 'Kano Oils', 4), (3, 'Lagos Tech', 3);\n```\n\nAlways list the columns: your insert keeps working if someone adds a column later.\n\n## UPDATE\n\n```sql\nupdate products set price = 1300 where id = 3;\nupdate products set price = round(price * 1.1) where category = 'Food';   -- a 10% rise for food\n```\n\n**Run the `where` as a `select` first** to see which rows will change. That one habit prevents most disasters.\n\n## DELETE\n\n```sql\ndelete from orders where status = 'cancelled';\n```\n\nMany real systems never delete business records. They mark them (`status = 'archived'`) so history and audits stay intact.\n\n## Transactions: all or nothing\n\nMoving money from one account to another is two updates. If the power goes off between them, money vanishes. A **transaction** groups statements so that either all happen or none do:\n\n```sql\nbegin;\nupdate accounts set balance = balance - 5000 where id = 1;\nupdate accounts set balance = balance + 5000 where id = 2;\ncommit;     -- or rollback; to undo everything since begin\n```\n\n## Your task\n\n1. Create a table `suppliers` with `id` (integer primary key), `name` (text, not null, unique) and `rating` (integer that must be between 1 and 5).\n2. Insert 3 suppliers.\n3. Raise the price of every **Electronics** product by **10%** (round to a whole number).\n4. Delete the **cancelled** orders.\n5. Finish with `select * from suppliers;`","task":"Create suppliers, add 3, raise Electronics prices 10%, delete cancelled orders.","starter":"-- 1. create table suppliers (...)\n\n-- 2. insert into suppliers ...\n\n-- 3. update products ...\n\n-- 4. delete from orders ...\n\nselect * from suppliers;\n","checks":[{"label":"suppliers table has 3 rows","test":"return await yes(\"select count(*) = 3 from suppliers\")"},{"label":"rating has a check rule","test":"return await yes(\"select sql like '%check%' from sqlite_master where name = 'suppliers'\")"},{"label":"Electronics prices up 10%","test":"return await yes(\"select sum(price) = 2750 + 19800 + 10450 from products where category = 'Electronics'\")"},{"label":"other prices unchanged","test":"return await yes(\"select sum(price) = 72000 + 15500 + 1200 + 800 + 1500 from products where category <> 'Electronics'\")"},{"label":"cancelled orders deleted, others kept","test":"return await yes(\"select count(*) = 9 and sum(status = 'cancelled') = 0 from orders\")"}],"hint":"update products set price = round(price * 1.1) where category = 'Electronics'; delete from orders where status = 'cancelled';"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-design$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Designing a good database","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Design decides everything later\n\nA badly planned building can be painted beautifully, but the doors will still be in the wrong places. A badly designed database makes every future query harder and every report less trustworthy. Ten minutes of design saves months of cleaning.\n\n## Start from the nouns\n\nList the **things** the business deals with: customers, products, orders, payments, branches, staff. Each thing usually becomes a table. Then list the **facts** about each one: those become columns.\n\n## Normalisation: one fact, one place\n\nThe main rule: **store each fact once**. If the customer's phone number is written on every order, it will eventually disagree with itself. Common signs of trouble:\n\n- Repeating columns: `product1`, `product2`, `product3`. Use a separate table with one row per item (like `order_items`).\n- Several values in one cell: `\"Rice, Oil, Bread\"`. Split into rows.\n- The same text typed in many rows: `\"Electronics\"`, `\"electronics\"`, `\"Electronic\"`. Use a lookup table or a check constraint.\n\n## Relationships\n\n- **One-to-many**: one customer has many orders. The \"many\" side holds the foreign key (`orders.customer_id`).\n- **Many-to-many**: an order has many products, and a product is in many orders. Use a **join table** in the middle (`order_items`), with a foreign key to each side.\n- **One-to-one**: rare; usually extra details split into another table.\n\n## Choosing keys and types\n\n- Use a meaningless id (a number or a UUID) as the primary key, not a name or phone number, because those change.\n- Store money as whole units (kobo, cents) or a `numeric` type, never as text, and avoid floating-point types for money.\n- Store dates as date types, not text like `\"5th of Nov\"`.\n\n## Indexes: the book's index\n\nFinding a word in a book without an index means reading every page. A database **index** on a column lets it jump straight to matching rows: `create index orders_customer_idx on orders (customer_id);`. Index the columns you search and join on often. Indexes speed up reading but slightly slow down writing, so do not index everything.\n\n## SQL injection: the most famous security hole\n\nIf an app glues user input straight into SQL text, an attacker can type SQL instead of a name:\n\n```\nname typed:  ' or '1'='1\nquery built: select * from users where name = '' or '1'='1'\n```\n\nThat returns every user. The fix is simple and non-negotiable: **parameterised queries**, where the database receives the query and the values separately and never treats a value as code. Every modern library supports them. Never build SQL by joining strings with user input.\n\n## Who may see what\n\nReal databases give each user only the access they need. In PostgreSQL and Supabase (which CDM Studio uses), **row level security** rules like *\"a user can only read their own orders\"* live in the database itself, so even a buggy app cannot leak other people's data.\n\n## Which database?\n\n**PostgreSQL** (free, powerful, used by Supabase), **MySQL / MariaDB** (common on web hosting), **SQL Server** (many corporates and banks), **SQLite** (inside phones and apps; this course), **Oracle** (large enterprises). The SQL you learned works on all of them with small differences.","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":10}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-design$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-design","title":"Designing a good database","minutes":30,"kind":"read","outline":"Normalisation, choosing keys, relationships, indexes, SQL injection and access rules in real systems.","body":"## Design decides everything later\n\nA badly planned building can be painted beautifully, but the doors will still be in the wrong places. A badly designed database makes every future query harder and every report less trustworthy. Ten minutes of design saves months of cleaning.\n\n## Start from the nouns\n\nList the **things** the business deals with: customers, products, orders, payments, branches, staff. Each thing usually becomes a table. Then list the **facts** about each one: those become columns.\n\n## Normalisation: one fact, one place\n\nThe main rule: **store each fact once**. If the customer's phone number is written on every order, it will eventually disagree with itself. Common signs of trouble:\n\n- Repeating columns: `product1`, `product2`, `product3`. Use a separate table with one row per item (like `order_items`).\n- Several values in one cell: `\"Rice, Oil, Bread\"`. Split into rows.\n- The same text typed in many rows: `\"Electronics\"`, `\"electronics\"`, `\"Electronic\"`. Use a lookup table or a check constraint.\n\n## Relationships\n\n- **One-to-many**: one customer has many orders. The \"many\" side holds the foreign key (`orders.customer_id`).\n- **Many-to-many**: an order has many products, and a product is in many orders. Use a **join table** in the middle (`order_items`), with a foreign key to each side.\n- **One-to-one**: rare; usually extra details split into another table.\n\n## Choosing keys and types\n\n- Use a meaningless id (a number or a UUID) as the primary key, not a name or phone number, because those change.\n- Store money as whole units (kobo, cents) or a `numeric` type, never as text, and avoid floating-point types for money.\n- Store dates as date types, not text like `\"5th of Nov\"`.\n\n## Indexes: the book's index\n\nFinding a word in a book without an index means reading every page. A database **index** on a column lets it jump straight to matching rows: `create index orders_customer_idx on orders (customer_id);`. Index the columns you search and join on often. Indexes speed up reading but slightly slow down writing, so do not index everything.\n\n## SQL injection: the most famous security hole\n\nIf an app glues user input straight into SQL text, an attacker can type SQL instead of a name:\n\n```\nname typed:  ' or '1'='1\nquery built: select * from users where name = '' or '1'='1'\n```\n\nThat returns every user. The fix is simple and non-negotiable: **parameterised queries**, where the database receives the query and the values separately and never treats a value as code. Every modern library supports them. Never build SQL by joining strings with user input.\n\n## Who may see what\n\nReal databases give each user only the access they need. In PostgreSQL and Supabase (which CDM Studio uses), **row level security** rules like *\"a user can only read their own orders\"* live in the database itself, so even a buggy app cannot leak other people's data.\n\n## Which database?\n\n**PostgreSQL** (free, powerful, used by Supabase), **MySQL / MariaDB** (common on web hosting), **SQL Server** (many corporates and banks), **SQLite** (inside phones and apps; this course), **Oracle** (large enterprises). The SQL you learned works on all of them with small differences.","quiz":[{"q":"An orders table has columns product1, product2, product3. The better design is…","options":["Add product4 and product5","A separate order_items table with one row per product","Put all products in one text cell"],"answer":1,"why":"Repeating columns become rows in a child table."},{"q":"Which is the best primary key for customers?","options":["Their phone number","Their full name","A meaningless id that never changes"],"answer":2,"why":"Names and phone numbers change and can repeat."},{"q":"The fix for SQL injection is…","options":["Parameterised queries","Hiding error messages","Using capital letters in SQL"],"answer":0,"why":"Values must never be treated as SQL code."},{"q":"An index helps most on…","options":["Columns you search and join on often","Every column","Columns you never read"],"answer":0,"why":"Indexes speed up lookups but cost a little on writes."}]}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$sql-window$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Window functions and where SQL takes you","kind":"code","task":"Paid orders with a running total, oldest first.","hint":"Add: sum(total) over (order by order_date, id) as running_total","body":"## Looking through a window\n\n`group by` squeezes rows into one per group. Sometimes you want to **keep every row** but still see something about its group, like each student's score *and* their position in the class. **Window functions** do that: each row looks through a \"window\" at the rows around it.\n\n```sql\nselect name, category, price,\n       rank() over (partition by category order by price desc) as rank_in_category\nfrom products;\n```\n\n- `over ( … )` makes it a window function.\n- `partition by category` makes a separate window per category (like separate classes).\n- `order by price desc` decides the order inside each window.\n\n## Useful window functions\n\n| Function | Gives |\n|---|---|\n| `row_number()` | 1, 2, 3 … with no ties |\n| `rank()` | positions, with ties sharing a rank |\n| `sum(x) over (order by …)` | a **running total** |\n| `lag(x)` / `lead(x)` | the value from the previous / next row |\n| `avg(x) over (partition by …)` | the group average next to each row |\n\n## Running totals\n\n```sql\nselect order_date, total,\n       sum(total) over (order by order_date, id) as running_total\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid';\n```\n\nThis is how a bank statement shows the balance after every transaction.\n\n## Comparing with the previous row\n\n`total - lag(total) over (order by order_date)` shows the change since the previous order: growth month on month, or the gap between a customer's visits.\n\n## Top N per group\n\n\"The best-selling product in each category\" is a classic interview question. Number the rows per group in a CTE, then keep number 1:\n\n```sql\nwith ranked as (\n  select name, category, price, row_number() over (partition by category order by price desc) as n\n  from products\n)\nselect * from ranked where n = 1;\n```\n\n## Where SQL takes you\n\n- **Data analyst / BI analyst**: SQL plus a dashboard tool (Power BI, Looker Studio, Metabase). Pair this course with **Data analytics for business**.\n- **Back-end developer**: SQL plus Python or JavaScript. Supabase and PostgreSQL are great places to practise; CDM Studio itself runs on them.\n- **Practice**: SQLBolt, Mode's SQL tutorial, and the free datasets on Kaggle. In interviews, explain your thinking out loud and test edge cases like NULLs and ties.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nFor **paid** orders, show `id`, `order_date`, `total` and `running_total` (the total of paid orders so far, in date order, using `id` to break ties), oldest first.","practice":null,"track":"sql","track_title":"SQL and databases","paid":true,"order":11}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$sql-window$cdm$, $cdm$sql$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"sql-window","title":"Window functions and where SQL takes you","minutes":35,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table customers (id integer primary key, name text not null, city text, joined text not null);\ninsert into customers values\n (1, 'Ada Obi', 'Abuja', '2026-01-10'), (2, 'Musa Bello', 'Kano', '2026-02-03'), (3, 'Chika Eze', 'Lagos', '2026-02-20'),\n (4, 'Tunde Ade', 'Lagos', '2026-03-15'), (5, 'Fatima Sani', 'Abuja', '2026-04-01'), (6, 'Grace Okon', 'Port Harcourt', '2026-05-12'),\n (7, 'Emeka Nwosu', null, '2026-06-30');\ncreate table products (id integer primary key, name text not null, category text not null, price integer not null);\ninsert into products values\n (1, 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 72000), (2, 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 15500), (3, 'Bread', 'Food', 1200),\n (4, 'Phone case', 'Electronics', 2500), (5, 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 18000), (6, 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 9500),\n (7, 'Notebook', 'Stationery', 800), (8, 'Pen pack', 'Stationery', 1500);\ncreate table orders (id integer primary key, customer_id integer not null references customers(id), order_date text not null, status text not null, total integer not null);\ninsert into orders values\n (1, 1, '2026-09-02', 'paid', 72000), (2, 2, '2026-09-05', 'paid', 27500), (3, 1, '2026-09-18', 'paid', 6000),\n (4, 3, '2026-10-01', 'cancelled', 5000), (5, 4, '2026-10-07', 'paid', 31000), (6, 3, '2026-10-15', 'paid', 11000),\n (7, 5, '2026-10-28', 'paid', 19000), (8, 2, '2026-11-03', 'pending', 87500), (9, 1, '2026-11-10', 'paid', 18000),\n (10, 4, '2026-11-12', 'paid', 12000);\ncreate table order_items (order_id integer not null references orders(id), product_id integer not null references products(id), qty integer not null);\ninsert into order_items values\n (1, 1, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (3, 3, 5), (4, 4, 2), (5, 2, 2), (6, 7, 10), (6, 8, 2), (7, 6, 2), (8, 1, 1), (8, 2, 1), (9, 5, 1), (10, 3, 10);\n","outline":"Rank rows, running totals and comparisons with the previous row using OVER (...), plus SQL careers and next steps.","body":"## Looking through a window\n\n`group by` squeezes rows into one per group. Sometimes you want to **keep every row** but still see something about its group, like each student's score *and* their position in the class. **Window functions** do that: each row looks through a \"window\" at the rows around it.\n\n```sql\nselect name, category, price,\n       rank() over (partition by category order by price desc) as rank_in_category\nfrom products;\n```\n\n- `over ( … )` makes it a window function.\n- `partition by category` makes a separate window per category (like separate classes).\n- `order by price desc` decides the order inside each window.\n\n## Useful window functions\n\n| Function | Gives |\n|---|---|\n| `row_number()` | 1, 2, 3 … with no ties |\n| `rank()` | positions, with ties sharing a rank |\n| `sum(x) over (order by …)` | a **running total** |\n| `lag(x)` / `lead(x)` | the value from the previous / next row |\n| `avg(x) over (partition by …)` | the group average next to each row |\n\n## Running totals\n\n```sql\nselect order_date, total,\n       sum(total) over (order by order_date, id) as running_total\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid';\n```\n\nThis is how a bank statement shows the balance after every transaction.\n\n## Comparing with the previous row\n\n`total - lag(total) over (order by order_date)` shows the change since the previous order: growth month on month, or the gap between a customer's visits.\n\n## Top N per group\n\n\"The best-selling product in each category\" is a classic interview question. Number the rows per group in a CTE, then keep number 1:\n\n```sql\nwith ranked as (\n  select name, category, price, row_number() over (partition by category order by price desc) as n\n  from products\n)\nselect * from ranked where n = 1;\n```\n\n## Where SQL takes you\n\n- **Data analyst / BI analyst**: SQL plus a dashboard tool (Power BI, Looker Studio, Metabase). Pair this course with **Data analytics for business**.\n- **Back-end developer**: SQL plus Python or JavaScript. Supabase and PostgreSQL are great places to practise; CDM Studio itself runs on them.\n- **Practice**: SQLBolt, Mode's SQL tutorial, and the free datasets on Kaggle. In interviews, explain your thinking out loud and test edge cases like NULLs and ties.\n\n## The shop database\n\nEvery lesson in this course uses the same small database of an online shop. It has four tables:\n\n| Table | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| `customers` | id, name, city, joined |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price |\n| `orders` | id, customer_id, order_date, status (paid, pending or cancelled), total |\n| `order_items` | order_id, product_id, qty |\n\nYou can always look around with `select * from products;` in the editor. Only the result of your **last** statement is checked.\n\n## Your task\n\nFor **paid** orders, show `id`, `order_date`, `total` and `running_total` (the total of paid orders so far, in date order, using `id` to break ties), oldest first.","task":"Paid orders with a running total, oldest first.","starter":"select id, order_date, total\nfrom orders\nwhere status = 'paid'\norder by order_date;\n","checks":[{"label":"id, order_date, total and running_total in date order","test":"return await same(\"select id, order_date, total, sum(total) over (order by order_date, id) from orders where status = 'paid' order by order_date, id\", true)"}],"hint":"Add: sum(total) over (order by order_date, id) as running_total"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
