insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-how-llms-work$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"How large language models really work","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Prediction, one token at a time\n\nA large language model (LLM) such as Claude reads text as **tokens** (pieces of words: \"unbelievable\" might be \"un\", \"believ\", \"able\") and predicts the most likely next token, again and again. Everything else, from answering questions to writing code, comes from doing that prediction extremely well after training on a huge amount of text.\n\n## The context window\n\nThe model only \"knows\" two things when it answers you:\n\n1. What it learned in training (frozen at a **cut-off date**)\n2. What is in the **context window**: the system instructions, the conversation so far, and any documents you paste\n\nThe window is large but limited (measured in tokens). Long chats push old messages out or make answers slower and more expensive. Good AI engineers decide **exactly what goes into the context**.\n\n## Cost\n\nAPIs charge per token, separately for **input** (what you send) and **output** (what the model writes). Output usually costs several times more per token. A rough rule for English: 1 token ≈ ¾ of a word, so 1,000 words ≈ 1,300 tokens.\n\n## Temperature\n\nTemperature controls randomness. Low (0 to 0.3): consistent, factual, best for extraction and classification. Higher (0.7 to 1): more varied, better for brainstorming.\n\n## Why models \"hallucinate\"\n\nThe model's goal is a **likely-sounding** continuation, not a checked fact. When it does not know, it can produce something confident and wrong: a fake citation, a wrong price, an invented law. You reduce this by:\n\n- Giving it the facts in the context (your price list, your policy)\n- Telling it to say \"I don't know\" when the context does not contain the answer\n- Asking for quotes or sources from the provided text\n- Checking important outputs with code or a human\n\n## System prompt vs user prompt\n\nApps send a **system prompt** (the rules and role, set by the developer) plus the **user's message**. Users should not be able to override the system prompt; designing it well is a core AI engineering skill.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":false,"order":0}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-templates$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Prompt templates that work every time","kind":"code","task":"Write fill(template, values) and variables(template).","hint":"const RE = /\\{\\{\\s*([a-zA-Z_][\\w]*)\\s*\\}\\}/g. variables: [...new Set([...template.matchAll(RE)].map(m => m[1]))]. fill: check every variable is in values first, then template.replace(RE, (_, n) => String(values[n])).","body":"## From one good prompt to a reusable tool\n\nA business does not need one clever prompt; it needs the **same quality every time**, for every customer, product or report. You get that with a **template**: fixed instructions plus variables.\n\n```text\nYou are a polite customer service assistant for {{business}}.\nReply to this customer message in {{language}}, under {{words}} words.\nOnly use facts from the policy below. If the answer is not in it, say you will check and reply.\n\nPolicy:\n{{policy}}\n\nCustomer message:\n{{message}}\n```\n\n## The parts of a strong template\n\n1. **Role and audience**: who the AI is, who it writes for.\n2. **Task**: one clear job.\n3. **Context**: the facts it may use (policy, price list, data).\n4. **Rules**: length, tone, language, what to avoid, what to do when unsure.\n5. **Output format**: bullets, a table, JSON (next lesson).\n6. **Examples** (optional): one or two input → output pairs (\"few-shot\") teach style better than descriptions.\n\nPutting long context in clear blocks (or XML-style tags such as `<policy>…</policy>`) helps the model tell instructions apart from data. That also defends against **prompt injection**, where text inside the data says *\"ignore your instructions\"*.\n\n## Your task: a template filler\n\nWrite `fill(template, values)`:\n\n- Replace every `{{name}}` with `values[name]` (spaces inside the braces are allowed: `{{ name }}`).\n- If a value is missing, **throw an Error** whose message includes the missing name, so a bad prompt is never sent.\n- Values are inserted as plain text (convert numbers with `String`).\n\nAlso write `variables(template)` that returns the list of variable names, each once, in order of first appearance.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":false,"order":1}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-json$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Structured output: getting JSON you can trust","kind":"code","task":"Write parseOrder(text) that extracts, parses and validates the JSON.","hint":"const a = text.indexOf('{'), b = text.lastIndexOf('}'); if (a < 0 || b < a) return null; let o; try { o = JSON.parse(text.slice(a, b + 1)) } catch { return null }. Use Number.isInteger(i.qty).","body":"## Why structure matters\n\nWhen AI output feeds another program (a spreadsheet, a database, an email sender), free text is fragile. Ask for **JSON** with exact fields:\n\n```text\nExtract the order from the message. Answer with JSON only:\n{\"customer\": string, \"items\": [{\"name\": string, \"qty\": number}], \"deliver\": \"pickup\" | \"delivery\"}\n```\n\nModern APIs also offer **tool use / structured outputs**, where you give a JSON schema and the model must fill it. CDM Studio's builder and data analyst work this way.\n\n## Never trust it blindly\n\nEven with good instructions, a model can:\n\n- Wrap the JSON in ```json fences or add a sentence before it\n- Miss a field or use the wrong type (\"qty\": \"two\")\n- Add values you did not allow\n\nSo your code must **parse defensively** and **validate**, and when it fails, retry or ask a human. Treat AI output like any input from the outside world.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite `parseOrder(text)`:\n\n1. Take the JSON out of the text: from the first `{` to the last `}`. If there is none, return `null`.\n2. `JSON.parse` it inside try/catch; on error return `null`.\n3. Validate: `customer` is a non-empty string; `items` is a non-empty array where every item has a non-empty string `name` and a whole number `qty` from 1 to 1000; `deliver` is `'pickup'` or `'delivery'`. If anything is wrong, return `null`.\n4. Return a clean object with **only** those three fields (drop anything extra), names trimmed.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":2}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-json$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-json","title":"Structured output: getting JSON you can trust","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Ask for JSON, then parse and validate it safely so bad AI answers never break your app.","body":"## Why structure matters\n\nWhen AI output feeds another program (a spreadsheet, a database, an email sender), free text is fragile. Ask for **JSON** with exact fields:\n\n```text\nExtract the order from the message. Answer with JSON only:\n{\"customer\": string, \"items\": [{\"name\": string, \"qty\": number}], \"deliver\": \"pickup\" | \"delivery\"}\n```\n\nModern APIs also offer **tool use / structured outputs**, where you give a JSON schema and the model must fill it. CDM Studio's builder and data analyst work this way.\n\n## Never trust it blindly\n\nEven with good instructions, a model can:\n\n- Wrap the JSON in ```json fences or add a sentence before it\n- Miss a field or use the wrong type (\"qty\": \"two\")\n- Add values you did not allow\n\nSo your code must **parse defensively** and **validate**, and when it fails, retry or ask a human. Treat AI output like any input from the outside world.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite `parseOrder(text)`:\n\n1. Take the JSON out of the text: from the first `{` to the last `}`. If there is none, return `null`.\n2. `JSON.parse` it inside try/catch; on error return `null`.\n3. Validate: `customer` is a non-empty string; `items` is a non-empty array where every item has a non-empty string `name` and a whole number `qty` from 1 to 1000; `deliver` is `'pickup'` or `'delivery'`. If anything is wrong, return `null`.\n4. Return a clean object with **only** those three fields (drop anything extra), names trimmed.","task":"Write parseOrder(text) that extracts, parses and validates the JSON.","starter":"<script>\n  function parseOrder(text) {\n    // your code\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"plain JSON works","test":"const o = parseOrder('{\"customer\":\"Ada\",\"items\":[{\"name\":\"Bread\",\"qty\":2}],\"deliver\":\"pickup\"}'); return o && o.customer === 'Ada' && o.items[0].qty === 2"},{"label":"JSON inside fences and chatter works","test":"const o = parseOrder('Sure! Here it is:\\n```json\\n{\"customer\":\"Bayo\",\"items\":[{\"name\":\" Rice \",\"qty\":1}],\"deliver\":\"delivery\"}\\n```'); return o && o.items[0].name === 'Rice'"},{"label":"extra fields are dropped","test":"const o = parseOrder('{\"customer\":\"Ada\",\"items\":[{\"name\":\"Tea\",\"qty\":1}],\"deliver\":\"pickup\",\"discount\":90}'); return o && !('discount' in o) && Object.keys(o).length === 3"},{"label":"wrong types are refused","test":"return parseOrder('{\"customer\":\"Ada\",\"items\":[{\"name\":\"Tea\",\"qty\":\"two\"}],\"deliver\":\"pickup\"}') === null"},{"label":"bad deliver value is refused","test":"return parseOrder('{\"customer\":\"Ada\",\"items\":[{\"name\":\"Tea\",\"qty\":1}],\"deliver\":\"drone\"}') === null"},{"label":"broken JSON returns null without crashing","test":"return parseOrder('{\"customer\": \"Ada\", items: [}') === null && parseOrder('no json') === null"}],"hint":"const a = text.indexOf('{'), b = text.lastIndexOf('}'); if (a < 0 || b < a) return null; let o; try { o = JSON.parse(text.slice(a, b + 1)) } catch { return null }. Use Number.isInteger(i.qty)."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-tokens$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Tokens, chunking and cost control","kind":"code","task":"Write estimateTokens, cost and chunk.","hint":"chunk: const step = size - overlap; const out = []; for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i += step) { out.push(words.slice(i, i + size)); if (i + size >= words.length) break } return out","body":"## Long documents do not fit\n\nA 200-page policy manual, a year of sales notes, a book: you often cannot (or should not) send everything to the model. Even when it fits, sending less is **cheaper, faster and often more accurate**.\n\n## Estimating tokens and cost\n\nFor planning, a simple estimate is enough: **tokens ≈ characters ÷ 4** for English (round up). If input costs \\$1 per million tokens and output \\$5 per million, a call with 8,000 input and 1,000 output tokens costs 8,000 × 1/1,000,000 + 1,000 × 5/1,000,000 = **\\$0.013**. Multiply by calls per day before you launch a feature.\n\n## Chunking\n\nTo search or summarise a long text, split it into **chunks** of a few hundred words, with a small **overlap** so a sentence cut at the edge still appears whole in one chunk. Each chunk can then be summarised separately (map) and the summaries combined (reduce), or searched (next lesson).\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `estimateTokens(text)`: `Math.ceil(text.length / 4)`.\n2. `cost(inTokens, outTokens, inPerM, outPerM)`: the US dollar cost, **rounded to 4 decimal places**.\n3. `chunk(words, size, overlap)`: `words` is an array of words. Return an array of chunks (each an array of words) of at most `size` words, where each chunk starts `size - overlap` words after the previous one. Stop when the last word is included. If `overlap >= size`, throw an Error.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":3}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-tokens$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-tokens","title":"Tokens, chunking and cost control","minutes":20,"kind":"code","outline":"Estimate tokens and cost, and split long documents into overlapping chunks.","body":"## Long documents do not fit\n\nA 200-page policy manual, a year of sales notes, a book: you often cannot (or should not) send everything to the model. Even when it fits, sending less is **cheaper, faster and often more accurate**.\n\n## Estimating tokens and cost\n\nFor planning, a simple estimate is enough: **tokens ≈ characters ÷ 4** for English (round up). If input costs \\$1 per million tokens and output \\$5 per million, a call with 8,000 input and 1,000 output tokens costs 8,000 × 1/1,000,000 + 1,000 × 5/1,000,000 = **\\$0.013**. Multiply by calls per day before you launch a feature.\n\n## Chunking\n\nTo search or summarise a long text, split it into **chunks** of a few hundred words, with a small **overlap** so a sentence cut at the edge still appears whole in one chunk. Each chunk can then be summarised separately (map) and the summaries combined (reduce), or searched (next lesson).\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `estimateTokens(text)`: `Math.ceil(text.length / 4)`.\n2. `cost(inTokens, outTokens, inPerM, outPerM)`: the US dollar cost, **rounded to 4 decimal places**.\n3. `chunk(words, size, overlap)`: `words` is an array of words. Return an array of chunks (each an array of words) of at most `size` words, where each chunk starts `size - overlap` words after the previous one. Stop when the last word is included. If `overlap >= size`, throw an Error.","task":"Write estimateTokens, cost and chunk.","starter":"<script>\n  function estimateTokens(text) {\n  }\n  function cost(inTokens, outTokens, inPerM, outPerM) {\n  }\n  function chunk(words, size, overlap) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"tokens estimate rounds up","test":"return estimateTokens('abcdefghi') === 3 && estimateTokens('') === 0"},{"label":"cost of 8,000 in and 1,000 out","test":"return cost(8000, 1000, 1, 5) === 0.013"},{"label":"cost is rounded to 4 places","test":"return cost(1234, 567, 3, 15) === 0.0122"},{"label":"chunks of 4 with overlap 1","test":"return JSON.stringify(chunk('a b c d e f g h'.split(' '), 4, 1)) === JSON.stringify([['a','b','c','d'],['d','e','f','g'],['g','h']])"},{"label":"a short text is one chunk","test":"return chunk(['x', 'y'], 5, 1).length === 1"},{"label":"overlap >= size throws","test":"try { chunk(['a'], 3, 3); return false } catch { return true }"}],"hint":"chunk: const step = size - overlap; const out = []; for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i += step) { out.push(words.slice(i, i + size)); if (i + size >= words.length) break } return out"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-rag$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Grounding AI in your documents (RAG)","kind":"code","task":"Write words, score, topChunks and groundedPrompt.","hint":"words: text.toLowerCase().split(/[^a-z0-9]+/).filter(w => w && !STOP.includes(w)). topChunks: chunks.map((c, i) => ({ c, i, s: score(question, c) })).filter(x => x.s > 0).sort((a, b) => b.s - a.s || a.i - b.i).slice(0, k).map(x => x.c)","body":"## Retrieval-augmented generation\n\n**RAG** means: before asking the model, **retrieve** the few pieces of your own documents that are relevant to the question, and put only those into the prompt. The model then answers from your facts instead of its memory.\n\nThe pipeline:\n\n1. **Split** documents into chunks (last lesson).\n2. **Index** them: production systems use **embeddings** (numbers that capture meaning) in a vector database; small systems can use keyword scoring.\n3. **Retrieve** the top few chunks for the question.\n4. **Generate**: a prompt with the chunks, the question, and the rule *\"answer only from these sources; if they do not contain the answer, say so; quote the source number\"*.\n\n## Keyword scoring\n\nA simple, surprisingly useful score: count how many of the question's meaningful words appear in each chunk. Ignore very common words (\"the\", \"is\", \"a\", \"what\"…) and compare in lower case.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `words(text)`: lower-case words (letters and numbers only; split on anything else), without the `STOP` words, and without empty strings.\n2. `score(question, chunk)`: how many of the question's **distinct** words appear in the chunk's words.\n3. `topChunks(question, chunks, k)`: the `k` chunks with the highest score (score above 0 only), highest first; ties keep their original order.\n4. `groundedPrompt(question, chunks)`: a prompt that lists each chunk as `[1] ...`, `[2] ...`, then `Question: ...`, and includes the sentence `If the sources do not contain the answer, say \"I don't know\".`","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":4}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-rag$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-rag","title":"Grounding AI in your documents (RAG)","minutes":30,"kind":"code","outline":"Retrieval-augmented generation: find the right chunks and build a grounded prompt.","body":"## Retrieval-augmented generation\n\n**RAG** means: before asking the model, **retrieve** the few pieces of your own documents that are relevant to the question, and put only those into the prompt. The model then answers from your facts instead of its memory.\n\nThe pipeline:\n\n1. **Split** documents into chunks (last lesson).\n2. **Index** them: production systems use **embeddings** (numbers that capture meaning) in a vector database; small systems can use keyword scoring.\n3. **Retrieve** the top few chunks for the question.\n4. **Generate**: a prompt with the chunks, the question, and the rule *\"answer only from these sources; if they do not contain the answer, say so; quote the source number\"*.\n\n## Keyword scoring\n\nA simple, surprisingly useful score: count how many of the question's meaningful words appear in each chunk. Ignore very common words (\"the\", \"is\", \"a\", \"what\"…) and compare in lower case.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `words(text)`: lower-case words (letters and numbers only; split on anything else), without the `STOP` words, and without empty strings.\n2. `score(question, chunk)`: how many of the question's **distinct** words appear in the chunk's words.\n3. `topChunks(question, chunks, k)`: the `k` chunks with the highest score (score above 0 only), highest first; ties keep their original order.\n4. `groundedPrompt(question, chunks)`: a prompt that lists each chunk as `[1] ...`, `[2] ...`, then `Question: ...`, and includes the sentence `If the sources do not contain the answer, say \"I don't know\".`","task":"Write words, score, topChunks and groundedPrompt.","starter":"<script>\n  const STOP = ['the', 'is', 'a', 'an', 'of', 'to', 'and', 'in', 'what', 'how', 'do', 'i', 'for', 'on', 'are', 'my']\n\n  function words(text) {\n  }\n  function score(question, chunk) {\n  }\n  function topChunks(question, chunks, k) {\n  }\n  function groundedPrompt(question, chunks) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"words drops stop words and punctuation","test":"return JSON.stringify(words('What is the refund policy?')) === '[\"refund\",\"policy\"]'"},{"label":"score counts distinct question words found","test":"return score('refund policy refund', 'Our refund policy: 7 days.') === 2"},{"label":"topChunks ranks by score","test":"const c = ['Delivery takes 2 working hours in Abuja.', 'Refunds within 7 days with receipt.', 'Refund policy: refunds by transfer within 7 days.']; return JSON.stringify(topChunks('refund policy days', c, 2)) === JSON.stringify([c[2], c[1]])"},{"label":"chunks with score 0 are left out","test":"return topChunks('opening hours', ['We sell bread.'], 3).length === 0"},{"label":"grounded prompt numbers the sources and keeps the rule","test":"const p = groundedPrompt('When?', ['Mon to Sat', 'Closed Sunday']); return p.includes('[1] Mon to Sat') && p.includes('[2] Closed Sunday') && p.includes('Question: When?') && p.includes('say \"I don\\'t know\"')"}],"hint":"words: text.toLowerCase().split(/[^a-z0-9]+/).filter(w => w && !STOP.includes(w)). topChunks: chunks.map((c, i) => ({ c, i, s: score(question, c) })).filter(x => x.s > 0).sort((a, b) => b.s - a.s || a.i - b.i).slice(0, k).map(x => x.c)"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-evals$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Testing AI: evaluations and guardrails","kind":"code","task":"Write grade(answer, test) and runEval(answers, tests).","hint":"const a = answer.toLowerCase(); for (const m of test.mustInclude || []) if (!a.includes(m.toLowerCase())) problems.push('missing: ' + m). Words: answer.trim().split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean).length","body":"## \"It worked when I tried it\" is not enough\n\nModel answers vary. Prompts change. Models get upgraded. Professional teams keep an **evaluation set**: a list of realistic inputs with what a good answer must (and must not) contain, and run it after every change, just like software tests.\n\n## Kinds of checks\n\n- **Exact / contains**: the answer includes the right order number, the right price.\n- **Must not contain**: no promises you cannot keep, no competitor names, no personal data.\n- **Format**: valid JSON, under N words, in the right language.\n- **Model-graded**: another model judges tone or helpfulness with a clear rubric (useful, but check it with humans too).\n\n## Guardrails\n\nChecks that run **before** sending to users: block outputs that contain phone numbers or emails that were not in the input, refuse when the input asks for something out of scope, cap length, and send uncertain cases to a person.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite `grade(answer, test)` and `runEval(answers, tests)`:\n\n- A test is `{ id, mustInclude: [...], mustNotInclude: [...], maxWords }` (all optional except id).\n- `grade` returns `{ id, pass, problems }`: `problems` lists text like `missing: 7 days`, `forbidden: guarantee`, `too long: 52 > 40`. Matching ignores case. Words are separated by whitespace.\n- `runEval` grades `answers[i]` against `tests[i]` and returns `{ passed, total, score }` where score is the percent passed, rounded.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":5}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-evals$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-evals","title":"Testing AI: evaluations and guardrails","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Build an evaluation harness that scores AI answers against test cases, plus simple guardrails.","body":"## \"It worked when I tried it\" is not enough\n\nModel answers vary. Prompts change. Models get upgraded. Professional teams keep an **evaluation set**: a list of realistic inputs with what a good answer must (and must not) contain, and run it after every change, just like software tests.\n\n## Kinds of checks\n\n- **Exact / contains**: the answer includes the right order number, the right price.\n- **Must not contain**: no promises you cannot keep, no competitor names, no personal data.\n- **Format**: valid JSON, under N words, in the right language.\n- **Model-graded**: another model judges tone or helpfulness with a clear rubric (useful, but check it with humans too).\n\n## Guardrails\n\nChecks that run **before** sending to users: block outputs that contain phone numbers or emails that were not in the input, refuse when the input asks for something out of scope, cap length, and send uncertain cases to a person.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite `grade(answer, test)` and `runEval(answers, tests)`:\n\n- A test is `{ id, mustInclude: [...], mustNotInclude: [...], maxWords }` (all optional except id).\n- `grade` returns `{ id, pass, problems }`: `problems` lists text like `missing: 7 days`, `forbidden: guarantee`, `too long: 52 > 40`. Matching ignores case. Words are separated by whitespace.\n- `runEval` grades `answers[i]` against `tests[i]` and returns `{ passed, total, score }` where score is the percent passed, rounded.","task":"Write grade(answer, test) and runEval(answers, tests).","starter":"<script>\n  function grade(answer, test) {\n  }\n  function runEval(answers, tests) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"a good answer passes","test":"const g = grade('Refunds are paid within 7 days.', { id: 't1', mustInclude: ['7 days'], mustNotInclude: ['guarantee'], maxWords: 20 }); return g.pass === true && g.problems.length === 0"},{"label":"missing text is reported","test":"const g = grade('We refund quickly.', { id: 't2', mustInclude: ['7 days'] }); return g.pass === false && g.problems.includes('missing: 7 days')"},{"label":"forbidden text is reported (any case)","test":"const g = grade('We GUARANTEE it.', { id: 't3', mustNotInclude: ['guarantee'] }); return g.problems.includes('forbidden: guarantee')"},{"label":"length limit is checked","test":"const g = grade('one two three four five', { id: 't4', maxWords: 3 }); return g.problems.includes('too long: 5 > 3')"},{"label":"runEval scores the set","test":"const r = runEval(['7 days', 'no'], [{ id: 'a', mustInclude: ['7 days'] }, { id: 'b', mustInclude: ['yes'] }]); return r.passed === 1 && r.total === 2 && r.score === 50"}],"hint":"const a = answer.toLowerCase(); for (const m of test.mustInclude || []) if (!a.includes(m.toLowerCase())) problems.push('missing: ' + m). Words: answer.trim().split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean).length"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-agents$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Automation and AI agents","kind":"code","task":"Write runTool(call, approve) and agentLoop(steps, approve, maxSteps).","hint":"const tool = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(TOOLS, call.name) ? TOOLS[call.name] : null. Build the result, push { name: call.name, ok: result.ok } to LOG, return it.","body":"## From answers to actions\n\nAn **AI agent** is a model that can **use tools**: search, read a spreadsheet, send an email, create an invoice. It works in a loop:\n\n1. Read the goal and the tools available (each with a name, description and input schema)\n2. Decide on a tool call (or a final answer)\n3. Your code **runs** the tool and sends back the result\n4. Repeat until done (with a maximum number of steps)\n\nNo-code automation tools (Zapier, Make, n8n) and APIs with tool use all follow this idea.\n\n## Agents need guardrails\n\nAn agent that can act can also act **wrongly**: email the wrong customer, pay twice, delete data. Safe agent design:\n\n- **Allow-list** tools: the model can only call tools you defined, with inputs you validate.\n- **Read vs write**: reading is low risk; anything that sends, pays or deletes needs **human approval**.\n- **Limits**: maximum steps, maximum money, rate limits.\n- **Logs**: record every tool call and result for review.\n- **Never** let instructions found inside data (emails, web pages) change the agent's rules: that is prompt injection.\n\n## Your task: a safe tool runner\n\nWrite `runTool(call, approve)`:\n\n- `call` is `{ name, input }` from the model. `TOOLS` lists allowed tools with `risky` (needs approval) and `run` functions.\n- Unknown tool → return `{ ok: false, error: 'unknown tool' }`.\n- If the tool is risky, call `approve(call)`; if it returns false → `{ ok: false, error: 'not approved' }`.\n- Otherwise return `{ ok: true, result: tool.run(call.input) }`; if `run` throws, return `{ ok: false, error: <the message> }`.\n- Every call (including refused ones) is pushed to `LOG` as `{ name, ok }`.\n\nThen write `agentLoop(steps, approve, maxSteps)` that runs the tool calls in `steps` in order but **stops** after `maxSteps` calls, returning the array of results.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":6}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-agents$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-agents","title":"Automation and AI agents","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Tools, the agent loop, and build a safe tool runner with allow-lists and human approval.","body":"## From answers to actions\n\nAn **AI agent** is a model that can **use tools**: search, read a spreadsheet, send an email, create an invoice. It works in a loop:\n\n1. Read the goal and the tools available (each with a name, description and input schema)\n2. Decide on a tool call (or a final answer)\n3. Your code **runs** the tool and sends back the result\n4. Repeat until done (with a maximum number of steps)\n\nNo-code automation tools (Zapier, Make, n8n) and APIs with tool use all follow this idea.\n\n## Agents need guardrails\n\nAn agent that can act can also act **wrongly**: email the wrong customer, pay twice, delete data. Safe agent design:\n\n- **Allow-list** tools: the model can only call tools you defined, with inputs you validate.\n- **Read vs write**: reading is low risk; anything that sends, pays or deletes needs **human approval**.\n- **Limits**: maximum steps, maximum money, rate limits.\n- **Logs**: record every tool call and result for review.\n- **Never** let instructions found inside data (emails, web pages) change the agent's rules: that is prompt injection.\n\n## Your task: a safe tool runner\n\nWrite `runTool(call, approve)`:\n\n- `call` is `{ name, input }` from the model. `TOOLS` lists allowed tools with `risky` (needs approval) and `run` functions.\n- Unknown tool → return `{ ok: false, error: 'unknown tool' }`.\n- If the tool is risky, call `approve(call)`; if it returns false → `{ ok: false, error: 'not approved' }`.\n- Otherwise return `{ ok: true, result: tool.run(call.input) }`; if `run` throws, return `{ ok: false, error: <the message> }`.\n- Every call (including refused ones) is pushed to `LOG` as `{ name, ok }`.\n\nThen write `agentLoop(steps, approve, maxSteps)` that runs the tool calls in `steps` in order but **stops** after `maxSteps` calls, returning the array of results.","task":"Write runTool(call, approve) and agentLoop(steps, approve, maxSteps).","starter":"<script>\n  const LOG = []\n  const STOCK = { bread: 10, milk: 4 }\n  const TOOLS = {\n    check_stock: { risky: false, run: (i) => { if (!(i.item in STOCK)) throw new Error('no such item'); return STOCK[i.item] } },\n    send_sms: { risky: true, run: (i) => 'sent to ' + i.to },\n  }\n\n  function runTool(call, approve) {\n    // your code\n  }\n\n  function agentLoop(steps, approve, maxSteps) {\n    // your code\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"a safe tool runs without approval","test":"LOG.length = 0; const r = runTool({ name: 'check_stock', input: { item: 'bread' } }, () => false); return r.ok === true && r.result === 10"},{"label":"unknown tools are refused","test":"return runTool({ name: 'delete_all', input: {} }, () => true).error === 'unknown tool'"},{"label":"risky tools need approval","test":"return runTool({ name: 'send_sms', input: { to: '0803' } }, () => false).error === 'not approved' && runTool({ name: 'send_sms', input: { to: '0803' } }, () => true).result === 'sent to 0803'"},{"label":"tool errors are caught","test":"const r = runTool({ name: 'check_stock', input: { item: 'gold' } }, () => true); return r.ok === false && r.error === 'no such item'"},{"label":"every call is logged","test":"LOG.length = 0; runTool({ name: 'nope', input: {} }, () => true); runTool({ name: 'check_stock', input: { item: 'milk' } }, () => true); return LOG.length === 2 && LOG[0].ok === false && LOG[1].ok === true"},{"label":"the loop stops at maxSteps","test":"const s = Array.from({ length: 10 }, () => ({ name: 'check_stock', input: { item: 'milk' } })); return agentLoop(s, () => true, 3).length === 3"}],"hint":"const tool = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(TOOLS, call.name) ? TOOLS[call.name] : null. Build the result, push { name: call.name, ok: result.ok } to LOG, return it."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-tools$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Tool use: letting AI call your functions","kind":"code","task":"Write validate and runTool.","hint":"For types: integer → Number.isInteger(v); number → typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v); string and boolean → typeof v === type. Loop over required, then over Object.keys(args).","body":"## The model cannot press buttons, but it can ask you to\n\nA language model on its own only produces text. It cannot check today's exchange rate, look up an order or send an SMS. **Tool use** (also called *function calling*) fixes that: you describe some functions to the model, and when a question needs one, the model replies with a structured request: *\"please call get_order with order_id = 1042\"*. **Your code** runs the function and sends the result back, and the model writes the final answer.\n\nThink of the model as a clever receptionist who cannot leave the desk. You give the receptionist a list of phone extensions (the tools) with a note on what each department does. When a visitor asks something, the receptionist decides whom to call and what to ask, but the department (your code) does the actual work.\n\n## Describing a tool\n\nEach tool has a name, a description, and an **input schema** written in JSON Schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"get_order\",\n  \"description\": \"Look up one customer order by its number. Use when the user asks about an order's status or delivery.\",\n  \"input_schema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": { \"order_id\": { \"type\": \"integer\", \"description\": \"The order number, e.g. 1042\" } },\n    \"required\": [\"order_id\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe **description is a prompt**: say when to use the tool and when not to. Good descriptions are the difference between a model that picks the right tool and one that guesses.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Send the user's message plus the tool list.\n2. If the model replies with a tool call, **validate** the arguments, run the function, and send the result back.\n3. Repeat until the model gives a normal text answer.\n\n## Never trust the arguments blindly\n\nThe model's tool call is a *suggestion* produced from text, possibly text written by an attacker (a web page or email can contain instructions: this is **prompt injection**). Treat tool arguments like form input from a stranger:\n\n- Check every required field exists and has the right type.\n- Reject unknown tools and unknown fields.\n- Enforce permissions in **your** code: the model asking to read order 1042 does not mean this user may see order 1042.\n- For actions that spend money or send messages, ask the human to confirm.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `validate(args, schema)`: check an arguments object against a simple schema (`type: \"object\"`, `properties` with `type` of `string`, `integer`, `number` or `boolean`, and `required`). Return a list of error messages (empty when valid). Report a missing required field as `\"order_id is required\"`, a wrong type as `\"order_id must be integer\"`, and an extra field as `\"unknown field x\"`.\n2. `runTool(call, tools)`: `call` is `{ name, input }`; `tools` maps a name to `{ schema, fn }`. Return `{ error: \"unknown tool\" }` for an unknown name, `{ error: <errors joined with \"; \"> }` when invalid, otherwise `{ result: fn(input) }`.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":7}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-tools$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-tools","title":"Tool use: letting AI call your functions","minutes":35,"kind":"code","outline":"How function calling works, describing tools with JSON Schema, validating the model's arguments and running the tool safely.","body":"## The model cannot press buttons, but it can ask you to\n\nA language model on its own only produces text. It cannot check today's exchange rate, look up an order or send an SMS. **Tool use** (also called *function calling*) fixes that: you describe some functions to the model, and when a question needs one, the model replies with a structured request: *\"please call get_order with order_id = 1042\"*. **Your code** runs the function and sends the result back, and the model writes the final answer.\n\nThink of the model as a clever receptionist who cannot leave the desk. You give the receptionist a list of phone extensions (the tools) with a note on what each department does. When a visitor asks something, the receptionist decides whom to call and what to ask, but the department (your code) does the actual work.\n\n## Describing a tool\n\nEach tool has a name, a description, and an **input schema** written in JSON Schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"get_order\",\n  \"description\": \"Look up one customer order by its number. Use when the user asks about an order's status or delivery.\",\n  \"input_schema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": { \"order_id\": { \"type\": \"integer\", \"description\": \"The order number, e.g. 1042\" } },\n    \"required\": [\"order_id\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe **description is a prompt**: say when to use the tool and when not to. Good descriptions are the difference between a model that picks the right tool and one that guesses.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Send the user's message plus the tool list.\n2. If the model replies with a tool call, **validate** the arguments, run the function, and send the result back.\n3. Repeat until the model gives a normal text answer.\n\n## Never trust the arguments blindly\n\nThe model's tool call is a *suggestion* produced from text, possibly text written by an attacker (a web page or email can contain instructions: this is **prompt injection**). Treat tool arguments like form input from a stranger:\n\n- Check every required field exists and has the right type.\n- Reject unknown tools and unknown fields.\n- Enforce permissions in **your** code: the model asking to read order 1042 does not mean this user may see order 1042.\n- For actions that spend money or send messages, ask the human to confirm.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `validate(args, schema)`: check an arguments object against a simple schema (`type: \"object\"`, `properties` with `type` of `string`, `integer`, `number` or `boolean`, and `required`). Return a list of error messages (empty when valid). Report a missing required field as `\"order_id is required\"`, a wrong type as `\"order_id must be integer\"`, and an extra field as `\"unknown field x\"`.\n2. `runTool(call, tools)`: `call` is `{ name, input }`; `tools` maps a name to `{ schema, fn }`. Return `{ error: \"unknown tool\" }` for an unknown name, `{ error: <errors joined with \"; \"> }` when invalid, otherwise `{ result: fn(input) }`.","task":"Write validate and runTool.","starter":"<script>\n  function validate(args, schema) {\n    return []\n  }\n  function runTool(call, tools) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"valid arguments give no errors","test":"const s = { type: 'object', properties: { order_id: { type: 'integer' }, note: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['order_id'] }; return validate({ order_id: 1042 }, s).length === 0 && validate({ order_id: 7, note: 'hi' }, s).length === 0"},{"label":"missing, wrong type and unknown fields reported","test":"const s = { type: 'object', properties: { order_id: { type: 'integer' } }, required: ['order_id'] }; const a = validate({}, s), b = validate({ order_id: '1042' }, s), c = validate({ order_id: 1.5 }, s), d = validate({ order_id: 1, x: 2 }, s); return a.includes('order_id is required') && b.includes('order_id must be integer') && c.includes('order_id must be integer') && d.includes('unknown field x')"},{"label":"number and boolean types","test":"const s = { type: 'object', properties: { amount: { type: 'number' }, urgent: { type: 'boolean' } }, required: [] }; return validate({ amount: 2.5, urgent: false }, s).length === 0 && validate({ urgent: 'yes' }, s).includes('urgent must be boolean')"},{"label":"runTool runs valid calls and refuses the rest","test":"const tools = { get_order: { schema: { type: 'object', properties: { order_id: { type: 'integer' } }, required: ['order_id'] }, fn: (i) => 'order ' + i.order_id + ' shipped' } }; return runTool({ name: 'get_order', input: { order_id: 5 } }, tools).result === 'order 5 shipped' && runTool({ name: 'delete_all', input: {} }, tools).error === 'unknown tool' && runTool({ name: 'get_order', input: {} }, tools).error === 'order_id is required'"}],"hint":"For types: integer → Number.isInteger(v); number → typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v); string and boolean → typeof v === type. Loop over required, then over Object.keys(args)."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-embeddings$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Embeddings and semantic search","kind":"code","task":"Write dot, norm, cosine and search.","hint":"cosine: const n = norm(a) * norm(b); return n === 0 ? 0 : dot(a, b) / n. search: docs.map(d => ({ text: d.text, score: Math.round(cosine(queryVec, d.vec) * 1000) / 1000 })).filter(…).sort(…).slice(0, k)","body":"## Meaning as a location\n\nImagine a huge map where every sentence has a position. Sentences that mean similar things sit close together: *\"How do I get my money back?\"* lands right next to *\"refund policy\"*, even though they share no words. An **embedding** is that position: a list of numbers (often 256 to 3,000 of them) produced by an embedding model.\n\nKeyword search (last lessons) only finds shared words. **Semantic search** with embeddings finds shared *meaning*, across synonyms and even across languages.\n\n## Measuring closeness: cosine similarity\n\nEach embedding is a vector, an arrow pointing in some direction. Two arrows pointing the same way mean similar things. **Cosine similarity** measures the angle between them:\n\n- `1`: same direction (very similar)\n- `0`: unrelated\n- `-1`: opposite\n\nThe formula: `cos(a, b) = (a · b) / (|a| × |b|)`, where `a · b` is the dot product (multiply matching numbers and add them up) and `|a|` is the length (the square root of the sum of squares).\n\n## Semantic search in four steps\n\n1. **Embed every chunk** of your documents once, and store the vectors.\n2. **Embed the question** when it arrives.\n3. **Find the nearest chunks** by cosine similarity: the top k.\n4. Put those chunks into a grounded prompt (the RAG lesson).\n\n## Vector databases\n\nWith millions of chunks, comparing the question to every vector is slow. **Vector databases** and extensions such as **pgvector** for PostgreSQL (available in Supabase) build special indexes for fast approximate nearest-neighbour search. The idea is the same as your code in this lesson, just at scale.\n\n## Practical tips\n\n- Use the **same embedding model** for documents and questions; vectors from different models are like coordinates on different maps.\n- **Hybrid search** (keywords plus embeddings) often beats either alone, especially for names, codes and numbers.\n- Set a **minimum similarity**: if nothing is close enough, say \"I don't know\" instead of grounding on irrelevant text.\n- Re-embed when documents change.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe vectors here are tiny (3 numbers) so you can check them by hand; real ones work exactly the same way.\n\n1. `dot(a, b)`, `norm(a)` and `cosine(a, b)` (return `0` if either vector has length 0).\n2. `search(queryVec, docs, k, minScore = 0)`: `docs` is a list of `{ text, vec }`. Return the top `k` as `{ text, score }` (score rounded to 3 decimals), highest first, leaving out any below `minScore`.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":8}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-embeddings$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-embeddings","title":"Embeddings and semantic search","minutes":35,"kind":"code","outline":"What embeddings are, cosine similarity, nearest-neighbour search and how vector databases power RAG.","body":"## Meaning as a location\n\nImagine a huge map where every sentence has a position. Sentences that mean similar things sit close together: *\"How do I get my money back?\"* lands right next to *\"refund policy\"*, even though they share no words. An **embedding** is that position: a list of numbers (often 256 to 3,000 of them) produced by an embedding model.\n\nKeyword search (last lessons) only finds shared words. **Semantic search** with embeddings finds shared *meaning*, across synonyms and even across languages.\n\n## Measuring closeness: cosine similarity\n\nEach embedding is a vector, an arrow pointing in some direction. Two arrows pointing the same way mean similar things. **Cosine similarity** measures the angle between them:\n\n- `1`: same direction (very similar)\n- `0`: unrelated\n- `-1`: opposite\n\nThe formula: `cos(a, b) = (a · b) / (|a| × |b|)`, where `a · b` is the dot product (multiply matching numbers and add them up) and `|a|` is the length (the square root of the sum of squares).\n\n## Semantic search in four steps\n\n1. **Embed every chunk** of your documents once, and store the vectors.\n2. **Embed the question** when it arrives.\n3. **Find the nearest chunks** by cosine similarity: the top k.\n4. Put those chunks into a grounded prompt (the RAG lesson).\n\n## Vector databases\n\nWith millions of chunks, comparing the question to every vector is slow. **Vector databases** and extensions such as **pgvector** for PostgreSQL (available in Supabase) build special indexes for fast approximate nearest-neighbour search. The idea is the same as your code in this lesson, just at scale.\n\n## Practical tips\n\n- Use the **same embedding model** for documents and questions; vectors from different models are like coordinates on different maps.\n- **Hybrid search** (keywords plus embeddings) often beats either alone, especially for names, codes and numbers.\n- Set a **minimum similarity**: if nothing is close enough, say \"I don't know\" instead of grounding on irrelevant text.\n- Re-embed when documents change.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe vectors here are tiny (3 numbers) so you can check them by hand; real ones work exactly the same way.\n\n1. `dot(a, b)`, `norm(a)` and `cosine(a, b)` (return `0` if either vector has length 0).\n2. `search(queryVec, docs, k, minScore = 0)`: `docs` is a list of `{ text, vec }`. Return the top `k` as `{ text, score }` (score rounded to 3 decimals), highest first, leaving out any below `minScore`.","task":"Write dot, norm, cosine and search.","starter":"<script>\n  function dot(a, b) {\n  }\n  function norm(a) {\n  }\n  function cosine(a, b) {\n  }\n  function search(queryVec, docs, k, minScore = 0) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"dot and norm","test":"return dot([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]) === 32 && norm([3, 4]) === 5"},{"label":"cosine of same, opposite and unrelated directions","test":"return Math.abs(cosine([1, 2], [2, 4]) - 1) < 1e-9 && Math.abs(cosine([1, 0], [-1, 0]) + 1) < 1e-9 && Math.abs(cosine([1, 0], [0, 1])) < 1e-9 && cosine([0, 0], [1, 1]) === 0"},{"label":"search ranks by meaning","test":"const docs = [{ text: 'refunds', vec: [0.9, 0.1, 0] }, { text: 'delivery', vec: [0, 1, 0.2] }, { text: 'returns', vec: [0.8, 0.3, 0.1] }]; const r = search([1, 0.2, 0], docs, 2); return r.length === 2 && r[0].text === 'refunds' && r[1].text === 'returns' && r[0].score === 0.996"},{"label":"minScore leaves out weak matches","test":"const docs = [{ text: 'a', vec: [1, 0] }, { text: 'b', vec: [0, 1] }]; const r = search([1, 0.1], docs, 5, 0.5); return r.length === 1 && r[0].text === 'a'"}],"hint":"cosine: const n = norm(a) * norm(b); return n === 0 ? 0 : dot(a, b) / n. search: docs.map(d => ({ text: d.text, score: Math.round(cosine(queryVec, d.vec) * 1000) / 1000 })).filter(…).sort(…).slice(0, k)"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-multimodal$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Images, speech and multimodal AI","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## AI that sees and hears\n\nModern models are **multimodal**: besides text, they can take in images, documents, audio and sometimes video, and some can produce images or speech. This opens up work that used to need a person looking at every page.\n\n## Vision: reading pictures and documents\n\nSend an image with a question and the model can:\n\n- **Read documents**: invoices, receipts, ID-free forms, handwritten notes, tables in PDFs. Combine with structured output (lesson 3) to turn a photo of a receipt into JSON: vendor, date, items, total.\n- **Describe and classify**: is this a photo of a damaged parcel? Which product is on the shelf?\n- **Understand charts and screenshots**: \"what does this error message on my screen mean?\"\n\nLimits: small or blurry text, unusual handwriting and dense tables cause mistakes. Models can misread numbers, which matters a lot for money. **Always validate**: check totals add up, dates are real, and ask a human to review low-confidence results.\n\n## Speech-to-text and text-to-speech\n\n- **Speech-to-text** (transcription) turns recordings into text: meeting notes, call-centre quality checks, voice notes on WhatsApp, captions for videos. Accuracy varies with accents, background noise and languages; test with your real users' voices.\n- **Text-to-speech** reads text aloud in natural voices: accessibility, phone menus, learning apps for people who read less comfortably.\n\nTogether they make **voice assistants**: speech in, model in the middle, speech out. Latency (the delay) is the main design challenge.\n\n## Image generation\n\nImage models create pictures from descriptions: marketing drafts, illustrations, product mock-ups, social media graphics. Be careful:\n\n- Do not present generated images as real photos of products, customers or events.\n- Avoid copying artists' styles or real brands and characters; respect copyright and trademarks.\n- Do not generate images of real people without consent.\n\n## Costs and privacy\n\nImages and audio use many more tokens than text, so they cost more. Resize images to what is needed. Photos and recordings often contain **personal data** (faces, voices, addresses): get consent, store as little as possible, and follow data protection law.\n\n## Designing multimodal features\n\n1. Decide what must be **extracted** (fields, labels, a transcript).\n2. Ask for **structured output** with a confidence or \"unsure\" option.\n3. **Validate** in code.\n4. Route doubtful cases to a **human**.\n5. Measure accuracy on a test set of real examples before launch (the evaluations lesson).","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":9}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-multimodal$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-multimodal","title":"Images, speech and multimodal AI","minutes":25,"kind":"read","outline":"Vision models, document reading, speech-to-text and text-to-speech, image generation, and their limits.","body":"## AI that sees and hears\n\nModern models are **multimodal**: besides text, they can take in images, documents, audio and sometimes video, and some can produce images or speech. This opens up work that used to need a person looking at every page.\n\n## Vision: reading pictures and documents\n\nSend an image with a question and the model can:\n\n- **Read documents**: invoices, receipts, ID-free forms, handwritten notes, tables in PDFs. Combine with structured output (lesson 3) to turn a photo of a receipt into JSON: vendor, date, items, total.\n- **Describe and classify**: is this a photo of a damaged parcel? Which product is on the shelf?\n- **Understand charts and screenshots**: \"what does this error message on my screen mean?\"\n\nLimits: small or blurry text, unusual handwriting and dense tables cause mistakes. Models can misread numbers, which matters a lot for money. **Always validate**: check totals add up, dates are real, and ask a human to review low-confidence results.\n\n## Speech-to-text and text-to-speech\n\n- **Speech-to-text** (transcription) turns recordings into text: meeting notes, call-centre quality checks, voice notes on WhatsApp, captions for videos. Accuracy varies with accents, background noise and languages; test with your real users' voices.\n- **Text-to-speech** reads text aloud in natural voices: accessibility, phone menus, learning apps for people who read less comfortably.\n\nTogether they make **voice assistants**: speech in, model in the middle, speech out. Latency (the delay) is the main design challenge.\n\n## Image generation\n\nImage models create pictures from descriptions: marketing drafts, illustrations, product mock-ups, social media graphics. Be careful:\n\n- Do not present generated images as real photos of products, customers or events.\n- Avoid copying artists' styles or real brands and characters; respect copyright and trademarks.\n- Do not generate images of real people without consent.\n\n## Costs and privacy\n\nImages and audio use many more tokens than text, so they cost more. Resize images to what is needed. Photos and recordings often contain **personal data** (faces, voices, addresses): get consent, store as little as possible, and follow data protection law.\n\n## Designing multimodal features\n\n1. Decide what must be **extracted** (fields, labels, a transcript).\n2. Ask for **structured output** with a confidence or \"unsure\" option.\n3. **Validate** in code.\n4. Route doubtful cases to a **human**.\n5. Measure accuracy on a test set of real examples before launch (the evaluations lesson).","quiz":[{"q":"A model reads a photographed receipt into JSON. What should your code do next?","options":["Trust it completely","Validate it, for example check the items add up to the total","Delete the photo and the JSON"],"answer":1,"why":"Models can misread numbers."},{"q":"Generated images should…","options":["Be presented as real product photos","Not be passed off as real photos, and respect copyright and consent","Always copy a famous artist"],"answer":1,"why":"Honesty and rights matter."},{"q":"Why test speech-to-text with your real users?","options":["Accuracy varies with accents, noise and language","It is always perfect","It only works in English"],"answer":0,"why":"Test with the voices you will actually get."}]}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-app$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Shipping an AI feature: memory, retries and limits","kind":"code","task":"Write trimHistory, backoff and shouldRetry.","hint":"trimHistory: start with used = count(system), walk the messages from the end backwards, and stop as soon as the next message would go over the budget; unshift the ones you keep.","body":"## From demo to product\n\nA demo works once on the developer's laptop. A product works thousands of times a day for people on slow networks, while the AI provider is busy, with users who paste whole novels into the chat box. This lesson covers the engineering that makes the difference.\n\n## Conversation memory\n\nModels do not remember previous messages. Every request must send the conversation so far. But conversations grow, and every message costs tokens. Like a notebook with limited pages, you must decide what to keep:\n\n- Always keep the **system prompt** (the rules).\n- Keep the **most recent** messages, because they matter most.\n- Drop (or summarise) the oldest messages when the budget is exceeded.\n- Never cut a message in half; drop whole turns.\n\n## Retries and exponential backoff\n\nNetworks fail and providers return \"overloaded\" or \"rate limited\" errors (HTTP 429, 500, 503, 529). Retrying immediately makes things worse, like everyone redialling a busy line at once. Wait longer each time: 1 second, then 2, then 4, then 8 (**exponential backoff**), with a little randomness (**jitter**) so many clients do not retry at the same moment. Give up after a few attempts and show a friendly message.\n\nDo **not** retry errors that will never succeed: a bad request (400) or a wrong key (401) needs fixing, not repeating.\n\n## Timeouts\n\nSet a timeout on every call. A request that hangs for two minutes is worse than a clear error after 30 seconds.\n\n## Limits protect you\n\n- **Per-user limits** (messages per day, as CDM Studio does) stop one person, or one bot, from spending your whole budget.\n- **Input limits**: cap the size of what users paste.\n- **Output limits**: set `max_tokens`.\n- **Spending alerts** at the provider.\n\n## Fallbacks\n\nIf the best model is unavailable, fall back to a smaller one, a cached answer, or a non-AI path (\"we could not generate a summary; here is the full text\"). The feature should degrade, not disappear.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `trimHistory(system, messages, budget, count)`: `count(text)` returns a token count. Return the most recent messages (keeping their order) such that `count(system)` plus the counts of the kept messages is within `budget`. Drop whole messages from the oldest end. Messages are `{ role, content }`.\n2. `backoff(attempt, base = 1000, max = 30000)`: the wait in milliseconds before retry number `attempt` (starting at 1): `base × 2^(attempt − 1)`, never more than `max`. (Jitter is left out so it can be checked.)\n3. `shouldRetry(status, attempt, maxAttempts = 4)`: `true` only for statuses 429, 500, 502, 503 or 529, and only while `attempt < maxAttempts`.","practice":null,"track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":10}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-app$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-app","title":"Shipping an AI feature: memory, retries and limits","minutes":35,"kind":"code","outline":"Conversation memory within a token budget, retries with exponential backoff, timeouts, rate limits and fallbacks.","body":"## From demo to product\n\nA demo works once on the developer's laptop. A product works thousands of times a day for people on slow networks, while the AI provider is busy, with users who paste whole novels into the chat box. This lesson covers the engineering that makes the difference.\n\n## Conversation memory\n\nModels do not remember previous messages. Every request must send the conversation so far. But conversations grow, and every message costs tokens. Like a notebook with limited pages, you must decide what to keep:\n\n- Always keep the **system prompt** (the rules).\n- Keep the **most recent** messages, because they matter most.\n- Drop (or summarise) the oldest messages when the budget is exceeded.\n- Never cut a message in half; drop whole turns.\n\n## Retries and exponential backoff\n\nNetworks fail and providers return \"overloaded\" or \"rate limited\" errors (HTTP 429, 500, 503, 529). Retrying immediately makes things worse, like everyone redialling a busy line at once. Wait longer each time: 1 second, then 2, then 4, then 8 (**exponential backoff**), with a little randomness (**jitter**) so many clients do not retry at the same moment. Give up after a few attempts and show a friendly message.\n\nDo **not** retry errors that will never succeed: a bad request (400) or a wrong key (401) needs fixing, not repeating.\n\n## Timeouts\n\nSet a timeout on every call. A request that hangs for two minutes is worse than a clear error after 30 seconds.\n\n## Limits protect you\n\n- **Per-user limits** (messages per day, as CDM Studio does) stop one person, or one bot, from spending your whole budget.\n- **Input limits**: cap the size of what users paste.\n- **Output limits**: set `max_tokens`.\n- **Spending alerts** at the provider.\n\n## Fallbacks\n\nIf the best model is unavailable, fall back to a smaller one, a cached answer, or a non-AI path (\"we could not generate a summary; here is the full text\"). The feature should degrade, not disappear.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `trimHistory(system, messages, budget, count)`: `count(text)` returns a token count. Return the most recent messages (keeping their order) such that `count(system)` plus the counts of the kept messages is within `budget`. Drop whole messages from the oldest end. Messages are `{ role, content }`.\n2. `backoff(attempt, base = 1000, max = 30000)`: the wait in milliseconds before retry number `attempt` (starting at 1): `base × 2^(attempt − 1)`, never more than `max`. (Jitter is left out so it can be checked.)\n3. `shouldRetry(status, attempt, maxAttempts = 4)`: `true` only for statuses 429, 500, 502, 503 or 529, and only while `attempt < maxAttempts`.","task":"Write trimHistory, backoff and shouldRetry.","starter":"<script>\n  // a rough token counter for this lesson: about 4 characters per token\n  const count = (text) => Math.ceil(text.length / 4)\n\n  function trimHistory(system, messages, budget, count) {\n    return messages\n  }\n  function backoff(attempt, base = 1000, max = 30000) {\n  }\n  function shouldRetry(status, attempt, maxAttempts = 4) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"keeps everything when it fits","test":"const m = [{ role: 'user', content: 'aaaa' }, { role: 'assistant', content: 'bbbb' }]; return trimHistory('ssss', m, 3, count).length === 2"},{"label":"drops the oldest whole messages first","test":"const m = [{ role: 'user', content: 'a'.repeat(40) }, { role: 'assistant', content: 'b'.repeat(8) }, { role: 'user', content: 'c'.repeat(8) }]; const t = trimHistory('ssss', m, 6, count); return t.length === 2 && t[0].content[0] === 'b' && t[1].content[0] === 'c'"},{"label":"never exceeds the budget","test":"const m = [{ role: 'user', content: 'x'.repeat(400) }]; return trimHistory('s'.repeat(8), m, 50, count).length === 0"},{"label":"exponential backoff with a cap","test":"return backoff(1) === 1000 && backoff(2) === 2000 && backoff(4) === 8000 && backoff(10) === 30000 && backoff(3, 500) === 2000"},{"label":"retries only temporary errors, a few times","test":"return shouldRetry(429, 1) && shouldRetry(503, 3) && !shouldRetry(503, 4) && !shouldRetry(400, 1) && !shouldRetry(401, 1) && shouldRetry(529, 1)"}],"hint":"trimHistory: start with used = count(system), walk the messages from the end backwards, and stop as soon as the next message would go over the budget; unshift the ones you keep."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$aieng-responsible$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Responsible AI, privacy and your AI career","kind":"prompt","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Use AI in ways people can trust\n\n- **Privacy**: do not paste customers' personal data, salaries or health details into public AI tools without permission and a lawful basis (Nigeria's NDPA 2023, the EU's GDPR). Use business accounts with data protection terms, and remove names where you can.\n- **Bias**: models learn from human text and can repeat unfair patterns, for example in screening CVs or approving credit. Test with varied examples and keep humans responsible for decisions about people.\n- **Accuracy**: an AI answer is a draft. For money, health, legal or safety matters, check it.\n- **Copyright and honesty**: do not pass off AI-generated work as human where that matters (schoolwork, journalism, client deliverables that require it). Say when content was AI-assisted if your audience would care.\n- **Security**: never put API keys in web pages (CDM Studio keeps them on the server with `studio.api`), and treat text from emails and websites as untrusted data that may contain prompt injection.\n\n## AI jobs and how to get them\n\nRoles that use these skills: **AI automation specialist** (builds workflows with Zapier/Make/n8n and APIs), **prompt engineer / AI content designer**, **AI product assistant**, **operations or finance analyst who automates reporting**, and developers who integrate AI into apps.\n\nShow proof:\n\n1. A portfolio of 3 small AI tools you built in CDM Studio (a grounded FAQ bot, an order extractor, an evaluated prompt)\n2. Before/after numbers: *\"cut weekly report time from 3 hours to 20 minutes\"*\n3. Your CDM Studio certificate and, later, provider certificates (Anthropic, Google, Microsoft and AWS all offer AI learning paths)\n\n## Practise\n\nUse the tutor to design an AI workflow for a real job you know. A strong answer lists: the input, the steps, which steps use AI and which need a human check, what data must never be sent to the AI, and how you will measure success.","practice":"Describe a repetitive task in a job you know (for example, answering customer WhatsApp questions about prices, or preparing a weekly sales summary). Ask the AI to design an automated workflow for it, with the steps, which steps need human approval, what personal data must be protected, and how to test it with 5 example cases.","track":"aieng","track_title":"AI engineering: prompts, data and automation","paid":true,"order":11}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$aieng-responsible$cdm$, $cdm$aieng$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"aieng-responsible","title":"Responsible AI, privacy and your AI career","minutes":20,"kind":"prompt","outline":"Bias, privacy, copyright, disclosure, and practise designing an AI workflow for a real job.","body":"## Use AI in ways people can trust\n\n- **Privacy**: do not paste customers' personal data, salaries or health details into public AI tools without permission and a lawful basis (Nigeria's NDPA 2023, the EU's GDPR). Use business accounts with data protection terms, and remove names where you can.\n- **Bias**: models learn from human text and can repeat unfair patterns, for example in screening CVs or approving credit. Test with varied examples and keep humans responsible for decisions about people.\n- **Accuracy**: an AI answer is a draft. For money, health, legal or safety matters, check it.\n- **Copyright and honesty**: do not pass off AI-generated work as human where that matters (schoolwork, journalism, client deliverables that require it). Say when content was AI-assisted if your audience would care.\n- **Security**: never put API keys in web pages (CDM Studio keeps them on the server with `studio.api`), and treat text from emails and websites as untrusted data that may contain prompt injection.\n\n## AI jobs and how to get them\n\nRoles that use these skills: **AI automation specialist** (builds workflows with Zapier/Make/n8n and APIs), **prompt engineer / AI content designer**, **AI product assistant**, **operations or finance analyst who automates reporting**, and developers who integrate AI into apps.\n\nShow proof:\n\n1. A portfolio of 3 small AI tools you built in CDM Studio (a grounded FAQ bot, an order extractor, an evaluated prompt)\n2. Before/after numbers: *\"cut weekly report time from 3 hours to 20 minutes\"*\n3. Your CDM Studio certificate and, later, provider certificates (Anthropic, Google, Microsoft and AWS all offer AI learning paths)\n\n## Practise\n\nUse the tutor to design an AI workflow for a real job you know. A strong answer lists: the input, the steps, which steps use AI and which need a human check, what data must never be sent to the AI, and how you will measure success.","practice":"Describe a repetitive task in a job you know (for example, answering customer WhatsApp questions about prices, or preparing a weekly sales summary). Ask the AI to design an automated workflow for it, with the steps, which steps need human approval, what personal data must be protected, and how to test it with 5 example cases."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
