insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-questions$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"The analyst's job: from questions to decisions","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## Analysts sell decisions, not charts\n\nA shop owner does not want \"a dashboard\". She wants to know **which branch to restock, which product to stop, and whether last month's promo paid off**. The analyst's job is to turn data into a decision someone can act on.\n\n## The process\n\n1. **Ask**: what decision is being made, by whom, and by when?\n2. **Get the data**: POS exports, spreadsheets, bank statements, forms. Know where each number comes from.\n3. **Clean**: fix types, duplicates, blanks and mistakes (most of the work).\n4. **Analyse**: summarise, compare, look for trends and outliers.\n5. **Share**: a short story with one clear message, the numbers that prove it, and a recommendation.\n6. **Act and measure**: did the decision work?\n\n## Four kinds of analysis\n\n- **Descriptive**: what happened? *Sales were ₦4.2m in October.*\n- **Diagnostic**: why? *Garki fell because the generator broke for a week.*\n- **Predictive**: what will happen? *December will be about 30% above October.*\n- **Prescriptive**: what should we do? *Stock 40% more rice in Wuse before the holidays.*\n\n## Good questions are SMART\n\nSpecific, measurable, action-oriented, relevant and time-bound. Weak: *\"How are sales?\"* Strong: *\"Which 5 products lost the most revenue in Q3 compared with Q2, by branch, so we can decide what to reorder for Q4?\"*\n\n## Tools\n\nSpreadsheets (Excel, Google Sheets) take you far. Then SQL to pull data from databases, a BI tool (Power BI, Looker Studio, Tableau) for dashboards, and Python for heavier work. CDM Studio's **Data** page lets you practise on your own files right now. In this course you will build the core calculations in code, so the logic behind every tool is clear.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":false,"order":0}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-cleaning$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Cleaning messy data","kind":"code","task":"Write toNumber, tidyName and dedupe.","hint":"toNumber: if (typeof text === 'number') return text; const s = String(text).trim().replace(/^[₦N]/, '').replace(/[,\\s]/g, ''); if (s === '') return null; const n = Number(s); return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : null","body":"## Real data is messy\n\nA POS export or a sheet many people type into usually has:\n\n- Money as text: `\"₦1,200\"`, `\"1200 \"`, `\"N1,200.50\"`\n- Names typed differently: `\"Indomie\"`, `\" indomie\"`, `\"INDOMIE \"`\n- Duplicate rows from exporting twice\n- Blank or impossible values (a negative quantity, a date in 2062)\n\nIf you sum before cleaning, the totals are simply wrong.\n\n## Rules of thumb\n\n1. **Keep the raw file** untouched; clean a copy.\n2. **Make each column one type**: numbers as numbers, dates as dates.\n3. **Standardise text**: trim spaces, consistent capitals, one name per thing.\n4. **Remove exact duplicates**, but check: two identical sales might be real.\n5. **Write down every rule** you applied, so others can trust the result.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `toNumber(text)`: remove `₦`, `N` at the start, commas and spaces, then convert. Return `null` if it is not a valid number. Real numbers pass through unchanged.\n2. `tidyName(text)`: trim, collapse inner spaces, and make it Title Case (`\" INDOMIE  noodles\"` → `\"Indomie Noodles\"`).\n3. `dedupe(rows)`: remove rows that are exactly the same as an earlier row (compare with `JSON.stringify`), keeping the first.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":false,"order":1}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-stats$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Descriptive statistics that matter","kind":"code","task":"Write median, percentile and outliers.","hint":"percentile: const s = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b); const pos = p * (s.length - 1); const lo = Math.floor(pos), hi = Math.ceil(pos); return s[lo] + (s[hi] - s[lo]) * (pos - lo). median is percentile(values, 0.5).","body":"## Average is not enough\n\nIf 9 customers spend ₦5,000 and one spends ₦500,000, the **mean** is ₦54,500, which describes nobody. Use the right summary:\n\n- **Mean**: sum ÷ count. Good when values are fairly even.\n- **Median**: the middle value when sorted (average of the two middle ones for an even count). Not pulled by extremes: ₦5,000 above.\n- **Percentiles**: the 90th percentile is the value 90% of data is below. Useful for delivery times: *\"90% of orders arrive within 2 days.\"*\n- **Spread**: range (max − min), standard deviation, and the **interquartile range (IQR)** = Q3 − Q1 (the middle 50%).\n\n## Outliers\n\nValues far from the rest may be **errors** (an extra zero typed) or **important** (a big new customer). A common rule: a value is an outlier if it is below **Q1 − 1.5 × IQR** or above **Q3 + 1.5 × IQR**. Always check outliers before deleting them.\n\n## Percentile method\n\nMany tools use linear interpolation: sort the values; position = p × (n − 1); if it is not a whole number, take the value between the two neighbours in proportion. For p = 0.5 this gives the median.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `median(values)`\n2. `percentile(values, p)` with the interpolation above (p between 0 and 1)\n3. `outliers(values)`: the values outside Q1 − 1.5×IQR and Q3 + 1.5×IQR, in their original order (Q1 = 25th percentile, Q3 = 75th).\n\nDo not change the input array (sort a copy).","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":2}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-stats$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-stats","title":"Descriptive statistics that matter","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Mean, median, percentiles and spotting outliers with the IQR rule.","body":"## Average is not enough\n\nIf 9 customers spend ₦5,000 and one spends ₦500,000, the **mean** is ₦54,500, which describes nobody. Use the right summary:\n\n- **Mean**: sum ÷ count. Good when values are fairly even.\n- **Median**: the middle value when sorted (average of the two middle ones for an even count). Not pulled by extremes: ₦5,000 above.\n- **Percentiles**: the 90th percentile is the value 90% of data is below. Useful for delivery times: *\"90% of orders arrive within 2 days.\"*\n- **Spread**: range (max − min), standard deviation, and the **interquartile range (IQR)** = Q3 − Q1 (the middle 50%).\n\n## Outliers\n\nValues far from the rest may be **errors** (an extra zero typed) or **important** (a big new customer). A common rule: a value is an outlier if it is below **Q1 − 1.5 × IQR** or above **Q3 + 1.5 × IQR**. Always check outliers before deleting them.\n\n## Percentile method\n\nMany tools use linear interpolation: sort the values; position = p × (n − 1); if it is not a whole number, take the value between the two neighbours in proportion. For p = 0.5 this gives the median.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `median(values)`\n2. `percentile(values, p)` with the interpolation above (p between 0 and 1)\n3. `outliers(values)`: the values outside Q1 − 1.5×IQR and Q3 + 1.5×IQR, in their original order (Q1 = 25th percentile, Q3 = 75th).\n\nDo not change the input array (sort a copy).","task":"Write median, percentile and outliers.","starter":"<script>\n  function median(values) {\n  }\n  function percentile(values, p) {\n  }\n  function outliers(values) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"median of an odd count","test":"return median([5, 1, 3]) === 3"},{"label":"median of an even count","test":"return median([4, 1, 3, 2]) === 2.5"},{"label":"percentile interpolates","test":"return Math.abs(percentile([10, 20, 30, 40], 0.25) - 17.5) < 1e-9 && Math.abs(percentile([10, 20, 30, 40], 0.9) - 37) < 1e-9"},{"label":"outliers found with the IQR rule","test":"return JSON.stringify(outliers([5000, 5200, 4800, 500000, 5100, 4900, 5050])) === '[500000]'"},{"label":"input is not changed","test":"const a = [3, 1, 2]; median(a); percentile(a, 0.5); return a.join() === '3,1,2'"}],"hint":"percentile: const s = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b); const pos = p * (s.length - 1); const lo = Math.floor(pos), hi = Math.ceil(pos); return s[lo] + (s[hi] - s[lo]) * (pos - lo). median is percentile(values, 0.5)."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-pivot$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Pivot tables: grouping and summarising","kind":"code","task":"Write pivot and totals.","hint":"pivot: const out = {}; for (const r of rows) { const a = r[rowKey], b = r[colKey]; out[a] = out[a] || {}; out[a][b] = (out[a][b] || 0) + r[valueKey] }","body":"## The most useful spreadsheet tool\n\nA **pivot table** answers questions like *\"total sales by branch and by month\"* in seconds. It:\n\n1. Groups rows by one category (rows of the pivot), and optionally a second (columns)\n2. Applies a measure to each group (sum, count, average)\n3. Adds totals\n\n## Shares tell the story\n\nAbsolute numbers are hard to compare; **shares** are easier: *\"Wuse is 38% of all sales.\"* Share = group total ÷ grand total.\n\n## Your task\n\nSales rows look like `{ branch, month, amount }`.\n\n1. `pivot(rows, rowKey, colKey, valueKey)`: return an object where `result[row][col]` is the **sum** of `valueKey` for that pair. Missing pairs are simply absent.\n2. `totals(rows, key, valueKey)`: an array of `{ name, total, share }` for each value of `key`, sorted by total, highest first. `share` is a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":3}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-pivot$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-pivot","title":"Pivot tables: grouping and summarising","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Build the logic of a pivot table: totals by row and column categories, plus share of total.","body":"## The most useful spreadsheet tool\n\nA **pivot table** answers questions like *\"total sales by branch and by month\"* in seconds. It:\n\n1. Groups rows by one category (rows of the pivot), and optionally a second (columns)\n2. Applies a measure to each group (sum, count, average)\n3. Adds totals\n\n## Shares tell the story\n\nAbsolute numbers are hard to compare; **shares** are easier: *\"Wuse is 38% of all sales.\"* Share = group total ÷ grand total.\n\n## Your task\n\nSales rows look like `{ branch, month, amount }`.\n\n1. `pivot(rows, rowKey, colKey, valueKey)`: return an object where `result[row][col]` is the **sum** of `valueKey` for that pair. Missing pairs are simply absent.\n2. `totals(rows, key, valueKey)`: an array of `{ name, total, share }` for each value of `key`, sorted by total, highest first. `share` is a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place.","task":"Write pivot and totals.","starter":"<script>\n  const SALES = [\n    { branch: 'Wuse', month: 'Sep', amount: 400 },\n    { branch: 'Wuse', month: 'Oct', amount: 600 },\n    { branch: 'Garki', month: 'Sep', amount: 300 },\n    { branch: 'Garki', month: 'Sep', amount: 100 },\n    { branch: 'Kubwa', month: 'Oct', amount: 200 },\n  ]\n  function pivot(rows, rowKey, colKey, valueKey) {\n  }\n  function totals(rows, key, valueKey) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"pivot sums each pair","test":"const p = pivot(SALES, 'branch', 'month', 'amount'); return p.Wuse.Sep === 400 && p.Wuse.Oct === 600 && p.Garki.Sep === 400"},{"label":"missing pairs are absent","test":"const p = pivot(SALES, 'branch', 'month', 'amount'); return p.Garki.Oct === undefined && p.Kubwa.Sep === undefined"},{"label":"totals sorted highest first","test":"return totals(SALES, 'branch', 'amount').map(t => t.name).join() === 'Wuse,Garki,Kubwa'"},{"label":"shares are percentages to 1 decimal","test":"const t = totals(SALES, 'branch', 'amount'); return t[0].share === 62.5 && t[2].share === 12.5"}],"hint":"pivot: const out = {}; for (const r of rows) { const a = r[rowKey], b = r[colKey]; out[a] = out[a] || {}; out[a][b] = (out[a][b] || 0) + r[valueKey] }"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-trends$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Trends: growth, moving averages and seasonality","kind":"code","task":"Write growth, movingAverage and cagr.","hint":"growth: values.slice(1).map((v, i) => values[i] === 0 ? null : Math.round((v - values[i]) / values[i] * 1000) / 10)","body":"## Is it really growing?\n\nDaily or weekly numbers bounce around. To see the real direction:\n\n- **Period growth**: (this − last) ÷ last × 100. October ₦4.2m vs September ₦3.5m is **+20%**.\n- **Year-on-year (YoY)**: compare with the same period last year, to remove **seasonality** (December is always busy; school fees peak in September and January).\n- **Moving average**: the average of the last n periods, recomputed each period. A 7-day moving average removes the weekday/weekend pattern.\n- **CAGR** (compound annual growth rate): the steady yearly rate that turns a start value into an end value over n years: `(end ÷ start)^(1/n) − 1`.\n\n## Careful with percentages\n\n- Growth from a tiny base looks huge: ₦10k → ₦30k is +200% but only ₦20k.\n- A fall of 50% needs a rise of 100% to recover.\n- Inflation: in a high-inflation year, sales up 20% in naira may be flat or down in real terms. Compare with inflation or with units sold.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `growth(values)`: an array of period-on-period growth percentages rounded to 1 decimal, starting from the second value (use `null` when the previous value is 0).\n2. `movingAverage(values, n)`: for each position from `n - 1` onward, the average of the last n values (so the result has `values.length - n + 1` items).\n3. `cagr(start, end, years)`: as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":4}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-trends$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-trends","title":"Trends: growth, moving averages and seasonality","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Period-on-period growth, compound growth and smoothing noisy daily sales with a moving average.","body":"## Is it really growing?\n\nDaily or weekly numbers bounce around. To see the real direction:\n\n- **Period growth**: (this − last) ÷ last × 100. October ₦4.2m vs September ₦3.5m is **+20%**.\n- **Year-on-year (YoY)**: compare with the same period last year, to remove **seasonality** (December is always busy; school fees peak in September and January).\n- **Moving average**: the average of the last n periods, recomputed each period. A 7-day moving average removes the weekday/weekend pattern.\n- **CAGR** (compound annual growth rate): the steady yearly rate that turns a start value into an end value over n years: `(end ÷ start)^(1/n) − 1`.\n\n## Careful with percentages\n\n- Growth from a tiny base looks huge: ₦10k → ₦30k is +200% but only ₦20k.\n- A fall of 50% needs a rise of 100% to recover.\n- Inflation: in a high-inflation year, sales up 20% in naira may be flat or down in real terms. Compare with inflation or with units sold.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `growth(values)`: an array of period-on-period growth percentages rounded to 1 decimal, starting from the second value (use `null` when the previous value is 0).\n2. `movingAverage(values, n)`: for each position from `n - 1` onward, the average of the last n values (so the result has `values.length - n + 1` items).\n3. `cagr(start, end, years)`: as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal.","task":"Write growth, movingAverage and cagr.","starter":"<script>\n  function growth(values) {\n  }\n  function movingAverage(values, n) {\n  }\n  function cagr(start, end, years) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"growth period on period","test":"return JSON.stringify(growth([100, 120, 90, 90])) === '[20,-25,0]'"},{"label":"growth from zero is null","test":"return JSON.stringify(growth([0, 50])) === '[null]'"},{"label":"3-period moving average","test":"return JSON.stringify(movingAverage([3, 6, 9, 12, 3], 3)) === '[6,9,8]'"},{"label":"CAGR","test":"return cagr(1000000, 2000000, 3) === 26 && cagr(100, 100, 5) === 0"}],"hint":"growth: values.slice(1).map((v, i) => values[i] === 0 ? null : Math.round((v - values[i]) / values[i] * 1000) / 10)"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-kpis$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"KPIs and dashboards","kind":"code","task":"Write avgBasket, grossMargin, repeatRate and stockCover.","hint":"grossMargin: const r = sum of revenue, c = sum of cost; Math.round((r - c) / r * 1000) / 10. repeatRate: count orders per customer in an object.","body":"## Measure what drives the business\n\nA **KPI** (key performance indicator) is a number that tells you if the business is on track. Good KPIs are few, clear, owned by someone, and tied to a target. Examples for a shop:\n\n- **Revenue** and **gross margin %** = (revenue − cost of goods) ÷ revenue\n- **Average basket (order value)** = revenue ÷ number of orders\n- **Repeat customer rate** = customers with 2+ orders ÷ all customers\n- **Stock cover (days)** = stock on hand ÷ average daily units sold: *how many days until we run out?*\n- **Conversion rate** for online shops: orders ÷ visitors\n\n## Dashboard design\n\n- Put the 3 to 5 most important numbers at the top, each with its **comparison** (vs last month, vs target)\n- One chart per question; bars for comparing categories, lines for time\n- Same scales and colours everywhere; red only for real problems\n- Update automatically if you can; a dashboard nobody trusts is worse than none\n\n## Your task\n\nOrders look like `{ customer, revenue, cost }`.\n\n1. `avgBasket(orders)`: revenue ÷ orders, rounded to whole naira.\n2. `grossMargin(orders)`: percentage rounded to 1 decimal.\n3. `repeatRate(orders)`: percentage of distinct customers with 2 or more orders, rounded to 1 decimal.\n4. `stockCover(stock, unitsSoldLast30Days)`: days of cover rounded down; `Infinity` if nothing sold.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":5}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-kpis$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-kpis","title":"KPIs and dashboards","minutes":25,"kind":"code","outline":"Choose the right KPIs and compute average basket, repeat rate, gross margin and stock cover.","body":"## Measure what drives the business\n\nA **KPI** (key performance indicator) is a number that tells you if the business is on track. Good KPIs are few, clear, owned by someone, and tied to a target. Examples for a shop:\n\n- **Revenue** and **gross margin %** = (revenue − cost of goods) ÷ revenue\n- **Average basket (order value)** = revenue ÷ number of orders\n- **Repeat customer rate** = customers with 2+ orders ÷ all customers\n- **Stock cover (days)** = stock on hand ÷ average daily units sold: *how many days until we run out?*\n- **Conversion rate** for online shops: orders ÷ visitors\n\n## Dashboard design\n\n- Put the 3 to 5 most important numbers at the top, each with its **comparison** (vs last month, vs target)\n- One chart per question; bars for comparing categories, lines for time\n- Same scales and colours everywhere; red only for real problems\n- Update automatically if you can; a dashboard nobody trusts is worse than none\n\n## Your task\n\nOrders look like `{ customer, revenue, cost }`.\n\n1. `avgBasket(orders)`: revenue ÷ orders, rounded to whole naira.\n2. `grossMargin(orders)`: percentage rounded to 1 decimal.\n3. `repeatRate(orders)`: percentage of distinct customers with 2 or more orders, rounded to 1 decimal.\n4. `stockCover(stock, unitsSoldLast30Days)`: days of cover rounded down; `Infinity` if nothing sold.","task":"Write avgBasket, grossMargin, repeatRate and stockCover.","starter":"<script>\n  const ORDERS = [\n    { customer: 'Ada', revenue: 5000, cost: 3500 },\n    { customer: 'Bayo', revenue: 12000, cost: 9000 },\n    { customer: 'Ada', revenue: 3000, cost: 2000 },\n    { customer: 'Chi', revenue: 8000, cost: 5500 },\n  ]\n  function avgBasket(orders) {\n  }\n  function grossMargin(orders) {\n  }\n  function repeatRate(orders) {\n  }\n  function stockCover(stock, unitsSoldLast30Days) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"average basket","test":"return avgBasket(ORDERS) === 7000"},{"label":"gross margin %","test":"return grossMargin(ORDERS) === 28.6"},{"label":"repeat customer rate","test":"return repeatRate(ORDERS) === 33.3"},{"label":"stock cover in days","test":"return stockCover(100, 60) === 50 && stockCover(10, 0) === Infinity"}],"hint":"grossMargin: const r = sum of revenue, c = sum of cost; Math.round((r - c) / r * 1000) / 10. repeatRate: count orders per customer in an object."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-sql$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Getting data with SQL","kind":"code","task":"Revenue, gross profit and margin for each branch and category.","hint":"round(sum(qty * (unit_price - unit_cost)) * 100.0 / sum(qty * unit_price), 1) as margin_pct … group by branch, category order by branch, revenue desc","body":"## Spreadsheets run out of room\n\nSpreadsheets are wonderful until the data reaches hundreds of thousands of rows, lives in the company's systems, or changes every hour. Then analysts go to the source: the **database**, and the language for asking it questions is **SQL**. Most analyst job adverts list SQL as a must-have skill. (The **SQL and databases** course goes deep; this lesson gives you the analyst's core.)\n\n## The analyst's query shape\n\nNearly every report query follows the same shape. Read it like a recipe:\n\n```sql\nselect   branch,                                   -- the groups you want\n         sum(qty * unit_price) as revenue,          -- the measures\n         sum(qty * (unit_price - unit_cost)) as gross_profit\nfrom     sales                                     -- the table\nwhere    sale_date >= '2026-10-01'                 -- the rows that count\ngroup by branch                                    -- one row per group\norder by revenue desc;                             -- the order to read it in\n```\n\nIt is the same idea as the pivot table lesson: choose rows, group them, summarise a measure. SQL simply does it on the full data, every time, the same way.\n\n## Calculated measures\n\n- **Revenue** = quantity × price.\n- **Gross profit** = quantity × (price − cost).\n- **Gross margin %** = gross profit ÷ revenue × 100. Calculate it from the **totals** (`sum(profit) / sum(revenue)`), not by averaging the margins of individual rows, which gives small sales the same weight as big ones.\n\nMultiply by `100.0` (with a decimal point) so the division keeps its decimals.\n\n## From question to query\n\n*\"Which category makes us the most money in each branch, and at what margin?\"*\n\n1. The groups: branch and category.\n2. The measures: revenue, profit, margin.\n3. The filter: none (all time) or a date range.\n4. The order: by branch, then revenue.\n\nWrite the question as a comment at the top of the query. Future you (and your manager) will thank you.\n\n## Check your numbers\n\nBefore sharing, sanity-check: does the total revenue match the finance report? Are there negative quantities or zero prices? Do the group totals add up to the grand total?\n\n## The table\n\n`sales` has: id, sale_date, branch (Wuse, Garki, Maitama), product, category (Food, Electronics), qty, unit_price, unit_cost.\n\n## Your task\n\nShow, for each `branch` and `category`: `revenue`, `gross_profit` and `margin_pct` (gross profit ÷ revenue × 100, rounded to 1 decimal). Columns in that order: branch, category, revenue, gross_profit, margin_pct. Sort by branch, then by revenue highest first.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":6}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-sql$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-sql","title":"Getting data with SQL","minutes":35,"kind":"code","lang":"sql","setup":"\ncreate table sales (id integer primary key, sale_date text not null, branch text not null, product text not null, category text not null, qty integer not null, unit_price integer not null, unit_cost integer not null);\ninsert into sales values\n (1, '2026-09-03', 'Wuse', 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 2, 72000, 61000), (2, '2026-09-03', 'Garki', 'Bread', 'Food', 30, 1200, 800),\n (3, '2026-09-10', 'Wuse', 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 3, 18000, 13500), (4, '2026-09-17', 'Maitama', 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 4, 9500, 6000),\n (5, '2026-09-24', 'Garki', 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 6, 15500, 12800), (6, '2026-10-01', 'Wuse', 'Bread', 'Food', 45, 1200, 800),\n (7, '2026-10-08', 'Maitama', 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 1, 72000, 61000), (8, '2026-10-15', 'Garki', 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 2, 18000, 13500),\n (9, '2026-10-22', 'Wuse', 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 6, 9500, 6000), (10, '2026-10-29', 'Maitama', 'Bread', 'Food', 25, 1200, 800),\n (11, '2026-11-05', 'Garki', 'Rice 50kg', 'Food', 3, 72000, 61000), (12, '2026-11-12', 'Wuse', 'Vegetable oil 5L', 'Food', 8, 15500, 12800),\n (13, '2026-11-12', 'Maitama', 'Power bank', 'Electronics', 5, 18000, 13500), (14, '2026-11-19', 'Garki', 'Earbuds', 'Electronics', 3, 9500, 6000);\n","outline":"Why analysts use SQL, revenue and margin by group, and turning a business question into a query.","body":"## Spreadsheets run out of room\n\nSpreadsheets are wonderful until the data reaches hundreds of thousands of rows, lives in the company's systems, or changes every hour. Then analysts go to the source: the **database**, and the language for asking it questions is **SQL**. Most analyst job adverts list SQL as a must-have skill. (The **SQL and databases** course goes deep; this lesson gives you the analyst's core.)\n\n## The analyst's query shape\n\nNearly every report query follows the same shape. Read it like a recipe:\n\n```sql\nselect   branch,                                   -- the groups you want\n         sum(qty * unit_price) as revenue,          -- the measures\n         sum(qty * (unit_price - unit_cost)) as gross_profit\nfrom     sales                                     -- the table\nwhere    sale_date >= '2026-10-01'                 -- the rows that count\ngroup by branch                                    -- one row per group\norder by revenue desc;                             -- the order to read it in\n```\n\nIt is the same idea as the pivot table lesson: choose rows, group them, summarise a measure. SQL simply does it on the full data, every time, the same way.\n\n## Calculated measures\n\n- **Revenue** = quantity × price.\n- **Gross profit** = quantity × (price − cost).\n- **Gross margin %** = gross profit ÷ revenue × 100. Calculate it from the **totals** (`sum(profit) / sum(revenue)`), not by averaging the margins of individual rows, which gives small sales the same weight as big ones.\n\nMultiply by `100.0` (with a decimal point) so the division keeps its decimals.\n\n## From question to query\n\n*\"Which category makes us the most money in each branch, and at what margin?\"*\n\n1. The groups: branch and category.\n2. The measures: revenue, profit, margin.\n3. The filter: none (all time) or a date range.\n4. The order: by branch, then revenue.\n\nWrite the question as a comment at the top of the query. Future you (and your manager) will thank you.\n\n## Check your numbers\n\nBefore sharing, sanity-check: does the total revenue match the finance report? Are there negative quantities or zero prices? Do the group totals add up to the grand total?\n\n## The table\n\n`sales` has: id, sale_date, branch (Wuse, Garki, Maitama), product, category (Food, Electronics), qty, unit_price, unit_cost.\n\n## Your task\n\nShow, for each `branch` and `category`: `revenue`, `gross_profit` and `margin_pct` (gross profit ÷ revenue × 100, rounded to 1 decimal). Columns in that order: branch, category, revenue, gross_profit, margin_pct. Sort by branch, then by revenue highest first.","task":"Revenue, gross profit and margin for each branch and category.","starter":"-- Which category makes the most money in each branch, and at what margin?\nselect * from sales;\n","checks":[{"label":"branch, category, revenue, gross_profit and margin_pct in order","test":"return await same(\"select branch, category, sum(qty * unit_price), sum(qty * (unit_price - unit_cost)), round(sum(qty * (unit_price - unit_cost)) * 100.0 / sum(qty * unit_price), 1) from sales group by branch, category order by branch, sum(qty * unit_price) desc\", true)"}],"hint":"round(sum(qty * (unit_price - unit_cost)) * 100.0 / sum(qty * unit_price), 1) as margin_pct … group by branch, category order by branch, revenue desc"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-charts$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Choosing the right chart","kind":"read","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## A chart answers one question\n\nA chart is a picture of an answer. If you cannot say the question in one sentence, the chart will confuse. Start from the question, then choose the chart that answers it most directly, the way you choose a tool from a toolbox: a hammer for nails, a screwdriver for screws.\n\n## The main chart types\n\n| Question | Best chart |\n|---|---|\n| How does it change over time? | **Line chart** (time on the horizontal axis) |\n| Which is bigger? Comparing categories | **Bar chart** (horizontal bars when labels are long) |\n| What is the share of a whole? | **Stacked bar** or a single **100% bar**; a pie only for 2 or 3 slices |\n| How are values spread? | **Histogram** or **box plot** |\n| Do two measures move together? | **Scatter plot** |\n| One key number | A big **number tile** with a comparison (\"₦4.2m, +12% vs last month\") |\n| Exact values to look up | A **table** |\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- **Pie charts with many slices**: people cannot compare angles well. Use a sorted bar chart instead.\n- **Bar charts that do not start at zero**: a bar's length is its value, so cutting the axis makes small differences look huge. (Line charts may zoom in, if labelled clearly.)\n- **3D effects, shadows and gradients**: they distort sizes and add nothing.\n- **Too many colours**: every colour should mean something.\n- **Unsorted bars**: sort by value so the order tells the story.\n- **Two different y-axes** on one chart: easy to mislead, hard to read.\n\n## Honest charts\n\nCharts persuade, so they carry responsibility. Show the full context: the time range, the units, the source, and whether numbers are adjusted for inflation. Cherry-picking a start date to make growth look bigger is a lie told with pictures.\n\n## Colour with purpose\n\nUse one neutral colour (grey) for most of the data and a strong colour to **highlight** what matters: the branch you are talking about, this year compared with last. Keep the same colour for the same thing across all charts in a report. Check that the chart still works for colour-blind readers (do not rely on red versus green alone).\n\n## Titles that say the answer\n\n\"Revenue by month\" describes the chart. \"Revenue fell 18% after the price rise in August\" says the insight. Use the second kind, then add a short **annotation** on the chart pointing at the moment that matters.\n\n## Dashboard tools\n\nPower BI, Tableau, Looker Studio and Metabase build interactive charts from your data; CDM Studio's Data page lets you practise. The principles are the same in every tool.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":7}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-charts$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-charts","title":"Choosing the right chart","minutes":25,"kind":"read","outline":"Matching charts to questions, common chart mistakes, honest axes, colour and annotation.","body":"## A chart answers one question\n\nA chart is a picture of an answer. If you cannot say the question in one sentence, the chart will confuse. Start from the question, then choose the chart that answers it most directly, the way you choose a tool from a toolbox: a hammer for nails, a screwdriver for screws.\n\n## The main chart types\n\n| Question | Best chart |\n|---|---|\n| How does it change over time? | **Line chart** (time on the horizontal axis) |\n| Which is bigger? Comparing categories | **Bar chart** (horizontal bars when labels are long) |\n| What is the share of a whole? | **Stacked bar** or a single **100% bar**; a pie only for 2 or 3 slices |\n| How are values spread? | **Histogram** or **box plot** |\n| Do two measures move together? | **Scatter plot** |\n| One key number | A big **number tile** with a comparison (\"₦4.2m, +12% vs last month\") |\n| Exact values to look up | A **table** |\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- **Pie charts with many slices**: people cannot compare angles well. Use a sorted bar chart instead.\n- **Bar charts that do not start at zero**: a bar's length is its value, so cutting the axis makes small differences look huge. (Line charts may zoom in, if labelled clearly.)\n- **3D effects, shadows and gradients**: they distort sizes and add nothing.\n- **Too many colours**: every colour should mean something.\n- **Unsorted bars**: sort by value so the order tells the story.\n- **Two different y-axes** on one chart: easy to mislead, hard to read.\n\n## Honest charts\n\nCharts persuade, so they carry responsibility. Show the full context: the time range, the units, the source, and whether numbers are adjusted for inflation. Cherry-picking a start date to make growth look bigger is a lie told with pictures.\n\n## Colour with purpose\n\nUse one neutral colour (grey) for most of the data and a strong colour to **highlight** what matters: the branch you are talking about, this year compared with last. Keep the same colour for the same thing across all charts in a report. Check that the chart still works for colour-blind readers (do not rely on red versus green alone).\n\n## Titles that say the answer\n\n\"Revenue by month\" describes the chart. \"Revenue fell 18% after the price rise in August\" says the insight. Use the second kind, then add a short **annotation** on the chart pointing at the moment that matters.\n\n## Dashboard tools\n\nPower BI, Tableau, Looker Studio and Metabase build interactive charts from your data; CDM Studio's Data page lets you practise. The principles are the same in every tool.","quiz":[{"q":"Monthly sales over two years are best shown as…","options":["A pie chart","A line chart","A table only"],"answer":1,"why":"Lines show change over time."},{"q":"A bar chart whose axis starts at 900 instead of 0…","options":["Is always fine","Exaggerates small differences","Is required for big numbers"],"answer":1,"why":"Bar length must reflect value."},{"q":"Comparing 9 product categories, the best choice is…","options":["A 9-slice pie","A sorted bar chart","A 3D pie"],"answer":1,"why":"People compare lengths better than angles."},{"q":"The strongest chart title is…","options":["\"Chart 3\"","\"Revenue by month\"","\"Revenue fell 18% after the August price rise\""],"answer":2,"why":"Titles should state the insight."}]}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-ab$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"A/B tests: did the change really work?","kind":"code","task":"Write rate, lift, zScore and significant.","hint":"zScore: const p = (a.conversions + b.conversions) / (a.visitors + b.visitors); const se = Math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) * (1 / a.visitors + 1 / b.visitors)); return Math.round((rate(b) - rate(a)) / se * 100) / 100","body":"## The coin that lands heads 6 times\n\nToss a fair coin 10 times and you might get 6 heads. That does not mean the coin is unfair: small samples wobble. Business numbers wobble too. If a new checkout page converts 5.4% of visitors and the old one 5.0%, is the new page better, or is it a lucky week?\n\nAn **A/B test** answers this. Visitors are split **at random**: half see version A (the control), half see version B (the change). Randomness is the key: it makes the two groups alike in every way except the change, so a real difference can be credited to the change.\n\n## The numbers\n\n- **Conversion rate** = conversions ÷ visitors.\n- **Lift** = (rate B − rate A) ÷ rate A. From 5.0% to 5.4% is a lift of +8%.\n\n## Is the difference real? The z-test\n\nThe **two-proportion z-test** asks: *if A and B were truly the same, how surprising would a difference this big be?*\n\n1. The pooled rate: `p = (convA + convB) / (nA + nB)`\n2. The standard error: `se = sqrt( p × (1 − p) × (1/nA + 1/nB) )`\n3. The z score: `z = (rateB − rateA) / se`\n\nA z score beyond about **±1.96** means a difference this large would happen by chance less than 5% of the time if there were no real effect. That 5% is the **p-value threshold** usually used: we call the result **statistically significant at 95%**.\n\n## Reading results honestly\n\n- **Decide the sample size and the duration before starting**, and do not stop the test early the moment it looks good. Peeking repeatedly and stopping when you like the result produces false winners.\n- Run for **whole weeks**, because weekday and weekend visitors behave differently.\n- **Significant is not the same as important**: a tiny lift can be significant with huge traffic but not worth the cost.\n- **Not significant does not mean no effect**: maybe the test was too small to detect it.\n- Change **one thing** at a time, or you will not know what caused the result.\n- Check the **guardrail metrics**: a pop-up may raise sign-ups but also raise complaints or refunds.\n\n## Beyond websites\n\nThe same thinking works for SMS reminders (does a reminder increase fee payments?), price tests, store layouts and training programmes. Wherever you can randomise, you can test.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `rate(conversions, visitors)`: conversions ÷ visitors (`0` when visitors is 0).\n2. `lift(a, b)`: `a` and `b` are `{ visitors, conversions }`. Return the lift of B over A as a **percentage** rounded to 1 decimal.\n3. `zScore(a, b)`: the two-proportion z score, rounded to 2 decimals.\n4. `significant(a, b)`: `true` when the absolute z score is at least 1.96.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":8}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-ab$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-ab","title":"A/B tests: did the change really work?","minutes":35,"kind":"code","outline":"Conversion rates, lift, random variation, a two-proportion z-test and how to read p-values honestly.","body":"## The coin that lands heads 6 times\n\nToss a fair coin 10 times and you might get 6 heads. That does not mean the coin is unfair: small samples wobble. Business numbers wobble too. If a new checkout page converts 5.4% of visitors and the old one 5.0%, is the new page better, or is it a lucky week?\n\nAn **A/B test** answers this. Visitors are split **at random**: half see version A (the control), half see version B (the change). Randomness is the key: it makes the two groups alike in every way except the change, so a real difference can be credited to the change.\n\n## The numbers\n\n- **Conversion rate** = conversions ÷ visitors.\n- **Lift** = (rate B − rate A) ÷ rate A. From 5.0% to 5.4% is a lift of +8%.\n\n## Is the difference real? The z-test\n\nThe **two-proportion z-test** asks: *if A and B were truly the same, how surprising would a difference this big be?*\n\n1. The pooled rate: `p = (convA + convB) / (nA + nB)`\n2. The standard error: `se = sqrt( p × (1 − p) × (1/nA + 1/nB) )`\n3. The z score: `z = (rateB − rateA) / se`\n\nA z score beyond about **±1.96** means a difference this large would happen by chance less than 5% of the time if there were no real effect. That 5% is the **p-value threshold** usually used: we call the result **statistically significant at 95%**.\n\n## Reading results honestly\n\n- **Decide the sample size and the duration before starting**, and do not stop the test early the moment it looks good. Peeking repeatedly and stopping when you like the result produces false winners.\n- Run for **whole weeks**, because weekday and weekend visitors behave differently.\n- **Significant is not the same as important**: a tiny lift can be significant with huge traffic but not worth the cost.\n- **Not significant does not mean no effect**: maybe the test was too small to detect it.\n- Change **one thing** at a time, or you will not know what caused the result.\n- Check the **guardrail metrics**: a pop-up may raise sign-ups but also raise complaints or refunds.\n\n## Beyond websites\n\nThe same thinking works for SMS reminders (does a reminder increase fee payments?), price tests, store layouts and training programmes. Wherever you can randomise, you can test.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `rate(conversions, visitors)`: conversions ÷ visitors (`0` when visitors is 0).\n2. `lift(a, b)`: `a` and `b` are `{ visitors, conversions }`. Return the lift of B over A as a **percentage** rounded to 1 decimal.\n3. `zScore(a, b)`: the two-proportion z score, rounded to 2 decimals.\n4. `significant(a, b)`: `true` when the absolute z score is at least 1.96.","task":"Write rate, lift, zScore and significant.","starter":"<script>\n  function rate(conversions, visitors) {\n  }\n  function lift(a, b) {\n  }\n  function zScore(a, b) {\n  }\n  function significant(a, b) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"conversion rate","test":"return rate(50, 1000) === 0.05 && rate(3, 0) === 0"},{"label":"lift as a percentage","test":"return lift({ visitors: 1000, conversions: 50 }, { visitors: 1000, conversions: 54 }) === 8 && lift({ visitors: 200, conversions: 20 }, { visitors: 200, conversions: 15 }) === -25"},{"label":"z score for two proportions","test":"return zScore({ visitors: 5000, conversions: 250 }, { visitors: 5000, conversions: 300 }) === 2.19 && zScore({ visitors: 1000, conversions: 50 }, { visitors: 1000, conversions: 54 }) === 0.4"},{"label":"significance at 95%","test":"return significant({ visitors: 5000, conversions: 250 }, { visitors: 5000, conversions: 300 }) === true && significant({ visitors: 1000, conversions: 50 }, { visitors: 1000, conversions: 54 }) === false"}],"hint":"zScore: const p = (a.conversions + b.conversions) / (a.visitors + b.visitors); const se = Math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) * (1 / a.visitors + 1 / b.visitors)); return Math.round((rate(b) - rate(a)) / se * 100) / 100"}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-cohorts$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Customers over time: cohorts and retention","kind":"code","task":"Write monthIndex, cohorts, retention and repeatRate.","hint":"First find each customer's earliest date (dates as YYYY-MM-DD sort as text). monthIndex: compare Number(date.slice(0, 4)) and Number(date.slice(5, 7)).","body":"## The leaking bucket\n\nPicture filling a bucket with water while it leaks from a hole in the bottom. You can pour faster (more marketing), but if the hole is big, the bucket never fills. For a business, new customers are the water being poured in, and customers who never come back are the leak. **Retention analysis** measures the size of the hole.\n\n## Why totals hide the problem\n\n\"We had 1,000 active customers this month and last month\" sounds stable. But maybe 400 left and 400 new ones replaced them. Totals mix old and new customers together. To see clearly, follow **groups of customers who started at the same time**.\n\n## Cohorts\n\nA **cohort** is a group of customers who share a starting point, usually the month of their first purchase. A **cohort table** shows, for each cohort, what share was still buying 1, 2, 3 months later:\n\n| First month | Customers | Month 0 | Month 1 | Month 2 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-09 | 120 | 100% | 42% | 30% |\n| 2026-10 | 150 | 100% | 48% | |\n| 2026-11 | 140 | 100% | | |\n\nRead **across** a row to see one cohort fading over time. Read **down** a column to see whether newer cohorts are retained better than older ones: if month-1 retention rose from 42% to 48%, something you changed (better onboarding, a loyalty programme) may be working.\n\n## Key measures\n\n- **Retention rate** for period n: customers from the cohort active in month n ÷ cohort size.\n- **Churn** = 1 − retention.\n- **Repeat purchase rate**: share of customers who bought more than once.\n- **Customer lifetime value (CLV)**, simply: average order value × purchases per year × years a customer stays (× margin for profit). Compare CLV with the cost to acquire a customer: if acquiring costs more than a customer is worth, growth loses money.\n\n## Month numbers\n\nTo place each purchase, work out the **months since the first purchase**: `(year − firstYear) × 12 + (month − firstMonth)`. A first purchase in November and a repeat in January of the next year is month 2.\n\n## Your task\n\nPurchases are `{ customer, date }` with dates like `\"2026-09-14\"`.\n\n1. `monthIndex(first, date)`: months between two dates' months (`\"2026-11-30\"` to `\"2027-01-02\"` is 2).\n2. `cohorts(purchases)`: an object mapping each first-purchase month (`\"2026-09\"`) to the **number of customers** whose first purchase was in that month.\n3. `retention(purchases, cohort, n)`: the share (0 to 1, rounded to 2 decimals) of that cohort's customers who made at least one purchase in month `n` after their first.\n4. `repeatRate(purchases)`: share of all customers with 2 or more purchases, rounded to 2 decimals.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":9}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-cohorts$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-cohorts","title":"Customers over time: cohorts and retention","minutes":35,"kind":"code","outline":"Why averages hide churn, building a cohort retention table, repeat rate and customer lifetime value.","body":"## The leaking bucket\n\nPicture filling a bucket with water while it leaks from a hole in the bottom. You can pour faster (more marketing), but if the hole is big, the bucket never fills. For a business, new customers are the water being poured in, and customers who never come back are the leak. **Retention analysis** measures the size of the hole.\n\n## Why totals hide the problem\n\n\"We had 1,000 active customers this month and last month\" sounds stable. But maybe 400 left and 400 new ones replaced them. Totals mix old and new customers together. To see clearly, follow **groups of customers who started at the same time**.\n\n## Cohorts\n\nA **cohort** is a group of customers who share a starting point, usually the month of their first purchase. A **cohort table** shows, for each cohort, what share was still buying 1, 2, 3 months later:\n\n| First month | Customers | Month 0 | Month 1 | Month 2 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-09 | 120 | 100% | 42% | 30% |\n| 2026-10 | 150 | 100% | 48% | |\n| 2026-11 | 140 | 100% | | |\n\nRead **across** a row to see one cohort fading over time. Read **down** a column to see whether newer cohorts are retained better than older ones: if month-1 retention rose from 42% to 48%, something you changed (better onboarding, a loyalty programme) may be working.\n\n## Key measures\n\n- **Retention rate** for period n: customers from the cohort active in month n ÷ cohort size.\n- **Churn** = 1 − retention.\n- **Repeat purchase rate**: share of customers who bought more than once.\n- **Customer lifetime value (CLV)**, simply: average order value × purchases per year × years a customer stays (× margin for profit). Compare CLV with the cost to acquire a customer: if acquiring costs more than a customer is worth, growth loses money.\n\n## Month numbers\n\nTo place each purchase, work out the **months since the first purchase**: `(year − firstYear) × 12 + (month − firstMonth)`. A first purchase in November and a repeat in January of the next year is month 2.\n\n## Your task\n\nPurchases are `{ customer, date }` with dates like `\"2026-09-14\"`.\n\n1. `monthIndex(first, date)`: months between two dates' months (`\"2026-11-30\"` to `\"2027-01-02\"` is 2).\n2. `cohorts(purchases)`: an object mapping each first-purchase month (`\"2026-09\"`) to the **number of customers** whose first purchase was in that month.\n3. `retention(purchases, cohort, n)`: the share (0 to 1, rounded to 2 decimals) of that cohort's customers who made at least one purchase in month `n` after their first.\n4. `repeatRate(purchases)`: share of all customers with 2 or more purchases, rounded to 2 decimals.","task":"Write monthIndex, cohorts, retention and repeatRate.","starter":"<script>\n  const P = [\n    { customer: 'ada', date: '2026-09-02' }, { customer: 'ada', date: '2026-10-11' }, { customer: 'ada', date: '2026-11-20' },\n    { customer: 'bola', date: '2026-09-15' }, { customer: 'bola', date: '2026-11-03' },\n    { customer: 'chi', date: '2026-09-28' },\n    { customer: 'dan', date: '2026-10-05' }, { customer: 'dan', date: '2026-11-09' },\n    { customer: 'eze', date: '2026-10-21' },\n  ]\n  function monthIndex(first, date) {\n  }\n  function cohorts(purchases) {\n  }\n  function retention(purchases, cohort, n) {\n  }\n  function repeatRate(purchases) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"months between dates","test":"return monthIndex('2026-11-30', '2027-01-02') === 2 && monthIndex('2026-09-02', '2026-09-30') === 0"},{"label":"cohort sizes by first-purchase month","test":"return JSON.stringify(cohorts(P)) === JSON.stringify({ '2026-09': 3, '2026-10': 2 })"},{"label":"retention per month","test":"return retention(P, '2026-09', 1) === 0.33 && retention(P, '2026-09', 2) === 0.67 && retention(P, '2026-10', 1) === 0.5 && retention(P, '2026-09', 0) === 1"},{"label":"repeat purchase rate","test":"return repeatRate(P) === 0.6"}],"hint":"First find each customer's earliest date (dates as YYYY-MM-DD sort as text). monthIndex: compare Number(date.slice(0, 4)) and Number(date.slice(5, 7))."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-forecast$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Simple forecasting","kind":"code","task":"Write naive, seasonalNaive, trend, forecastTrend and mape.","hint":"trend: const n = series.length; const tm = (n - 1) / 2; const ym = mean of series; slope = Σ (i - tm)(y - ym) / Σ (i - tm)². forecastTrend: intercept + slope * (n - 1 + ahead).","body":"## Forecasts are weather reports for business\n\nA weather forecast does not promise rain; it says \"70% chance\". Business forecasts work the same way: they help plan stock, staff and cash, but they are always uncertain. The goal is not to be perfect; it is to be **less wrong** than guessing, and to know how wrong you might be.\n\n## Start with simple baselines\n\nBefore any clever model, try the simplest forecasts. Surprisingly often, they are hard to beat.\n\n- **Naive forecast**: next period = this period. (\"Tomorrow's sales will be like today's.\")\n- **Moving average**: next period = average of the last k periods. Smooths out noise.\n- **Seasonal naive**: next period = the same period last season. For a shop with a December rush, this December ≈ last December. For daily data with a weekly pattern, next Monday ≈ last Monday.\n\n## Linear trend\n\nIf sales grow steadily, fit a straight line through time (the least-squares method from the regression lesson):\n\n```\nslope = Σ (t − t̄)(y − ȳ) / Σ (t − t̄)²\nintercept = ȳ − slope × t̄\nforecast(t) = intercept + slope × t\n```\n\nHere `t` is the period number (0, 1, 2 …) and `y` the value. Extend the line forward for future periods. Be careful: straight lines never bend, and real growth always slows eventually. Do not extend a trend far into the future.\n\n## Measuring forecast error\n\nCompare forecasts with what actually happened, on periods the model did not see:\n\n- **MAE** (mean absolute error): the average size of the miss, in units (₦, items).\n- **MAPE** (mean absolute percentage error): the average miss as a percentage of the actual value: `mean( |actual − forecast| ÷ |actual| ) × 100`. Easy for managers to understand (\"we are usually within 8%\"). It breaks when actual values are zero, so skip those.\n\nAlways compare a fancy model's error with the naive baseline. If it does not beat the baseline, it is not worth the complexity.\n\n## Communicating forecasts\n\nGive a range, not only a single number: \"between ₦3.8m and ₦4.6m, most likely ₦4.2m\". State the assumptions (\"assumes no price change and normal stock levels\"). Update forecasts as new data arrives.\n\n## Going further\n\nExponential smoothing (Holt-Winters), ARIMA, and tools like Prophet handle trend and seasonality together. Excel and Google Sheets have a FORECAST function and forecast sheets. The baseline thinking from this lesson still applies.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `naive(series)`: the forecast for the next period (the last value).\n2. `seasonalNaive(series, season)`: the forecast for the next period, which is the value `season` periods before it (`series[series.length - season]`).\n3. `trend(series)`: return `{ slope, intercept }` of the least-squares line with `t = 0, 1, 2 …`.\n4. `forecastTrend(series, ahead)`: the trend line's value `ahead` periods after the last one, rounded to 2 decimals.\n5. `mape(actual, forecast)`: MAPE as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal, skipping periods where actual is 0.","practice":null,"track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":10}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-forecast$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-forecast","title":"Simple forecasting","minutes":35,"kind":"code","outline":"Naive and seasonal forecasts, a linear trend, measuring forecast error with MAPE, and communicating uncertainty.","body":"## Forecasts are weather reports for business\n\nA weather forecast does not promise rain; it says \"70% chance\". Business forecasts work the same way: they help plan stock, staff and cash, but they are always uncertain. The goal is not to be perfect; it is to be **less wrong** than guessing, and to know how wrong you might be.\n\n## Start with simple baselines\n\nBefore any clever model, try the simplest forecasts. Surprisingly often, they are hard to beat.\n\n- **Naive forecast**: next period = this period. (\"Tomorrow's sales will be like today's.\")\n- **Moving average**: next period = average of the last k periods. Smooths out noise.\n- **Seasonal naive**: next period = the same period last season. For a shop with a December rush, this December ≈ last December. For daily data with a weekly pattern, next Monday ≈ last Monday.\n\n## Linear trend\n\nIf sales grow steadily, fit a straight line through time (the least-squares method from the regression lesson):\n\n```\nslope = Σ (t − t̄)(y − ȳ) / Σ (t − t̄)²\nintercept = ȳ − slope × t̄\nforecast(t) = intercept + slope × t\n```\n\nHere `t` is the period number (0, 1, 2 …) and `y` the value. Extend the line forward for future periods. Be careful: straight lines never bend, and real growth always slows eventually. Do not extend a trend far into the future.\n\n## Measuring forecast error\n\nCompare forecasts with what actually happened, on periods the model did not see:\n\n- **MAE** (mean absolute error): the average size of the miss, in units (₦, items).\n- **MAPE** (mean absolute percentage error): the average miss as a percentage of the actual value: `mean( |actual − forecast| ÷ |actual| ) × 100`. Easy for managers to understand (\"we are usually within 8%\"). It breaks when actual values are zero, so skip those.\n\nAlways compare a fancy model's error with the naive baseline. If it does not beat the baseline, it is not worth the complexity.\n\n## Communicating forecasts\n\nGive a range, not only a single number: \"between ₦3.8m and ₦4.6m, most likely ₦4.2m\". State the assumptions (\"assumes no price change and normal stock levels\"). Update forecasts as new data arrives.\n\n## Going further\n\nExponential smoothing (Holt-Winters), ARIMA, and tools like Prophet handle trend and seasonality together. Excel and Google Sheets have a FORECAST function and forecast sheets. The baseline thinking from this lesson still applies.\n\n## Your task\n\n1. `naive(series)`: the forecast for the next period (the last value).\n2. `seasonalNaive(series, season)`: the forecast for the next period, which is the value `season` periods before it (`series[series.length - season]`).\n3. `trend(series)`: return `{ slope, intercept }` of the least-squares line with `t = 0, 1, 2 …`.\n4. `forecastTrend(series, ahead)`: the trend line's value `ahead` periods after the last one, rounded to 2 decimals.\n5. `mape(actual, forecast)`: MAPE as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal, skipping periods where actual is 0.","task":"Write naive, seasonalNaive, trend, forecastTrend and mape.","starter":"<script>\n  function naive(series) {\n  }\n  function seasonalNaive(series, season) {\n  }\n  function trend(series) {\n  }\n  function forecastTrend(series, ahead) {\n  }\n  function mape(actual, forecast) {\n  }\n</script>\n","checks":[{"label":"naive and seasonal naive","test":"return naive([5, 7, 9]) === 9 && seasonalNaive([10, 20, 30, 40, 12, 22, 33, 41], 4) === 12"},{"label":"least-squares trend","test":"const t = trend([3, 5, 7, 9]); const u = trend([2, 4, 5, 4, 5]); return t.slope === 2 && t.intercept === 3 && Math.abs(u.slope - 0.6) < 1e-9 && Math.abs(u.intercept - 2.8) < 1e-9"},{"label":"forecast along the trend","test":"return forecastTrend([3, 5, 7, 9], 1) === 11 && forecastTrend([2, 4, 5, 4, 5], 2) === 6.4"},{"label":"MAPE skipping zero actuals","test":"return mape([100, 200, 0, 400], [110, 180, 5, 400]) === 6.7 && mape([50], [50]) === 0"}],"hint":"trend: const n = series.length; const tm = (n - 1) / 2; const ym = mean of series; slope = Σ (i - tm)(y - ym) / Σ (i - tm)². forecastTrend: intercept + slope * (n - 1 + ahead)."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_meta (id, meta) values ($cdm$analytics-story$cdm$, $cdm${"title":"Data storytelling and your analyst career","kind":"prompt","task":null,"hint":null,"body":"## The story structure that works\n\nBusy managers read the first two lines. Lead with the answer:\n\n1. **Headline**: the one message. *\"Garki is losing ₦600k a month in stock-outs of 5 fast movers.\"*\n2. **Evidence**: 2 or 3 numbers or one clear chart that prove it.\n3. **Why it matters**: money, customers, risk.\n4. **Recommendation**: what to do, who, by when, and what it will cost or save.\n5. **Next check**: how you will know it worked.\n\nAvoid: 20 charts, jargon, numbers without comparison (\"₦4m\": good or bad?), and hiding the uncertainty. Say what you are not sure about.\n\n## Charts that tell the truth\n\n- Bar charts start at zero\n- Do not use 3D or pie charts with many slices\n- Label directly; title the chart with the insight, not the field name (\"Sales fell 18% after the price rise\", not \"Sales by month\")\n\n## Getting into analytics\n\n- Master **Excel / Google Sheets** (pivot tables, XLOOKUP, charts), then **SQL**, then **Power BI** or **Looker Studio**, then Python\n- Build 3 portfolio projects on real-looking data: a sales dashboard, a customer analysis, a forecast. Publish them with a one-page write-up each.\n- Certificates employers know: **Google Data Analytics**, **Microsoft PL-300 (Power BI)**; your CDM Studio certificate shows the foundations\n- Freelance on Upwork or Fiverr: small businesses need monthly sales reports and dashboards\n\n## Practise\n\nAsk the tutor to help you turn a set of numbers into a one-page story with a recommendation.","practice":"Give the AI some numbers from a business you know (for example, monthly sales for 3 branches for 4 months, with one branch falling), and ask it to help you write a one-page story: headline, the evidence, why it matters, a recommendation with owner and deadline, and how to check it worked. Then ask it to critique your headline.","track":"analytics","track_title":"Data analytics for business","paid":true,"order":11}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set meta = excluded.meta, updated_at = now();
insert into public.lesson_content (id, track, body) values ($cdm$analytics-story$cdm$, $cdm$analytics$cdm$, $cdm${"id":"analytics-story","title":"Data storytelling and your analyst career","minutes":20,"kind":"prompt","outline":"Turn analysis into a one-page story with a recommendation, and plan your path into analytics jobs.","body":"## The story structure that works\n\nBusy managers read the first two lines. Lead with the answer:\n\n1. **Headline**: the one message. *\"Garki is losing ₦600k a month in stock-outs of 5 fast movers.\"*\n2. **Evidence**: 2 or 3 numbers or one clear chart that prove it.\n3. **Why it matters**: money, customers, risk.\n4. **Recommendation**: what to do, who, by when, and what it will cost or save.\n5. **Next check**: how you will know it worked.\n\nAvoid: 20 charts, jargon, numbers without comparison (\"₦4m\": good or bad?), and hiding the uncertainty. Say what you are not sure about.\n\n## Charts that tell the truth\n\n- Bar charts start at zero\n- Do not use 3D or pie charts with many slices\n- Label directly; title the chart with the insight, not the field name (\"Sales fell 18% after the price rise\", not \"Sales by month\")\n\n## Getting into analytics\n\n- Master **Excel / Google Sheets** (pivot tables, XLOOKUP, charts), then **SQL**, then **Power BI** or **Looker Studio**, then Python\n- Build 3 portfolio projects on real-looking data: a sales dashboard, a customer analysis, a forecast. Publish them with a one-page write-up each.\n- Certificates employers know: **Google Data Analytics**, **Microsoft PL-300 (Power BI)**; your CDM Studio certificate shows the foundations\n- Freelance on Upwork or Fiverr: small businesses need monthly sales reports and dashboards\n\n## Practise\n\nAsk the tutor to help you turn a set of numbers into a one-page story with a recommendation.","practice":"Give the AI some numbers from a business you know (for example, monthly sales for 3 branches for 4 months, with one branch falling), and ask it to help you write a one-page story: headline, the evidence, why it matters, a recommendation with owner and deadline, and how to check it worked. Then ask it to critique your headline."}$cdm$::jsonb) on conflict (id) do update set body = excluded.body, track = excluded.track, updated_at = now();
