**What it does:** it reads the month's sales from CSV text (at least 12 rows that you write yourself, with columns such as date, product, quantity and price, including at least 2 deliberately bad rows), cleans the data, and prints a clear report.

**The report must show:** the total revenue, revenue per product, the 3 best-selling products, the best day, the average sale, and how many rows were skipped and why.

**How to work:** build it in the Studio code playground or on your own computer, test it until the output is right, then paste the **whole program** here. Use functions (and a class if it helps), and keep the data inside the program so the marker can read it.$cdm$, $cdm$python$cdm$, $cdm$["Keeps the sales data as CSV text inside the program with at least 12 rows and at least 2 bad rows","Reads the CSV with the csv module (DictReader or reader) rather than only splitting on commas","Converts text to numbers and handles bad rows with try / except, counting what was skipped","Is organised into small, well-named functions (at least 3), each with one job","Uses a dictionary (or class) to total revenue per product","Finds the top 3 products using sorting with a key","Works out the total revenue, average sale and best day correctly","Prints a clear, readable report using f-strings with formatted money","Uses constants or parameters instead of repeating magic numbers, and has helpful comments or docstrings"]$cdm$::jsonb, 70) on conflict (track) do update set title = excluded.title, brief = excluded.brief, lang = excluded.lang, rubric = excluded.rubric, pass_pct = excluded.pass_pct;
